window.__TG1={"EN70B_I":"","EN70B_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: A student collects a suit for a job interview at a tailor’s shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Tailor:</b> Ah, Mr. Krit. Your suit is ready. Would you like to try it on before you take it home?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Yes, please. The interview is tomorrow morning, so {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tailor:</b> Very sensible. The fitting room is just behind you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tailor:</b> (a few minutes later) So, how does it feel?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Well, the length is perfect, but the jacket {{2}} I can hardly lift my arms.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tailor:</b> Hmm, I think I took it in a little too much last time. I can let it out, but {{3}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Tonight? Is that even possible?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tailor:</b> It’ll be tight, if you’ll pardon the pun, but I’ll make it work.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> {{4}} I owe you one.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tailor:</b> Not at all. Just get the job, and come back to me for your next suit.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: A bicycle mechanic phones a student about his repair","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Lek:</b> Hello, is that Arthit? It’s Lek from Spoke and Chain. I’m calling about your bike. {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arthit:</b> Oh no. That doesn’t sound good. What’s wrong with it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Lek:</b> Well, the tyre was the easy part. But then we found the chain is badly worn and the back brake cable is close to snapping.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arthit:</b> Seriously? I only brought it in for a flat tyre. So {{6}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Lek:</b> For everything, about 1,200 baht, instead of the 150 we quoted you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arthit:</b> Ouch. {{7}} I’m a student, and that’s more than I spend on food in a week.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Lek:</b> I understand. If money’s tight, {{8}} The chain can wait a month or two.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arthit:</b> That sounds sensible. Just the tyre and the cable, then. When can I collect it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Lek:</b> Give me an hour.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two teammates walk home after losing a futsal match","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ton:</b> Well, that was a disaster. Five nil.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Wan:</b> Don’t remind me. And that penalty I missed at the start? {{9}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ton:</b> A little? The ball went over the fence. Someone in the car park is probably still looking for it.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Wan:</b> All right, all right. {{10}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ton:</b> Fair enough. To be honest, the whole team was half asleep. We didn’t even warm up.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Wan:</b> Exactly. {{11}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ton:</b> Good point. I’ll ask the coach to book the court thirty minutes earlier next time. And you, my friend, {{12}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Wan:</b> Very funny. I’m not giving up my place up front that easily.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A student phones a language school abroad about her summer course","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> Good morning, Westbridge Language School, admissions. Emma speaking.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Hi. My name is Nattaya Srisuk, and I’m enrolled on the four week course starting on July 6. {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> Oh dear. What seems to be the problem?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> My student visa still hasn’t come through. The embassy says it could take another three weeks, {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> I see. So realistically, you won’t be here for the first part of the course. Well, you could join the class late, or you could transfer to our August course. {{15}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> The August one, definitely. I’d hate to miss any lessons. Is there a fee for transferring?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> There’s normally a 50 dollar administration fee, but {{16}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Really? That’s very kind of you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> Not at all. Now, you’ve also booked a homestay with the Wilson family for July. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Oh, I hadn’t thought of that. Could they take me in August instead?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> Let me check. Unfortunately, they’re hosting another student from August 1. {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> That’s fine, as long as it isn’t too far from the school.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> All our families live within a thirty minute bus ride. Oh, and one last thing: {{19}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Of course. I’ll forward you the embassy’s email this afternoon. Thank you so much, Emma. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Emma:</b> It’s my pleasure. These things happen. We’ll see you in August.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">DON’T JUST DESIGN IT. MAKE IT.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">The Forge: Faculty of Engineering Makerspace</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Open to all current students and staff<br>Building 4, Room 112<br>Mon to Fri, 9 a.m. to 8 p.m.; Sat, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">BEFORE YOU BOOK A MACHINE</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Complete our free online safety module and score at least 80% on the quiz.</span><span><b>2</b>Attend a one hour practical induction for EACH machine you plan to use. Inductions run every Tuesday and Thursday at 5 p.m., with a maximum of six people per session.</span><span><b>3</b>Book machine time on the Forge app. Inducted users may book up to 4 hours per week.</span></span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>3D Printers</b>2 baht per gram of material</span><span><b>Laser Cutter</b>150 baht per hour; plywood and acrylic only</span><span><b>Electronics Bench</b>Free to use; parts sold at cost</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\">Inductions are free for engineering students. Students and staff from other faculties pay 100 baht per machine.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Users who miss two booked sessions without cancelling at least 24 hours in advance will lose their booking rights for 30 days.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">THE OLD TOWN LOOKS DIFFERENT AFTER DARK</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Lanterns and Lenses: Night Photo Walk</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Every Friday and Saturday</b>, 7 p.m. to 10 p.m.<br>Meeting point: the clock tower, Harbour Square, Port Sila</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Small Groups</b>No more than 8 photographers per guide</span><span><b>Any Camera</b>Phones are just as welcome as professional cameras</span><span><b>Learn</b>Long exposures, light trails and street portraits</span><span><b>Included</b>Tripod loan and a hot drink at the halfway stop</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>Adults 1,200 baht. Students with a valid ID card 900 baht.</b><br>Bring a friend: book two places together and save 10% on the total.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Walks go ahead in light rain but are cancelled during thunderstorms, in which case you may choose a full refund or a free place on another night. Comfortable shoes are essential: the route covers about 4 kilometers, including 120 steps up to Lighthouse Hill. Not suitable for children under 12.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: Lumen Folio 7 Ebook Reader","<span class=\"xp\">(1) For years I insisted that nothing could replace the feel of a paper book, so I grudgingly admit that the Lumen Folio 7 (5,490 baht) has changed my reading habits more in six weeks than any bookshop has in six years. It is not perfect, and for some readers it may be the wrong purchase, but it does one thing remarkably well.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) That one thing is the screen. The seven inch display looks like printed paper rather than a glowing tablet, and it stays perfectly readable in direct sunlight, as I found on a ferry ride. A warm light setting removes most of the blue glare at night, and physical page turn buttons make one handed reading easy. Lumen promises six weeks of battery life, a figure based on thirty minutes of reading a day with the light off. Reading for about an hour every evening with the light at half strength, I needed to recharge after four weeks, which hardly seems unreasonable.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The weaknesses become obvious once you leave novels behind. Most of my course readings are PDF files full of charts, and on the Folio they are a chore: zooming in is slow, and diagrams that rely on color become meaningless shades of gray. Highlighting is easy, but exporting your notes to a computer requires a Lumen Plus subscription at 99 baht a month, which feels like being charged for your own notes.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) What surprised me most was not a feature but an absence. There are no notifications, no social media and no games. On my phone, a chapter rarely survives a single message; on the Folio, I regularly read for an hour without looking up. In this sense, the device’s greatest feature is what it cannot do.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) My verdict: if you mainly read fiction and want to spend less time on your phone, the Folio 7 is a delight. If your reading consists largely of textbooks and PDFs, you would be wiser to look elsewhere.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ สินค้าในรีวิวเป็นสินค้าสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Mangroves return to Ban Laem Sai, but not everyone is cheering</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Three years after fishing families in Ban Laem Sai began replanting mangroves along their eroded coast, the shoreline has stopped retreating for the first time in two decades, the provincial marine office said on Monday. Between 2005 and 2022, the village lost an average of four meters of land a year to the sea, and at least eleven houses had to be moved inland.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Since 2023, volunteers have planted 36,000 seedlings on 15 hectares of mudflat. About 60 percent have survived, which officials call “well above expectations” for a site exposed to monsoon waves. Before planting began, the villagers built low fences of bamboo poles offshore. The fences slow incoming waves and allow mud to settle around the young roots.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) “People think we planted a forest. Really, we planted a sea wall that grows,” said Somsak Thongdee, the head of the village fishing group. A concrete wall of similar length would have cost the province about 40 million baht and would need repairs every few years, whereas the replanting has so far cost less than three million baht, most of it on bamboo and seedlings.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) The benefits are not only physical. Fishers report that crabs and small shrimp have returned to the channels between the young trees, and several families now earn extra money guiding school groups at low tide.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Not everyone is pleased. Some fishers from the neighboring village of Ban Tha Rua say that the bamboo fences have cut them off from shallow waters where their families have set crab traps for generations. “Nobody asked us before the fences went up,” one said. The marine office has promised a meeting between the two villages next month to agree on gaps in the fences for small boats.</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Scientists also urge caution. Dr. Kanya Ruangsri, a coastal ecologist who was not involved in the project, said three years was “far too short to call it a success,” since a single severe storm could wash away seedlings that have not yet developed strong roots. “Mangroves are a long game,” she said. “The real test will come in ten years.”</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ หมู่บ้าน บุคคล และตัวเลขในข่าวเป็นข้อมูลสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">How do students get to campus?</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Main way of traveling to Riverside University, with average one way journey time and average monthly cost (survey of 1,500 students; each student chose one main mode)</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Main mode</b><b>Share of students</b><b>One way time (minutes)</b><b>Monthly cost (baht)</b></span><span><b>Rail</b><i>28%</i><i>42</i><i>1,350</i></span><span><b>Bus</b><i>24%</i><i>55</i><i>620</i></span><span><b>Motorcycle taxi</b><i>9%</i><i>18</i><i>2,700</i></span><span><b>Own motorcycle</b><i>17%</i><i>25</i><i>900</i></span><span><b>Car</b><i>7%</i><i>48</i><i>4,200</i></span><span><b>Bicycle or on foot</b><i>15%</i><i>14</i><i>0</i></span><span class=\"en7-tot\"><b>Total</b><i>100%</i><i></i><i></i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Monthly cost includes fares, fuel and parking, based on 20 days of travel to campus per month.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">New motorcycle registrations in Northgate City, 2019 to 2025</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Number of new motorcycles registered each year (thousands), by type of engine</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"New motorcycle registrations, petrol and electric, 2019 to 2025\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"154.0\" y2=\"154.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"157.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"122.0\" y2=\"122.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"125.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"90.0\" y2=\"90.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"93.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"58.0\" y2=\"58.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"61.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"26.0\" y2=\"26.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"29.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2019</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text><text x=\"186\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2023</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2024</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2025</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,32.4 72,56.4 110,62.8 148,67.6 186,80.4 224,93.2 262,106.0\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"32.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"56.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"62.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"67.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"80.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"93.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"106.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"104.0\" class=\"lb\">Petrol</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,182.8 72,179.6 110,174.8 148,166.8 186,154.0 224,136.4 262,114.0\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"182.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"179.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"174.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"166.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"154.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"136.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"114.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"122.0\" class=\"lb\">Electric</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Year</b><b>2019</b><b>2020</b><b>2021</b><b>2022</b><b>2023</b><b>2024</b><b>2025</b></span><span><b>Petrol</b><i>96</i><i>81</i><i>77</i><i>74</i><i>66</i><i>58</i><i>50</i></span><span><b>Electric</b><i>2</i><i>4</i><i>7</i><i>12</i><i>20</i><i>31</i><i>45</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>Why Everything Takes Longer Than We Think","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When work began on the Sydney Opera House in 1959, the building was expected to open within a few years. It finally opened in 1973, about a decade behind schedule and at more than fourteen times its original budget. It would be comforting to dismiss this as a rare disaster, but the pattern behind it is remarkably ordinary. The same error appears whenever a student promises to finish an essay by Friday or a friend says they will be ready “in five minutes.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) In 1979, the psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky gave this error a name: the planning fallacy. It describes our tendency to underestimate how long a future task will take, even when we know that similar tasks in the past ran over time. In one well known study, university students were asked to predict when they would finish their final year research projects. They were also asked how long the projects would take if everything went as badly as possible. On average, the students took around three weeks longer than they had predicted, and fewer than a third finished by the date they had given. The most sobering finding was that, on average, they missed even their worst case estimates.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Why do intelligent people make the same mistake again and again? The main reason, researchers believe, is that we plan from the inside. We picture the specific steps of the task ahead, and we imagine those steps going smoothly. Past delays are remembered, but they are treated as exceptions: the printer broke, a friend fell ill, that project was unusually difficult. This allows us to keep our faith in the current plan, since the next project, we assume, will not suffer from such bad luck. Interestingly, the error largely disappears when we predict for other people. Observers who know little about a person’s plans tend to rely on that person’s track record instead, and their estimates are often more accurate.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) This suggests a simple remedy, which researchers call taking the outside view. Instead of asking how long a task should take, planners ask how long similar tasks have actually taken, either for themselves or for others. Some governments now require large building projects to compare their budgets with a database of earlier projects of the same type. Another useful technique is to break a task into its smaller parts before estimating; people who list every stage of a job usually give longer and more realistic estimates than those who consider the job as a whole.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) None of this means that optimism is worthless. A realistic estimate of the effort involved might stop some people from starting valuable projects at all. Still, the cost of the fallacy is real, and it is paid in missed deadlines, sleepless nights and broken promises. The writer Douglas Hofstadter captured the problem in what he jokingly called a law: “It always takes longer than you expect, even when you take into account Hofstadter’s Law.” The joke works because it is true. Knowing about the bias is not enough to escape it; we also need habits that force us to look outside our own hopeful plans.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านจิตวิทยาการตัดสินใจที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Stone That Mends Itself","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Walk through Rome today and you will pass buildings that have survived for almost two thousand years. The dome of the Pantheon, completed in the second century, remains the largest dome in the world made of concrete without steel reinforcement. Along the coasts of the Mediterranean, Roman harbor walls still stand in seawater, a setting in which many modern concrete structures begin to crumble within a few decades. For engineers who spend their careers fighting cracks, this is not merely impressive; it is a puzzle.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Roman builders made their concrete by mixing lime with volcanic ash and pieces of rock. For marine structures, they packed this mixture into wooden frames that stood in the sea itself, so that seawater soaked through it as it set. To a modern engineer, this sounds like a recipe for failure. Salt water is one of the main enemies of today’s concrete, largely because it reaches the steel bars inside and causes them to rust and expand, splitting the concrete from within.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) A study published in 2017 helped to explain why the Roman method worked. Researchers found that seawater slowly dissolved parts of the volcanic ash, allowing rare minerals to grow in the tiny spaces of the concrete. These interlocking crystals made the material tougher over time. In other words, the sea that destroys modern harbors had been quietly strengthening Roman ones for centuries.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) A second clue was hiding in plain sight. Roman concrete is dotted with small white lumps of lime, which scientists had long dismissed as a sign of careless mixing. In 2023, however, a team of researchers argued that the lumps were the result of a deliberate technique in which the lime was mixed while still extremely hot. When a crack forms and water seeps in, it reaches these lumps and dissolves some of their calcium, which then hardens again inside the crack and seals it. When the team made concrete using this hot method and cracked it on purpose, the cracks closed within two weeks, while those in ordinary samples did not.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) It would be tempting to conclude that we should simply return to the Roman recipe, but the matter is not so straightforward. Roman concrete is considerably weaker than modern concrete under heavy loads, and it gains strength slowly, which would make it impractical for a modern construction schedule. The right kind of volcanic ash is also not available everywhere. Instead, engineers are borrowing the principles rather than the recipe, adding materials that react with water to repair small cracks. The stakes are high: producing cement accounts for roughly eight percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, so concrete that lasts twice as long could greatly reduce the need to rebuild. Sometimes the way forward begins with a careful look back.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านวัสดุศาสตร์ที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Few inventions are as ordinary, or as useful, as the barcode. Its story began in 1948, when Bernard Silver, a graduate student in Philadelphia, overheard a supermarket executive asking for a system that could record products automatically at the checkout. Silver shared the idea with his friend Norman Joseph Woodland, {{61}} the problem soon became an obsession. While sitting on a Miami beach, Woodland dragged his fingers through the sand and realized that the dots and dashes of Morse code could be stretched into thick and thin lines. The pair {{62}} a patent for their design in 1952, but {{63}} lasers and cheap computers, there was no practical way to read the code quickly. Not until June 1974 {{64}} scanned at a real checkout, on a packet of chewing gum in Ohio. Today, barcodes are scanned billions of times a day, yet few shoppers ever {{65}} a thought to where they came from.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">Almost everyone has had hiccups, yet they remain one of the body’s stranger habits. Each hiccup begins when the diaphragm, the dome shaped muscle beneath the lungs, suddenly tightens and pulls air into the chest. A fraction of a second later, the vocal cords snap shut. The abrupt closing of the vocal cords, rather than the movements of the diaphragm, {{66}} the familiar “hic” sound. {{67}} any useful purpose in adults remains unclear. One theory holds that hiccups are a leftover from ancient ancestors that breathed with gills, while another notes that babies, who are far more {{68}} to hiccups than adults, may use them to push swallowed air out of the stomach after feeding. Fortunately, most attacks are harmless. {{69}} untreated, they usually stop on their own within a few minutes. In rare cases, however, hiccups continue for days, and an attack that lasts that long {{70}} a sign of an underlying illness, so it should be checked by a doctor.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Two years ago, I owned more than four hundred books, three bicycles and more kitchen gadgets than I could name. {{71}} to a smaller flat, I would probably still be surrounded by them today. Packing forced me to handle every object, and I was shocked by how many I had not touched in years. I decided to stop {{72}} anything without a clear purpose, and slowly the clutter began to disappear. Minimalism is often {{73}} as a fashionable trend for people who can afford to replace whatever they throw away, and that criticism is not entirely unfair. However, the real lesson I learned was not about owning less but about choosing more carefully. A friend who works as a professional organizer recommends that every new item {{74}} a home before it is bought. It is simple advice, yet it has saved me more money than any budgeting app. {{75}}, I have found that the fewer things I own, the more I notice and enjoy the ones I keep.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>This leaves only radiation, heat that travels as invisible waves, so the inner surfaces of the walls are given a shiny, reflective coating that sends these waves back toward the drink.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Finally, a tightly fitting stopper closes the opening at the top, which is the only part of the flask that the vacuum cannot protect.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>A vacuum flask can keep coffee hot for hours, not because it produces any heat of its own, but because it blocks each of the three ways in which heat normally escapes.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>First, the flask has two walls with almost all of the air removed from the narrow gap between them, and because heat cannot be conducted or carried by moving air across an empty space, very little passes through the sides.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The first, igneous rock, forms when melted rock from deep inside the Earth cools and hardens, either slowly underground, as with granite, or rapidly on the surface after a volcanic eruption.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Finally, when either of these types is buried deep underground and exposed to intense heat and pressure, it can be transformed into metamorphic rock, as happens when limestone becomes marble.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Geologists divide the rocks of the Earth’s crust into three main groups, according to the way in which each of them was originally formed.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Over millions of years, wind and water break existing rocks down into small particles, which settle in layers and are gradually pressed together to form the second type, sedimentary rock.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Some of his customers, however, assumed that the little bags were meant to go straight into the pot, and they began dropping them into hot water without ever opening them.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Instead of being annoyed, the customers found the method so convenient that they soon wrote back to ask for more tea packed in exactly the same way.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>To save money, Sullivan began sending samples of his tea to customers in small silk bags rather than in the expensive metal tins that were normally used at the time.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>According to a popular story, the tea bag was invented by accident in 1908 by Thomas Sullivan, a tea merchant in New York who was looking for a cheaper way to send samples.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>For young people starting their careers, the question of whether to rent or to buy a home is one of the most important financial decisions that they will ever face.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Buying, by contrast, requires a large down payment and ties the owner to one place, but each monthly payment slowly turns into something that the owner actually possesses.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In the end, the wiser choice often depends less on the figures than on how long a person realistically expects to stay in the same city.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Renting offers flexibility, since tenants can move easily when they change jobs, and it requires no large payment at the start apart from a refundable deposit.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Such simple measures cost relatively little, yet studies suggest that they can reduce heat related illness significantly without lowering the amount of work that gets done.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>As heatwaves become longer and more frequent, outdoor workers such as builders, farmers and delivery riders face a growing risk of heat stress, a condition in which the body can no longer cool itself.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Its early signs, including headaches, dizziness and heavy sweating, are easy to ignore, especially for workers who are paid by the job, until the condition suddenly becomes dangerous.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>To prevent this, some employers have begun moving the heaviest tasks to the early morning and providing shaded rest areas with cool drinking water.</span></span>"]},"EN70C_I":"","EN70C_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: At a hair salon, the day before a customer’s graduation photos","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Back so soon, Ms. Lin? Is everything all right with the cut?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Well, that’s actually why I’m here. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Oh dear. Let me have a look. Hmm, I see what you mean. The left side is a little shorter than the right.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> A little? {{2}} And my graduation photos are tomorrow morning.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> I’m so sorry. I can even it out for you right now, free of charge, of course.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> I appreciate that, but won’t that make it even shorter? {{3}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Only by a centimeter or so. Or we could add some soft layers so the difference doesn’t show.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{4}} I don’t want to look completely different in the photos.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Absolutely. Have a seat, and I’ll take it slowly.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two members of a university choir, three days before a concert","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Anna:</b> Mint, do you have a second? I need to ask you a huge favor.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> {{5}} Last time you said that, I ended up carrying chairs for three hours.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Anna:</b> Ha, this is different, I promise. I’ve lost my voice, and the concert is on Saturday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Oh no. {{6}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Anna:</b> Exactly. The director says you’re the only one who knows the solo well enough to take over.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Me? I’ve only ever sung it in rehearsals, never in front of an audience. {{7}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Anna:</b> You’ll be brilliant. Your voice suits that song better than mine, anyway.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Now you’re just flattering me. But fine, I’ll do it, on one condition.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Anna:</b> Name it.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> {{8}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Anna:</b> Deal. I’ll be there every evening, croaking along with you.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two friends talk about a scholarship and a family business","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> So, have you told your parents about the scholarship yet?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Not yet. {{9}} Dad’s been talking about me taking over the printing shop since I was ten.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> But a master’s in digital marketing in Australia is a big deal. Surely they’d be proud of you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Proud, yes. Happy, I’m not so sure. {{10}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> I get that. But it’s your life, not theirs. {{11}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> That’s easy for you to say. Your family doesn’t depend on you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Fair enough. Sorry, I didn’t mean it like that. Have you thought about a compromise?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Maybe. Actually, the shop is moving more into online orders, {{12}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Exactly. You’d be coming home with skills they actually need.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A traveller phones her travel insurance company from an airport abroad","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> SafeRoute Travel Insurance, Daniel speaking. How can I help?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Hi. I’m stuck at the airport in Sapporo. My flight home was cancelled because of a snowstorm, and {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I’m sorry to hear that. Could I take your policy number, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Sure, it’s TR 4471 9902.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you. I can see you’re on our Standard plan. {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> That’s a relief. I’m on a flight tomorrow evening now, but I need somewhere to stay tonight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Your plan covers up to 3,000 baht a night for a hotel, as long as you keep the receipts.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Three thousand? {{15}} Everything near the airport is at least five thousand tonight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I understand, but I’m afraid the limit is fixed. {{16}} Hotels in the city center are usually cheaper, and your plan covers the train fare there and back.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> OK, that could work. And my suitcase? Nobody seems to know where it is.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> That’s covered too. If it’s delayed over twelve hours, you can claim up to 2,000 baht for essentials like clothes and toiletries.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{17}} I’m literally wearing the only clothes I have.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Then buy what you need today. Just make sure you get a property irregularity report first. {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> A what report? Sorry, I’m a bit frazzled.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> It’s a form that confirms your bag is missing. {{19}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Got it. I’ll go to the baggage desk right after this call.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Perfect. I’ve emailed you a claim form. Anything else?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> No, I think that’s everything. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> You’re welcome. Stay warm, and have a safe trip home.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">HEALTH CENTER NOTICE</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Free Flu Vaccination Clinic</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Northfield University Health Center<br><b>Monday 2 March to Friday 13 March</b>, 9 a.m. to 3 p.m. (closed 12 to 1 p.m.)</span><span class=\"xv-p\">The flu virus changes from year to year, so last season’s vaccine will not fully protect you this season. Protection begins about two weeks after the injection.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">WHO IS ELIGIBLE</b><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Students</b>All currently enrolled students: free. Bring your student ID card.</span><span><b>Staff</b>Full time and part time staff: free. Bring your staff card.</span><span><b>Staff families</b>Family members of staff aged 18 or over: 350 baht per person.</span></span><b class=\"xv-t2\">HOW TO BOOK</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Book a slot in the HealthHub app at least 24 hours in advance.</span><span><b>2</b>Fill in the health questionnaire in the app.</span><span><b>3</b>Arrive 10 minutes early, and stay 15 minutes after your injection.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Walk ins are accepted on Fridays only, if slots remain. If you have a fever on the day, please reschedule. Anyone who has reacted badly to a flu vaccine before must see a doctor first.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">GRADUATING? DON’T DRAG IT. DON’T DUMP IT.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">MoveOut Market</b><span class=\"xv-p\">We collect the furniture and appliances you no longer need, clean them, and sell them to new students in August. You get paid when your item sells.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">WHAT YOU EARN</b><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Under 1,000 baht</b>You keep 60% of the selling price.</span><span><b>1,000 baht or more</b>You keep 70% of the selling price.</span><span><b>Unsold after 60 days</b>Donated to a local shelter, or returned to you for a 200 baht delivery fee.</span></span><b class=\"xv-t2\">HOW IT WORKS</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Upload photos of your items on our website by 31 May.</span><span><b>2</b>We confirm a pickup time within three working days.</span><span><b>3</b>We collect for free from any dorm within 5 km of campus.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>Early bird bonus:</b> book a pickup before 15 May and we add 100 baht to every item that sells.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">We do not accept mattresses, pillows, or items with cracks or missing parts. Pickups beyond 5 km cost 150 baht.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: A Digital Detox Weekend at Slow Hill Farm","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When my phone told me I was spending seven hours a day staring at it, I decided something had to change. A friend recommended Slow Hill Farm, a family run property in the hills of Mae Kham offering a “Digital Detox Weekend”: two nights, three meals a day, and no devices allowed. I booked it, half curious and half dreading it.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The rules are strict. On arrival, guests hand over every phone, tablet, and smartwatch, which are sealed in envelopes and locked in a safe until Sunday afternoon. There is no wifi and only one clock, in the dining hall. At first, I kept reaching for a pocket that was no longer heavy. By the evening, though, the itch had faded into something closer to relief.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The activities were the highlight. We picked coffee cherries, learned to cook a curry from scratch over a wood fire, and spent Saturday night lying on mats, naming stars we did not know the names of. None of it was sophisticated, but all of it demanded the kind of attention I usually spread across three screens at once.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The weekend was not flawless, however. The rooms, while spotless, are basic, with thin mattresses and shared bathrooms that could use more hot water. At 6,900 baht per person, the price is hard to justify on comfort alone, and guests expecting a spa will be sorely disappointed. I also found the group meditation sessions on Sunday morning rather too long; several of us were visibly fidgeting by the end.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) So, did it work? Partly. My Sunday calm lasted until about Wednesday, when my screen time crept back to its old levels. Yet the weekend left me with one lasting habit: I now leave my phone in the kitchen when I go to bed. If you are looking for a miracle cure, look elsewhere. If you want a gentle reminder of what life feels like with your eyes up, Slow Hill Farm is worth every baht.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Old railway becomes a park in the sky, but not everyone is cheering</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) HARBORTON, 18 October: After nine years of planning, the 2.3 kilometer Skyline Walk opened on Saturday, turning a rusting elevated railway that had been closed since 2009 into a landscaped park nine meters above the city’s old warehouse district. More than 40,000 people visited over the opening weekend, nearly double the number organizers had expected.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) “This district has been in decline for two decades,” said deputy mayor Lena Ortiz. “Every study we commissioned showed that green space draws investment. We expect new cafés, studios, and apartments within five years.” Its designers also claim the park will lower summer temperatures in the streets below by up to two degrees.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Not all local business owners share her optimism. Several long established shops, including a hardware store that has operated beneath the tracks for over sixty years, fear that rising rents will force them out. “Everyone talks about what the park will bring,” said its owner, Somchai Wattana, 67. “Nobody talks about who it will push away. My landlord has already hinted that my rent will go up by forty percent next year.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Researchers who study similar projects say both sides have a point. Dr. Mei Tan, an urban geographer at Harborton University, noted that elevated parks in other cities have often raised nearby property values within a few years, benefiting owners while squeezing out tenants. “Green space is not neutral,” she said. “Whether it helps a neighborhood or replaces it depends on decisions made long before the first tree is planted.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) In response to such concerns, the city council last week approved a rent stabilization scheme for 120 small businesses within 200 meters of the park, limiting annual rent increases to five percent for the next ten years. Critics say the scheme covers too few shops and came too late. Ortiz admitted that the scheme “is not a perfect solution” but insisted that “doing nothing would have been far worse.”</span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">How first year students use their phones</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Average minutes per day spent on each type of app, weekdays and weekends (survey of 500 first year students)</span><span class=\"en7-tb\"><span><b>Type of app</b><b>Weekdays</b><b>Weekends</b></span><span><b>Social media</b><i>142</i><i>198</i></span><span><b>Video streaming</b><i>96</i><i>175</i></span><span><b>Messaging</b><i>64</i><i>58</i></span><span><b>Gaming</b><i>38</i><i>85</i></span><span><b>Study and productivity</b><i>47</i><i>12</i></span><span><b>Music</b><i>25</i><i>30</i></span><span><b>Other</b><i>18</i><i>22</i></span><span class=\"en7-tot\"><b>Total</b><i>430</i><i>580</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Tourism on two islands, 2019 to 2025</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Line chart: tourist arrivals per year (thousands). Table: average length of stay (nights per tourist).</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tourist arrivals on two islands, 2019 to 2025\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"148.0\" y2=\"148.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"151.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"110.0\" y2=\"110.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"113.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"72.0\" y2=\"72.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"75.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">120</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"37.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">160</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2019</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text><text x=\"186\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2023</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2024</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2025</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,72.0 72,152.8 110,167.0 148,119.5 186,81.5 224,53.0 262,43.5\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"72.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"152.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"167.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"119.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"81.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"53.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"43.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"39.5\" class=\"lb\">Pearl Island</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,110.0 72,157.5 110,162.2 148,105.2 186,67.2 224,62.5 262,48.2\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"110.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"157.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"162.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"105.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"67.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"62.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"48.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"57.2\" class=\"lb\">Coral Island</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Year</b><b>2019</b><b>2020</b><b>2021</b><b>2022</b><b>2023</b><b>2024</b><b>2025</b></span><span><b>Pearl</b><i>5.0</i><i>6.0</i><i>7.0</i><i>5.5</i><i>4.5</i><i>4.0</i><i>3.6</i></span><span><b>Coral</b><i>3.0</i><i>3.5</i><i>4.0</i><i>3.5</i><i>3.2</i><i>3.0</i><i>3.0</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-mut\">Arrivals (thousands), Pearl Island: 120, 35, 20, 70, 110, 140, 150. Coral Island: 80, 30, 25, 85, 125, 130, 145.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Visitor nights = number of tourists × average length of stay.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>The Option Nobody Chooses","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Imagine you are at a cinema snack counter. A small popcorn costs 60 baht and a large one costs 120 baht. Faced with this choice, most people buy the small one; doubling the price seems a lot to pay for more of the same. Now imagine that the menu also lists a medium popcorn, also for 120 baht. Hardly anyone buys it, yet its mere presence changes behavior. Suddenly the large size, which costs no more than the medium, looks like a bargain, and sales of large popcorn rise. The medium option has done its job without ever being chosen.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Marketers call this the decoy effect, and psychologists refer to it, rather less memorably, as asymmetric dominance. The decoy is deliberately designed to be worse than one option, the target, in every respect, while being better than the other option, the competitor, in some respects and worse in others. Because the target clearly beats the decoy while the competitor does not, the comparison makes the target appear more attractive than it would on its own. Crucially, the decoy adds no new information about the real options. The price and size of the small and large popcorn are exactly what they were before; only the context has changed.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Why should an irrelevant option matter? Classical economic theory holds that it should not. A rational chooser who prefers A to B should continue to prefer A to B regardless of whether a third option is added, and the decoy effect is a direct violation of that principle. For that reason, it attracted considerable attention when it was first documented in the early 1980s. One explanation is that people rarely judge value in absolute terms. Asked whether a popcorn is worth 120 baht, most of us have no idea. Asked whether it is better than a smaller one at the same price, the answer is obvious. Comparisons are easy and absolute judgments are hard, so the mind seizes on whichever comparison is most readily available.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The effect has since been reported far beyond the snack counter, in choices involving restaurant menus, subscription plans, job applicants, and even candidates in laboratory elections. It is also, however, less reliable than popular accounts suggest. Several large replication attempts have found that the effect weakens or disappears when options are shown as pictures rather than numbers, or when people are already familiar with the products. In some studies, an obviously inferior decoy even backfired, making shoppers suspicious and pushing them toward the competitor instead.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) None of this means that the decoy effect is a myth. It means that, like many findings in behavioral science, it is a tendency rather than a law, strongest when choices are unfamiliar, features are easy to compare, and decisions are made quickly. For consumers, the practical lesson is modest but useful. When a choice suddenly seems easy, it is worth asking which option made it seem so, and whether you would still want the same thing if that option vanished from the menu.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านการตัดสินใจที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ ตัวอย่างราคาเป็นตัวอย่างสมมติ</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Problem with Normal","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In 1995, the fisheries scientist Daniel Pauly published a short essay that gave a name to a problem many researchers had sensed but never clearly described. Each generation of scientists, he argued, accepts as its baseline the size and variety of fish populations that existed at the start of its own career, and measures later losses against that point. When the next generation begins work, it takes the already reduced populations as its starting point. Over decades, the baseline quietly slides downward, and enormous declines can go unnoticed because no one working remembers what came before. Pauly called this the shifting baseline syndrome.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The idea soon spread well beyond fisheries, and researchers began to find the same pattern among ordinary people. In one widely cited study of fishing communities in the Gulf of California, older fishers named several species they had once caught in large numbers, while younger fishers working the same waters often did not know that those species had ever been common there. Similar gaps have been recorded in the way different generations describe the birdlife of their local countryside. In each case, the younger group was not ignorant or careless; it simply had nothing to compare the present with.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Part of the problem is psychological. Human beings are remarkably good at adapting to their surroundings, a capacity that serves us well in most areas of life. The same flexibility, however, means that gradual change rarely feels like change at all. A forest that loses one percent of its birds each year looks much the same from one spring to the next; only over a lifetime does the silence become noticeable, and by then it may be the only forest a child has ever known. Our sense of what is normal, in other words, is set by experience rather than by history.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Part of the problem is also practical. Scientific monitoring programs rarely last more than a few decades, and many began only after major changes had already taken place. Conservation targets are often based on these records, which means that a population restored to its “historical” level may in fact be restored only to its level of the 1970s or 1980s, itself a fraction of what once existed. To push their baselines further back, some researchers have turned to unusual sources: old restaurant menus, newspaper photographs of prize catches, ships’ logbooks, and the diaries of naturalists. Such sources are imprecise, but they can reveal that what we now consider abundant would once have been regarded as scarce.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Recognizing the syndrome does not by itself solve anything, and some critics warn that an obsession with a lost golden age can lead to unrealistic goals. Ecosystems have always changed, they point out, and returning to a single fixed point in the past is neither possible nor necessarily desirable. Yet the concept remains valuable as a corrective. It reminds us that “normal” is a moving target, and that the most dangerous losses are often the ones we can no longer see.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากแนวคิดทางนิเวศวิทยาที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies leave Canada and the northern United States and fly thousands of kilometers to a handful of mountain forests in central Mexico, {{61}} they spend the winter clustered on the branches of fir trees. Remarkably, no single butterfly ever completes the round trip. The monarchs that fly south belong to a special generation that lives {{62}} than its summer born parents, surviving for up to eight months instead of a few weeks. In spring they begin the journey north but die along the way, leaving three or four {{63}} generations to finish it. For decades, the destination was a mystery. Not until 1975 {{64}} the wintering sites, after a long search helped by volunteers who had tagged butterflies. Exactly how insects that have never made the trip find the same forests is still debated; {{65}}, there is strong evidence that they navigate by the sun, using an internal clock to adjust for its movement.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">If you have ever looked out of an airplane window, you may have noticed that its corners are rounded. This is not simply a matter of style. In 1954, two Comet jets, the first jet airliners to carry paying passengers, broke apart in midair within months of each other. {{66}} the wreckage recovered from the sea, investigators concluded that the cabin had been torn open by metal fatigue, a weakening caused by repeated cycles of stress. Each time a jet climbs, its cabin is pressurized and expands slightly; on landing, it shrinks again. Sharp corners {{67}} this stress instead of spreading it, so cracks tend to form there first. The evidence showed that the fatal crack {{68}} at the corner of an opening in the cabin roof. {{69}} the danger been fully understood earlier, such openings would surely have been designed differently. Rounded shapes allow stress to flow smoothly around an opening; {{70}}, they are now used on every passenger jet.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Of the roughly 7,000 languages spoken today, around 40 percent are considered endangered, and the number of languages with fewer than a thousand speakers {{71}} much larger than most people realize. In many cases, the decline began when parents, hoping to give their children better opportunities, stopped speaking their own language at home. Many older speakers now wish they {{72}} it on when they had the chance. When a language disappears, the loss is not only linguistic; knowledge of local plants, customs, and ways of thinking can {{73}} with it. Some communities, however, are fighting back. In New Zealand, Māori “language nests” were set up in the early 1980s so that preschool children could grow up hearing the language every day. {{74}} such efforts can fully reverse decades of decline is a question only time will answer, but they have already shown that a language’s fate is not {{75}}.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>When the wax reaches the top of the bottle, it is farther from the heat, so it cools, becomes denser again, and slowly sinks back toward the base, where the whole cycle begins once more.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>A lava lamp consists of a glass bottle filled with a clear liquid and lumps of colored wax, which at room temperature is slightly denser than the liquid around it and therefore rests at the bottom.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>As a result, the warmed wax begins to rise in soft, irregular blobs through the cooler liquid above it, stretching and splitting as it goes.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>When the lamp is switched on, a bulb hidden in its base gently heats the wax, causing it to expand and become less dense than the surrounding liquid.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Mass is the amount of matter that an object contains, and it stays exactly the same wherever the object happens to be, whether on a mountaintop, at the bottom of the sea, or in space.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>An astronaut with a mass of 70 kilograms on Earth, for example, would still have a mass of 70 kilograms on the Moon, yet she would weigh only about one sixth as much there because the Moon’s gravity is so much weaker.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In everyday speech, people often use the words “mass” and “weight” as if they meant the same thing, but scientists draw a clear distinction between the two.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Weight, by contrast, is the force with which gravity pulls on that matter, so it changes depending on the strength of gravity in a particular place.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Curious, he placed various objects between the tube and the screen and found that the mysterious rays passed easily through paper, wood, and even human flesh, but were blocked by bone and metal.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>To his surprise, a screen coated with a fluorescent chemical on a nearby bench began to glow faintly, even though the tube was completely covered and the room was dark.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>A few weeks later, he produced the first X ray photograph of a part of the human body, an image of his wife’s hand in which her bones and her wedding ring could be clearly seen.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>In November 1895, the German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen was experimenting with a cathode ray tube that he had covered with thick black cardboard so that no light could escape from it.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>In recent years, many small historic towns have become victims of their own popularity, attracting far more visitors than their narrow streets and ancient buildings were ever designed to handle.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Such measures are often unpopular with tour companies, but supporters argue that without them, the very charm that draws tourists in the first place could eventually disappear.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>The crowds bring money, but they also drive up rents, push local residents out of the center, and turn neighborhoods that once had bakeries and schools into rows of souvenir shops.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Some towns have responded by limiting the number of daily visitors, charging entry fees for day trippers, or banning new short stay rental apartments in their old centers.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The deep reds and purples of some species are a different matter, however, since these pigments are newly produced in autumn rather than simply uncovered.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>As the days grow shorter and cooler, trees stop producing chlorophyll, and the green gradually breaks down, revealing the yellow and orange pigments that were there all along.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>The brilliant colors of autumn leaves are not created in autumn at all; most of them have been hidden inside the leaves throughout the summer.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>During the growing season, leaves are packed with chlorophyll, a green pigment that captures sunlight for photosynthesis and is so abundant that it masks every other color.</span></span>"]},"EN70D_I":"","EN70D_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: A student knocks on a neighbor’s door in a university dormitory late at night","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Hi, sorry to bother you so late. I’m from the room next door. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Oh no, is it the music? I honestly thought these walls were thicker than that.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Apparently not. I’ve got an exam at eight tomorrow morning, and {{2}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Ah, I had no idea. We’re celebrating my flatmate’s birthday. {{3}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> That would be great, thanks. And could you possibly keep it down after midnight, too?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Midnight? To be honest, the party’s only just getting started. {{4}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Deal. That works for everyone. And happy birthday to your flatmate.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two friends on a mountain hiking trail","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Look at those clouds over the ridge. They weren’t there twenty minutes ago.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> They’ll probably blow over. We’re nearly at the viewpoint, and {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> I get that, but the ranger said storms up here can roll in really fast. {{6}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Come on, you’re such a worrier. {{7}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> For a start, we could slip on the wet rocks on the way down. Look, {{8}} We can come back early on Saturday, when the forecast is clear.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Fine, you win. But you’re buying lunch on the way home.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two flatmates look at their electricity bill","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mai:</b> Have you seen this month’s electricity bill? It’s 4,200 baht! Last month it was only half that.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Wow. {{9}} It hasn’t been any hotter than usual.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mai:</b> Oh, I don’t know. {{10}} Could it be a brand new gaming PC that stays on all night?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Point taken. {{11}} I have been leaving it on to download games overnight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mai:</b> Partly? Anyway, I’m not paying for your hobby. {{12}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Fair enough. I’ll cover the extra 2,100, and I’ll set it to sleep mode at night from now on. Promise.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A video call interview for a summer internship at a small travel company","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Hi, Krit. Can you hear me all right? My connection keeps freezing.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Yes, loud and clear now. {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Not at all. So tell me, why Greenleaf Journeys? We’re tiny compared with the big agencies.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> {{14}} At a big agency, I’d probably just be making photocopies. Here I’d actually get to help plan trips.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Fair point. Our interns do get thrown in at the deep end. Last summer, one of them ran a homestay tour on her own when a guide got sick. {{15}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> I think I’d manage. I led a volunteer camp for forty students in Nan last year, and things went wrong almost every day.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Forty students! {{16}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> It certainly did. On the second day, our bus broke down halfway up a mountain.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Oh dear. So what did you do?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> {{17}} I got everyone off the bus, found a farmer with a pickup truck, and we took turns riding to the village in small groups.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Resourceful. I like that. Now, {{18}} This job involves a lot of emailing overseas clients.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> I’d say it’s good, though not perfect. I write all my reports in English, and I’m working on making my emails sound less stiff.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> I appreciate the honesty. One last thing: we can only offer a small allowance. {{19}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Not at all. At this stage, the experience matters far more to me than the money.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Reyes:</b> Great. {{20}} We’ll let all the candidates know by Friday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Thank you. I look forward to hearing from you.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">WHY BREAK THE BANK FOR A ONE DAY SHOOT?</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Lensbox Media Loan Desk</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Room 104, Communication Arts Building<br><b>Open Monday to Friday</b>, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.</span><span class=\"xv-p\">Filming a class project or recording a podcast? Borrow professional equipment for far less than it would cost to buy, and simply bring it back when you are done.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">DAILY LOAN FEES (per kit)</b><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Camera Kit</b>Mirrorless camera, two lenses and a tripod: 350 baht per day</span><span><b>Audio Kit</b>Two wireless microphones and a portable recorder: 150 baht per day</span><span><b>Lighting Kit</b>Three LED panels with stands: 200 baht per day</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>WEEKEND SPECIAL:</b> Collect any kit on Friday, return it by 10 a.m. on Monday, and pay for one day only.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">*A refundable deposit of 2,000 baht per kit is required. Kits may be borrowed for up to 3 days at a time. Late returns are charged at twice the daily fee for each extra day. Damaged or missing items are repaired or replaced at the borrower’s cost. Please bring your student ID card.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">IT’S NEVER TOO LATE TO MAKE A SPLASH</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Learn to Swim for Adults</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Chalerm Park Aquatic Centre<br>Nervous around water? Our patient, certified coaches have helped more than 1,500 adults swim their very first lap.</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Level 1: Water Confidence</b>For complete beginners. Breathing, floating and standing up safely. Tue and Thu, 6:30 to 7:30 p.m.</span><span><b>Level 2: First Strokes</b>For learners who can already float. Front crawl and backstroke basics. Mon and Wed, 7:00 to 8:00 p.m.</span><span><b>Level 3: Stamina</b>For learners who can swim 25 meters. Build up to 200 meters without stopping. Sat, 8:00 to 9:30 a.m.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>8 lessons per course: 2,400 baht</b> (Level 3: 2,800 baht)<br>Sign up together with a friend and you BOTH save 10%.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Minimum age 18. No more than 6 learners per coach. Missed lessons cannot be made up. Not sure of your level? Book a free 15 minute assessment at the front desk.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: The Parla Go Pocket Translator","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Before my exchange semester in a small town in northern Italy, I knew exactly four words of Italian, and two of them were types of pasta. My host university warned that few people in the town spoke English, so I paid 4,900 baht for the Parla Go, a handheld translator about the size of a chocolate bar. After five months of daily use, I can say that it took the edge off my first weeks abroad, even if it never let me forget that I was a foreigner.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Setting it up took ten minutes. You hold down a button, speak, and the device reads out a translation in a clear, if slightly robotic, voice while the text appears on its small screen. An offline mode covers 20 languages, so I could use it on trains and in mountain villages with no signal, although offline translations were noticeably less accurate than online ones.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) For practical tasks, it was a lifeline. It got me through opening a bank account, explaining an allergy to a pharmacist and querying, politely, a wrong bill at a café.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Conversation is another matter. Every exchange felt stilted: you speak, you wait, the other person listens to a machine, and only then do they reply. Jokes and idioms came out flat at best and meaningless at worst. One elderly neighbor was so puzzled by its version of a local saying that we both ended up laughing, which was, in its own way, a kind of communication.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) The battery lasts around two days, and the price includes two years of online translation; after that, the service costs 900 baht a year. My only real complaint is the protective case, which cracked within a month.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Would I buy it again? Yes, but as a safety net rather than a substitute for learning the language. By my third month I was reaching for it less and less, which is, I suspect, exactly how it should be.</span><span class=\"xp\"><b>Rating: 4 out of 5</b></span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ สินค้าในรีวิวเป็นสินค้าสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Hillcrest school swaps letter grades for written feedback, and the debate begins</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) For the first time in its 60 year history, Hillcrest Secondary School will not print a single A, B or C on its report cards this term. Instead, 1,140 students in Years 7 to 9 will receive written comments from each teacher, plus a label showing whether they are “beginning,” “developing” or “secure” in each skill.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The pilot, approved by the district education board in March, will run for two years. Older students preparing for national exams will keep traditional grades.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Head teacher Sunee Wattana says the change targets what she calls “grade chasing.” “Too many of our students look at the letter and throw the comments in the bin,” she said. “A grade tells you where you are. Feedback tells you how to get somewhere else.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Supporters point to a smaller trial the school ran last year with two Year 8 classes. Teachers reported that students in those classes were more willing to redo assignments voluntarily, and the number of students asking for extra help after class rose from about 4 a week to 15.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Some parents worry that the labels are too vague. “If my daughter is ‘developing’ in maths, is she nearly top of the class or close to failing?” asked parent Anan Prasert. “I don’t want to find out when she gets her first exam result in two years.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Teachers have raised a different concern: time. Writing individual comments for 150 students can take an entire weekend, said one science teacher. The school has promised two extra planning days each term, though the teacher described this as “a drop in the ocean.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(8) Education researcher Dr. Mali Thongdee of Northgate University said evidence from similar schemes abroad was “encouraging but mixed.” “Comments without grades can help students focus on improving,” she said, “but only if teachers have the time to write comments that are actually useful. Vague praise is no better than a letter.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(9) The school will survey students, parents and teachers each term, and the board will decide later whether to extend the scheme.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โรงเรียน บุคคล และตัวเลขในข่าวเป็นข้อมูลสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Where does your 75 baht go?</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Average breakdown of the price of one large iced latte (75 baht) at Bean There, a campus café, in 2026</span><span class=\"en7-piew\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 200\" class=\"en7-pie\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pie chart of the cost of one iced latte\"><path d=\"M100,100 L100.0,12.0 A88,88 0 0 1 186.4,116.5 Z\" fill=\"#E31414\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"143.4\" y=\"67.1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"en7-pl\">28%</text><path d=\"M100,100 L186.4,116.5 A88,88 0 0 1 89.0,187.3 Z\" fill=\"#F24A4A\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"133.1\" y=\"148.6\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"en7-pl\">24%</text><path d=\"M100,100 L89.0,187.3 A88,88 0 0 1 32.2,156.1 Z\" fill=\"#FF7A7A\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"72.9\" y=\"152.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"en7-pl\">12%</text><path d=\"M100,100 L32.2,156.1 A88,88 0 0 1 12.2,94.5 Z\" fill=\"#B3122E\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"46.4\" y=\"120.4\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"en7-pl\">12%</text><path d=\"M100,100 L12.2,94.5 A88,88 0 0 1 32.2,43.9 Z\" fill=\"#FFA3A3\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"47.6\" y=\"82.3\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"en7-pl\">10%</text><path d=\"M100,100 L32.2,43.9 A88,88 0 0 1 57.6,22.9 Z\" fill=\"#FFC9C9\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M100,100 L57.6,22.9 A88,88 0 0 1 89.0,12.7 Z\" fill=\"#C9B9BA\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M100,100 L89.0,12.7 A88,88 0 0 1 100.0,12.0 Z\" fill=\"#8A6F72\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></svg><span class=\"en7-lg\"><span><i style=\"background:#E31414\"></i>Rent and utilities<b>28%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#F24A4A\"></i>Staff wages<b>24%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#FF7A7A\"></i>Coffee beans<b>12%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#B3122E\"></i>Profit<b>12%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#FFA3A3\"></i>Milk<b>10%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#FFC9C9\"></i>Cup, lid and straw<b>6%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#C9B9BA\"></i>Card fees and other costs<b>6%</b></span><span><i style=\"background:#8A6F72\"></i>Sugar and syrup<b>2%</b></span></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Market prices of limes and shallots, January to June</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Average retail price at Talad Mai fresh market (baht per kilogram)</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Market prices of limes and shallots, January to June\"><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"250\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"250\" y1=\"148.5\" y2=\"148.5\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"151.5\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">30</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"250\" y1=\"111.0\" y2=\"111.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"114.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"250\" y1=\"73.5\" y2=\"73.5\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"76.5\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">90</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"250\" y1=\"36.0\" y2=\"36.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"39.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">120</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan</text><text x=\"82\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feb</text><text x=\"124\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mar</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Apr</text><text x=\"208\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jun</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"40,136.0 82,117.2 124,73.5 166,36.0 208,86.0 250,123.5\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"40\" cy=\"136.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"82\" cy=\"117.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"124\" cy=\"73.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"166\" cy=\"36.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"208\" cy=\"86.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"250\" cy=\"123.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"258\" y=\"127.5\" class=\"lb\">Limes</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"40,98.5 82,104.8 124,111.0 166,111.0 208,92.2 250,61.0\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"40\" cy=\"98.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"82\" cy=\"104.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"124\" cy=\"111.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"166\" cy=\"111.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"208\" cy=\"92.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"250\" cy=\"61.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"258\" y=\"65.0\" class=\"lb\">Shallots</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Month</b><b>Jan</b><b>Feb</b><b>Mar</b><b>Apr</b><b>May</b><b>Jun</b></span><span><b>Limes</b><i>40</i><i>55</i><i>90</i><i>120</i><i>80</i><i>50</i></span><span><b>Shallots</b><i>70</i><i>65</i><i>60</i><i>60</i><i>75</i><i>100</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>The Evidence That Never Came Home","<span class=\"xp\">(1) During the Second World War, the story goes, military analysts examined the bombers that returned from missions and marked where each one had been hit. The bullet holes clustered on the wings, the tail and the main body, while the engines showed surprisingly little damage. The obvious recommendation was to add armor where the holes were thickest. The statistician Abraham Wald is said to have argued the opposite. The planes being studied, he pointed out, were the ones that had made it back. Hits to the wings and tail were evidently survivable. The missing holes were on the planes that had never returned, and they pointed to the engines as the parts most in need of protection.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Whether every detail of this story is accurate is still debated, but the error it describes has a name: survivorship bias. It occurs when we draw conclusions from the examples that have passed through some kind of filter while ignoring those that did not. Because the failures are no longer around to be counted, the sample that remains looks more successful or more impressive than the full picture would justify.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Once you know to look for it, the bias turns up almost everywhere. Books about successful companies often study a handful of famous firms and list the habits they share. Yet many firms that went bankrupt may have shared exactly the same habits; they simply did not last long enough to be written about. Similarly, people often remark that buildings in the past were built to last, unlike modern ones. But the old buildings we admire today are the few that endured. The poorly built ones collapsed or were pulled down long ago.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The bias can also skew how we judge our own chances. Stories of university dropouts who became billionaires are told and retold, while the many more dropouts who struggled are rarely interviewed. A young musician who studies only the careers of stars may conclude that talent and hard work always pay off, without ever hearing from the equally gifted players who gave up after years of half empty venues. The lesson drawn from such stories is not necessarily wrong, but it rests on evidence that has been filtered in advance.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) What makes survivorship bias so stubborn is that the missing evidence is, by its very nature, invisible. To guard against the bias, careful researchers ask a simple but uncomfortable question before trusting any sample: what happened to the cases that are not here? In medicine, for instance, a study that follows only the patients who completed a treatment may make the treatment look better than it is, if those who dropped out did so because it was not working. Good studies therefore track everyone who started, not just those who finished.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) None of this means that successful examples have nothing to teach us. It means that they cannot teach us much on their own. Indeed, as with Wald’s bombers, the most important data may be precisely the data we never get to see.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากแนวคิดทางสถิติที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ เรื่องเล่าเกี่ยวกับเครื่องบินทิ้งระเบิดเป็นเรื่องที่เล่าต่อกันมาและรายละเอียดบางส่วนยังเป็นที่ถกเถียง</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>Why Richer Streets Are Greener","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Walk through almost any large city and you may notice something that ecologists have been measuring for two decades: the leafiest neighborhoods tend to be the wealthiest ones. In the early 2000s, researchers studying plant life in a desert city in the United States found that household income was a strong predictor of how many plant species grew in a neighborhood. They called the pattern the “luxury effect,” and it has since been documented in cities on several continents, not only for plants but also for birds, bats and insects.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The causes are partly obvious. Wealthier residents can afford larger gardens, and they can pay to plant and water trees. But history matters as much as money. Many neighborhoods that are now poor were built quickly and cheaply, with little space set aside for parks, while affluent districts were often designed with tree lined avenues from the start. A tree planted a century ago cannot be bought today at any price, so the gap tends to persist long after the original decisions were made.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The consequences go well beyond appearance. Trees cool the air through shade and through the water they release from their leaves, and on a hot afternoon a street with a full canopy can be several degrees cooler than a treeless one a few blocks away. Since poorer households are less likely to own air conditioners, they are the people who suffer most from heat, yet they often live in the hottest parts of the city. In this sense, the luxury effect is not simply an ecological curiosity; it is a matter of public health.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Some cities have tried to close the gap by planting trees where they are scarce. The results have been mixed. Young trees need years of watering and care, and programs that count the number of trees planted, rather than the number that survive, can look impressive on paper while achieving little. Residents have also raised a less expected concern. In several cities, new parks and greenery have made poor neighborhoods more attractive to wealthier buyers, pushing up rents and eventually forcing out the very people the trees were meant to help. Researchers call this “green gentrification.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) None of these problems is a reason to stop planting. They suggest, rather, that greening works best when it is done with residents rather than to them. Programs that involve local people in deciding where trees go, and that pair new parks with protections for renters, have a better chance of lasting. The aim, as one urban planner put it, is “to make the city greener without making it less welcoming.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) The luxury effect is ultimately a reminder that nature in cities is not distributed by nature. It is the result of choices about land, money and who gets to enjoy shade on a summer afternoon, and choices can be made differently.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากแนวคิดทางนิเวศวิทยาเมืองที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ คำพูดของนักวางผังเมืองเป็นข้อความสมมติ</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">In May 2000, the United States government stopped deliberately reducing the accuracy of the GPS signals available to the public. Within days, a computer consultant in Oregon hid a bucket of small items in the woods and posted its coordinates online, inviting others to find it. Since then, this simple idea {{61}} into a worldwide hobby known as geocaching. Players use a phone or a GPS device to {{62}} containers, or “caches,” hidden in parks, forests and even busy city streets. Most caches hold a logbook {{63}} finders write their names and the date. Some also contain small trinkets, which may be taken as long as something of equal or greater value is left behind. {{64}} a treasure hunt in the traditional sense, geocaching rarely involves anything valuable; the reward is the search itself. Not only {{65}} people outdoors, but it also leads them to places they would never otherwise have visited.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">Every evening, bakeries, cafés and supermarkets face the same problem: food that is perfectly safe to eat but can no longer be sold at full price the next day. The amount of bread and pastry thrown away in a single large city each night {{66}} surprisingly large. A growing number of apps now offer a solution. Shops list their leftover food in the afternoon, and users reserve a “surprise bag” through the app. {{67}} at a fraction of the usual price, these bags are collected just before closing time. Shop owners say the apps have stopped them from {{68}} food they once had no choice but to waste, while customers enjoy a cheap meal. The environmental benefit is real, too, since food waste {{69}} for roughly 8 to 10 percent of global greenhouse gas emissions. {{70}}, critics warn that the apps treat the symptom rather than the cause. If shops keep baking far more than they can sell, they argue, the problem has simply been moved, not solved.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Many adults who dream of playing a musical instrument never begin, convinced that they have missed their chance. “If I had started piano lessons as a child,” they say, “I {{71}} able to play properly now.” It is true that young brains adapt quickly and that, in general, the older we get, {{72}} it becomes to master fine finger movements. Yet adults bring strengths of their own. They can understand music theory, plan their practice sensibly, and show greater {{73}}: the ability to keep going even when progress feels painfully slow. Teachers usually recommend that an adult beginner {{74}} for twenty minutes a day rather than for hours at the weekend, because short, regular sessions are far easier to fit into a busy life. {{75}}, starting late is rarely the real obstacle; giving up too early usually is.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>As long as there is liquid water in the pot, the temperature at the bottom cannot rise much above 100 degrees Celsius, because the heat is used up in turning water into steam.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>A small magnet under the pot loses its magnetism at this higher temperature and releases a switch, which turns the heater down so that the rice is kept warm instead of burning.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>A basic electric rice cooker has no clock, no timer and no computer chip, yet it almost always seems to know exactly when the rice is ready.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Once all of the water has been absorbed by the rice or boiled away, however, nothing is left to hold the temperature down, and it begins to climb above that point.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>In 1971, an American engineer named Ray Tomlinson sent a message from one computer to another across a network, creating what we now call email.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>His choice proved so practical that, more than half a century later, the same little symbol appears in billions of email addresses all over the world.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>To make this work, Tomlinson needed a symbol that would separate the user’s name from the name of the computer, and it had to be one that never appeared in people’s names.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>He chose the @ sign, which was already on his keyboard and which, conveniently, meant “at” in English, so that an address read naturally as a user at a particular machine.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The result is jet lag: you may feel wide awake at midnight and exhausted at noon, and your appetite and digestion may be upset for several days.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>When you fly quickly across several time zones, however, this internal clock is suddenly out of step with the sun and with the local timetable.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Because the clock can shift by only about an hour a day, travelers are often advised to spend time in bright daylight at their destination, which helps to reset it more quickly.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Deep inside the brain, a tiny group of cells acts as a master clock, telling the body when to feel sleepy, when to feel hungry and when to be alert.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Because it can double the area it covers in about two weeks, it soon spread into rivers and canals, where thick mats of it now block boats, trap rubbish and reduce the oxygen in the water.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Villagers dry its long, spongy stems and weave them into baskets, bags and even furniture, turning a costly problem into a welcome source of income.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Clearing it by machine is expensive, and the plants quickly grow back, so a number of communities have begun to see the weed as a resource rather than a pest.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Water hyacinth, a floating plant with attractive purple flowers, was brought to Thailand more than a century ago to decorate ponds and gardens.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Critics, on the other hand, worry that if every lecture is available online, many students will simply stop coming to class and miss the discussion that happens there.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Many universities now record their lectures and post them online, a practice that has become far more common in recent years.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Some lecturers have found, however, that attendance stays high when class time is used for activities that a recording cannot provide, such as debates and group problem solving.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Supporters argue that recordings allow students to review difficult material at their own pace, which is especially valuable for those studying in a second language.</span></span>"]},"EN70E_I":"","EN70E_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: At a self service laundromat","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me, are you the attendant? Machine number six just swallowed my coins and stopped halfway through the cycle. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Attendant:</b> Let me guess. The little screen is flashing E4?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Yes! {{2}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Attendant:</b> Not really. It does that when someone overloads the drum, and it looks like you’ve stuffed about two weeks of laundry in there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Three, actually. {{3}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Attendant:</b> Fair enough. I’ll restart it for you, but I’d split the load between two machines. Otherwise it’ll just stop again.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{4}} I’ve only got enough coins left for one more wash.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Attendant:</b> Tell you what, the second machine is on the house this time. Just don’t make a habit of it.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: On board a flight","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> Excuse me, I’m sorry to bother you. {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Flight attendant:</b> Of course. What seems to be the problem?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> The man next to me has been coughing nonstop since we took off, and he hasn’t covered his mouth once. I’d hate to catch something right before my exams.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Flight attendant:</b> I see. {{6}} Let me check whether anything is free further back.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> Thanks. Honestly, I’d take a middle seat next to the toilets at this point.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Flight attendant:</b> Well, {{7}} There’s an empty aisle seat in row 32, although it doesn’t recline.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> That’s perfectly fine. {{8}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Flight attendant:</b> No trouble at all. I’ll bring you a mask as well, just in case.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two friends talk after one of them receives an email","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Why the long face? You look like someone cancelled your birthday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Close enough. You know that internship at Northbridge Design? I just got the email. {{9}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Oh no, I’m sorry. Did they at least say why?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Just the usual line: “We were impressed by your portfolio, but we have decided to move forward with other candidates.” {{10}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Ouch. Still, if they liked your portfolio, you clearly got further than most people.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Maybe. But I’d already told my parents I’d be working there this summer. {{11}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Come on, it’s one company. {{12}} You’ve got three more applications waiting for replies, haven’t you?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Four, actually. All right, maybe it isn’t the end of the world.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: Two cousins who run their family’s homestay read a new online review","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> Have you seen the review that went up last night? Two stars, and the headline says “Lovely garden, shame about the hosts.”</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> I saw it. {{13}} I was up half the night reading it over and over.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> Same here. They say the hot water ran out and nobody answered the phone after nine.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> Well, the water heater did break that evening. {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> Sure, but they didn’t know that while they were standing in a cold shower. And the phone?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> I was at the night market with some friends. {{15}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> You suppose? It’s the one thing we promise on our website: someone is always reachable.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> All right, all right. {{16}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> Thank you. Now the question is how we reply.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> Do we have to? {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> That’s wishful thinking. People always read the bad ones first. I think we thank them, apologize for the water and the phone, and explain what we’ve changed.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> {{18}} We haven’t actually changed anything yet.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> Then let’s change something. What if we bought a backup water heater and set up a rota, so one of us always keeps the phone on at night?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> A rota? {{19}} Fine, but I’m not taking Saturdays.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nok:</b> Deal. I’ll draft the reply tonight, and you can check it over in the morning.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tam:</b> {{20}} And Nok, thanks for not saying “I told you so.”</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">DON’T BIN IT. FIX IT.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Fixit Corner Repair Café</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Second Sunday of every month</b>, 1 p.m. to 5 p.m. (last item accepted at 4 p.m.)<br>Ban Mai Community Hall</span><span class=\"xv-p\">Bring us your broken lamps, torn jackets, wobbly chairs and bicycles with flat tyres. Our volunteer fixers repair things <b>WITH you, not FOR you</b>, so you go home with a working item and a new skill.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">HOW IT WORKS</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Register your item at the door and describe the problem.</span><span><b>2</b>Wait for a free fixer. Tea is on us.</span><span><b>3</b>Repair it together at the table. Spare parts, if needed, are sold at cost.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p\">Repairs are free. Donations help us buy tools and spare parts.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">For safety reasons, we cannot accept microwaves, gas appliances or phones with swollen batteries. Our fixers do their best, but no repair is guaranteed. When more than 20 people are waiting, only one item per person can be registered.</span><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Handy with a screwdriver or a sewing machine?</b> We need more volunteer fixers. Ask at the welcome desk.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">BE PART OF THE STORY</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Lumen Short Film Festival 2027: Volunteers Wanted</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>18 to 24 January</b>, Riverside Arts Centre</span><span class=\"xv-p\">We need 60 volunteers for three teams.</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Front of House</b>Welcome audiences, check tickets and show guests to their seats. Minimum of 3 shifts.</span><span><b>Subtitle Team</b>Translate and time Thai subtitles for international films. Work from home from 1 December. Excellent English required.</span><span><b>Artist Care</b>Meet visiting directors at the airport and assist them during their stay. Must be 20 or over and hold a driving licence.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>What you get:</b> a pass to every screening, a volunteer certificate, lunch on each shift, and an invitation to our Meet the Directors evening.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">All volunteers must attend a training day on 10 January. Subtitle Team applicants will also take a short translation test. Applicants under 18 need written permission from a parent or guardian. Applications close on 15 November.</span><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Apply online or at the box office.</b></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review of The Margin, a Pay by the Hour Study Café","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When The Margin opened on Charoen Road last spring, it promised students something Bangkok’s coffee shops rarely offer: a guaranteed seat, a power socket, and permission to stay as long as they like, all for 40 baht an hour. Having spent most of my exam month there, I can report that it keeps two of those three promises.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The seats are the real triumph. Every desk has its own lamp, a socket and a shelf for bags, and the chairs were clearly designed by someone who has sat through a long revision session. The silent room upstairs is policed with almost religious strictness; a staff member once asked me to stop clicking my pen. Downstairs, a “murmur zone” allows quiet group work, although “quiet” is interpreted generously in the evenings.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Staying as long as you like, however, comes with a catch. The hourly charge is capped at 200 baht a day, which sounds generous until you notice that the cap applies only to members, who pay a monthly fee of 300 baht. A non member who settles in for a ten hour day will pay 400 baht, more than I spend on food in two days. The pricing is not hidden, exactly, but it is printed in small grey type.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Drinks are included in name only. The “free refills” turned out to be hot water and a single kind of tea bag; anything else costs extra, at prices that would embarrass a hotel. I soon started bringing my own snacks, which the staff tolerated, if not encouraged.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) My real complaint, though, is the internet. Fast in the morning, it slows to a crawl after 6 p.m., precisely when the place fills up. Twice I had to finish an online quiz on my phone.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Would I go back? Yes, but strategically. For a member who studies in the morning, The Margin is the best value in the neighbourhood. For anyone who needs to download lecture videos at night, it is an expensive lesson in patience.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ร้านในรีวิวเป็นร้านสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Drones fly medicine to villages cut off by floods</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Health officials in Nam Sai Province have begun using drones to deliver medicine to villages that floodwaters have cut off from the rest of the province for almost two weeks.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Since the service began on Monday, six drones operated by the provincial hospital have made 140 flights to 11 villages, carrying insulin, blood pressure tablets, antibiotics and test kits. Each drone can carry up to five kilograms and fly 30 kilometres on a single charge.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) “Before this, a delivery to the furthest village meant a four hour boat trip, and only when the current was safe,” said Dr. Pimchanok Rattana, who leads the project. “Now the same journey takes 25 minutes, and the medicine arrives cold.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Insulin, which must be kept between 2 and 8 degrees Celsius, travels in insulated boxes fitted with temperature sensors. If the temperature leaves that range during a flight, the box sends a warning to the pilot, and the medicine is replaced on the next flight.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) In Ban Khok Wan, one of the worst affected villages, village health volunteer Somsak Inthon collects each package from a landing spot painted on the roof of the local school. “People used to ration their tablets, taking one every two days instead of every day to make them last,” he said. “That has stopped.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) The project has not been without problems. Strong winds grounded the drones for most of Wednesday, and two packages fell into the water after a sensor failed. Both were recovered undamaged.</span><span class=\"xp\">(8) Some experts also warn against expecting too much from the technology. “A drone can drop a box of tablets, but it cannot notice that a patient’s foot is infected,” said Wanida Sook, a public health researcher. She argues that the province should invest just as much in training village volunteers to carry out basic health checks.</span><span class=\"xp\">(9) Officials say the drones will keep flying until the roads reopen, which could take another three weeks. After that, the hospital hopes to run a smaller service for mountain villages that are difficult to reach even in the dry season.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ จังหวัด บุคคล และตัวเลขในข่าวเป็นข้อมูลสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Four ways to travel from Ravenport to Kessling</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Door to door travel time, one way cost and carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions per passenger for a single trip</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Mode</b><b>Door to door time</b><b>One way cost (baht)</b><b>CO2 per passenger (kg)</b></span><span><b>Plane</b><i>3 h 30 min</i><i>1,850</i><i>96</i></span><span><b>Express train</b><i>6 h 00 min</i><i>1,200</i><i>24</i></span><span><b>Coach</b><i>7 h 30 min</i><i>650</i><i>30</i></span><span><b>Car (driver alone)</b><i>6 h 30 min</i><i>1,600*</i><i>120</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">*Fuel and road tolls for the whole car. If passengers share the car, the cost and the CO2 per passenger are divided equally among everyone in it.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Applicants and admitted students, Faculty of Design, Eastlake University</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Number of people who applied to the faculty and number who were admitted each year, 2019 to 2025</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Applicants and admitted students, Faculty of Design, 2019 to 2025\"><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"161.0\" y2=\"161.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"164.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">500</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"136.0\" y2=\"136.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"139.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">1,000</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"111.0\" y2=\"111.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"114.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">1,500</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"86.0\" y2=\"86.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"89.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">2,000</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"61.0\" y2=\"61.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"64.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">2,500</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"36.0\" y2=\"36.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"39.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">3,000</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2019</text><text x=\"76\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2023</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2024</text><text x=\"256\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2025</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"40,126.0 76,111.0 112,66.0 148,81.0 184,96.0 220,51.0 256,36.0\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"40\" cy=\"126.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"76\" cy=\"111.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"112\" cy=\"66.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"81.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"184\" cy=\"96.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"220\" cy=\"51.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"256\" cy=\"36.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"39.0\" class=\"lb\">Applicants</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"40,171.0 76,171.0 112,170.0 148,168.5 184,168.0 220,168.0 256,167.2\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"40\" cy=\"171.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"76\" cy=\"171.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"112\" cy=\"170.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"168.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"184\" cy=\"168.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"220\" cy=\"168.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"256\" cy=\"167.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"170.2\" class=\"lb\">Admitted</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Year</b><b>2019</b><b>2020</b><b>2021</b><b>2022</b><b>2023</b><b>2024</b><b>2025</b></span><span><b>Applicants</b><i>1,200</i><i>1,500</i><i>2,400</i><i>2,100</i><i>1,800</i><i>2,700</i><i>3,000</i></span><span><b>Admitted</b><i>300</i><i>300</i><i>320</i><i>350</i><i>360</i><i>360</i><i>375</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>The Tasks That Will Not Leave Us Alone","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Sometime in the 1920s, a group of psychologists in Berlin were sitting in a café when one of them, Kurt Lewin, noticed something odd about their waiter. The waiter had kept a long, complicated order in his head without writing anything down, yet when he was asked about it shortly after the bill had been paid, he could recall almost none of it. The order, it seemed, had stayed in his mind only for as long as it remained unfinished.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Lewin’s student Bluma Zeigarnik turned this observation into a series of experiments. She gave participants simple tasks, such as solving puzzles or shaping figures out of clay, and interrupted some of them before they could finish. When she later asked participants to list the tasks, they recalled the interrupted ones considerably more often than the completed ones. The finding, published in 1927, became known as the Zeigarnik effect, and the explanation she offered was elegant: starting a task creates a kind of mental tension, which is released only when the task is done.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The idea has proved remarkably durable outside the laboratory. Television writers end episodes on cliffhangers, and some teachers deliberately stop a lesson in the middle of a problem, trusting that students will keep turning it over on the way home. Productivity advisers, for their part, recommend beginning an unpleasant task for just five minutes, on the theory that once it has been started, the discomfort of leaving it unfinished will pull us back to it.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Yet the effect is less solid than its popularity suggests. Later researchers who tried to repeat Zeigarnik’s experiments obtained mixed results; some even found that people remembered completed tasks better, particularly when they felt that being interrupted reflected badly on their ability. A recent review that pooled decades of studies concluded that there is little consistent evidence that unfinished tasks are better remembered. What did hold up was a related tendency first documented by another of Lewin’s students, Maria Ovsiankina: people who are interrupted show a strong urge to go back and complete what they started.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) That urge has a cost. Unfinished goals tend to intrude on our thoughts at unwelcome moments, which is one reason a half written essay can keep a student awake long after the lights are off. Interestingly, a 2011 study suggested that these intrusions can be quietened without finishing the task at all. Participants who were asked to make a specific plan for when and how they would complete an outstanding goal were troubled by it far less afterwards, as though the mind accepted the plan as a reliable promise.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) The lesson, then, is not that we should leave things unfinished in order to remember them, a claim the evidence does little to support. It is rather that the mind treats an open task as unfinished business, and that it can be satisfied by something short of completion: a clear decision about what will happen next.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านจิตวิทยาเรื่องงานที่ทำค้างไว้ซึ่งเป็นที่รู้จัก</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>Speaking in Whistles","<span class=\"xp\">(1) On the steep island of La Gomera, in the Canary Islands, a shepherd standing on one side of a ravine can hold a conversation with a friend on the other side, more than a kilometre away, without raising his voice at all. Instead, he whistles. The language he is using, known as Silbo Gomero, is not a handful of agreed signals like those used by sailors or referees. It is Spanish, transferred whole into whistled form.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Whistled languages of this kind have been documented in dozens of communities around the world, from northern Turkey to the Amazon and Mexico. They nearly always arise in the same kind of landscape: rugged mountains or dense forest, where people work far apart and the ordinary voice fades long before it reaches its listener. A whistle, by contrast, concentrates its energy in a narrow band of high frequencies that carries much further and stands out against the low rumble of wind and rivers.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) How can a whistle carry an entire language? The answer depends on the language being whistled. In tonal languages, where a change in pitch can change the meaning of a word, whistlers mainly reproduce the melody of speech. In non tonal languages such as Spanish or Turkish, they imitate something subtler: the way the shape of the mouth alters the sound of each vowel and consonant, which they translate into rises, falls and breaks in the whistle. Much of the detail of ordinary speech is inevitably lost, so listeners rely heavily on context, much as we do when making out a conversation in a noisy room.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Whistled speech has also intrigued brain scientists. In most people, spoken language is processed mainly in the left hemisphere of the brain, whereas melodies and other non linguistic sounds draw more heavily on the right. In a study published in 2015, researchers tested whistlers in a Turkish village and found that, when they listened to whistled Turkish, both hemispheres were involved roughly equally. The result suggests that the brain’s division of labour is more flexible than textbooks often imply, and that the form in which language arrives can shape how it is handled.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) For all their ingenuity, most whistled languages are in steep decline. Roads have replaced mountain paths, and mobile phones now do the work that whistles once did, more conveniently if not more cheaply. Young people who move to cities have little reason to learn a skill that takes years to master and is useless on a crowded street. Several whistled languages are thought to have vanished within living memory without ever being properly recorded.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) La Gomera offers a rare counterexample. In 1999, when only a small number of elderly islanders were still fluent, the regional government made Silbo Gomero a compulsory subject in the island’s schools. Today, thousands of Gomeran children can whistle at least a little. Whether a language kept alive in classrooms rather than in ravines remains quite the same language is a question its teachers sometimes ask themselves; but it is, at the very least, still being whistled.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากข้อมูลและงานวิจัยเรื่องภาษาผิวปากที่เผยแพร่แล้ว</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">In 1806, a Boston businessman named Frederic Tudor filled a ship with 130 tons of ice cut from frozen ponds and sent it to the Caribbean island of Martinique. Friends warned him that the ice {{61}} long before the ship reached the tropics, and at first they were right: much of the cargo melted, and Tudor lost a fortune. {{62}} to his critics, the story would have ended there. Instead, he spent years testing ways to keep ice cold and found that sawdust, a waste product {{63}} local sawmills had plenty, slowed melting dramatically. Within a few decades, ice from New England was being shipped as far as India. His trade also helped to {{64}} a habit that now seems perfectly ordinary: expecting a cold drink in a hot country. The business lasted until machines that could make ice artificially became common in the late nineteenth century. {{65}}, those machines owed much of their early demand to the taste for cold drinks that Tudor himself had created.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">The axolotl, a pale salamander {{66}} only in the lakes and canals around Mexico City, has fascinated scientists for well over a century. Unlike most amphibians, it never goes through metamorphosis; {{67}}, it keeps the feathery gills and long tail of its young stage throughout its adult life. Even more remarkable is its ability to regrow lost body parts. An axolotl that loses a leg can grow a perfect replacement within weeks, and it can also repair its spinal cord, heart and even parts of its brain. What allows these animals to rebuild such complex structures without leaving scars {{68}} unclear, although researchers suspect that certain immune cells play a key role. Because of polluted water, invasive fish and expanding cities, its wild population has {{69}} so sharply that surveys now struggle to locate any animals at all. Some biologists fear that the species {{70}} from parts of its original home already.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Despite its name, a “lead” pencil contains no lead at all. Its core is made of graphite, a soft form of carbon that leaves a dark mark on paper. When a large deposit of the mineral was discovered in Borrowdale, England, in the sixteenth century, people mistook it for a type of lead, and the name {{71}} ever since. Pure Borrowdale graphite was so valuable that the mine was closely guarded. Not until 1795 {{72}} a reliable way to make good pencils without it. In that year, a French engineer named Nicolas Conté mixed powdered graphite with clay and baked the mixture in a kiln. His method also made it possible {{73}} pencils of different hardness simply by changing the amount of clay, a system that is still used today. Many art teachers insist that a beginner {{74}} a range of these pencils before moving on to paint. Although digital devices have become {{75}} in classrooms, billions of pencils are still produced every year.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The headphones then generate a second sound wave that is an exact mirror image of the first, with its peaks falling precisely where the original wave has its troughs.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Active noise cancelling headphones do not simply block out sound with thick padding around the ears; instead, they fight unwanted noise by creating even more sound.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>When the two waves meet inside the ear cup, they cancel each other out, so the listener hears far less of the steady hum of an aircraft engine or the rumble of a train.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>To achieve this, tiny microphones on the outside of each ear cup continuously pick up the sound waves arriving from the surroundings and send them to a small processor.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Intrinsic motivation, by contrast, comes from within: a person learns a language because she finds its sounds beautiful, or practises the guitar simply because playing it is satisfying.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Psychologists generally distinguish between two broad sources of motivation, depending on whether the reward for an activity lies outside it or inside the activity itself.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Extrinsic motivation drives us when we act in order to gain something separate from the activity, such as a good grade, a higher salary or the approval of other people.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Research suggests that the second type tends to produce more lasting effort, which is one reason teachers are often advised not to rely on prizes and rewards alone.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>To his surprise, it took him about a month to solve the puzzle he had created, since every turn that put one piece in place seemed to move several others out of position.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>In 1974, Ernő Rubik, a young lecturer in design in Budapest, built a small wooden cube to help his students understand how objects can move in three dimensions.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>After it was launched internationally in 1980, the toy became one of the best selling puzzles in history, with hundreds of millions of copies sold around the world.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Realizing that other people might enjoy the same challenge, he applied for a patent and began working with a Hungarian toy company to produce the cube.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Even a fragment no larger than a coin travels at several kilometres per second, so a collision with it can disable a satellite or puncture the wall of a crewed spacecraft.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Several solutions are now being tested, from nets and harpoons that capture dead satellites to rules requiring operators to bring old spacecraft down within a few years of the end of their missions.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Since the first artificial satellite was launched in 1957, the space around Earth has gradually filled with the remains of old rockets, broken satellites and fragments from collisions.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Worse still, each such collision creates thousands of new fragments, raising the risk of a chain reaction that could eventually make some orbits too dangerous to use.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Honey contains very little water, so any bacteria that land in it lose moisture to the surrounding sugar and are unable to multiply.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Archaeologists have reported finding pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that, after thousands of years, still appeared to be safe to eat.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In addition to being dry, honey is also fairly acidic, and an enzyme added by bees produces small amounts of hydrogen peroxide, which further slows the growth of microbes.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>The reason for this remarkable resistance to spoiling lies not in any preservative added by humans but in the chemistry of honey itself.</span></span>"]},"EN70F_I":"","EN70F_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: A customer returns to a dry cleaner the day before a job interview","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Good morning. Is everything all right with your order?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Not exactly. I picked up this shirt yesterday, and look, there’s a yellow mark right on the collar. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Oh dear, I see it. I’m terribly sorry. That may well be from our pressing machine.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> The thing is, I have a job interview at nine tomorrow, and it’s my only white shirt. {{2}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> I can’t promise it’ll come out completely, but we’ll treat it again this afternoon at no charge.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> And if it doesn’t come out?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{3}} We’d cover the cost of a replacement shirt of the same quality.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Well, I suppose that’s fair. {{4}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Absolutely. It’ll be ready by five, and I’ll text you the moment it’s done.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two backpackers talk in a hostel common room","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> Guess what? I’ve signed us both up for the sunrise trek tomorrow. The van leaves at four.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> Four in the morning? {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> Come on, the reviews all say the view from the top is out of this world.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> I’m sure it is, but my legs are still killing me from yesterday’s bike ride. {{6}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> Well, the guide says it’s only a ninety minute climb, and it’s mostly steps.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> Only ninety minutes of steps. {{7}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> OK, OK. Look, if you really don’t feel up to it, I can cancel your place. It’s free to cancel until eight tonight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> {{8}} I’d hate to miss it and regret it later. Just promise me there’s coffee at the top.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: A student asks a classmate to lend him a camera for a weekend wedding","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> You look worried. Is something wrong?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Sort of. {{9}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Sure, if I can. What’s going on?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> My friend’s wedding is on Saturday and her photographer has pulled out. Could I borrow your camera to take the photos?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> I’d love to help, but {{10}} It belongs to the photography club, and only members can sign it out.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Couldn’t you just sign it out and pass it to me? Nobody would know.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{11}} If it broke, I’d be the one paying.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Fair enough. I shouldn’t have asked.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Don’t worry. Actually, the club rents its older cameras to non members for 300 baht a day.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> {{12}} Could you put me in touch with whoever handles that?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Sure. I’ll send you the secretary’s number.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: The captain of a university esports team meets the owner of a café","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Hi, Mr. Wong. Thanks for seeing me. {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> Not at all. Your message said something about a viewing night?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> That’s right. Our university esports team has reached the national final, and it’s being streamed live on Saturday the fourteenth. We’d love to watch it here on your big screen with our supporters.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> Sounds fun. How many people are we talking about?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{14}} Probably around sixty, if everyone who has signed up actually turns up.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> Sixty? Hmm. On a Saturday night I’d normally have my regular customers in. {{15}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> I completely understand. What if we booked only the upstairs area? That way your regulars wouldn’t be disturbed.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> The upstairs room only seats forty, I’m afraid.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{16}} We could ask the rest to watch the stream from home.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> All right. Now, I normally charge a room fee of 3,000 baht for private events.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Ouch. {{17}} We’re a student team, and that would eat up most of our budget.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> Tell you what. If every guest orders at least one drink, I’ll waive the fee.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> That’s very generous of you. {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> Good. Just one more thing. The last group that used the room left it in a terrible state.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{19}} We’ll stay behind to clean up, and I’ll make sure nobody brings in outside food.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Wong:</b> Then we have a deal. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Brilliant. I’ll send you the final list by Wednesday.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">NOTICE TO ALL POOL USERS</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Pine Hill University Aquatic Center reopens on 3 February</b><span class=\"xv-p\">After four months of renovation, our 50 meter pool is open again with new heating and changing rooms.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">WHAT HAS CHANGED</b><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Online booking</b>All lane swimming must now be booked in the PHU Sport app, no more than 48 hours in advance. Each booking is for one 45 minute slot.</span><span><b>Speed lanes</b>Lanes 1 and 2: fast (under 2 minutes per 100 meters). Lanes 3 to 6: medium. Lanes 7 and 8: slow swimmers and learners only.</span><span><b>Open swim</b>Sundays, 1 p.m. to 5 p.m. Lane ropes are removed and no booking is needed.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>Students 40 baht, staff 60 baht, public 120 baht per slot</b><br>Students and staff who book 10 slots in a single booking pay for only 8.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">*Slots cancelled less than 6 hours before the start time are charged in full. Users who miss three booked slots in one calendar month cannot make new bookings for 14 days. Swimming caps must be worn at all times.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">PLAY NOW. OWN IT LATER.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Tempo House Rent to Own</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Not sure you will stick with it? Rent a brand new instrument by the month, and let your rent pay for it.</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Acoustic guitar</b>500 baht a month (shop price 6,000 baht)</span><span><b>Ukulele</b>250 baht a month (shop price 2,000 baht)</span><span><b>Digital keyboard</b>750 baht a month (shop price 12,000 baht)</span></span><b class=\"xv-t2\">HOW IT WORKS</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Pay a refundable deposit equal to one month’s rent, and take your instrument home.</span><span><b>2</b>Keep renting for as long as you like. The minimum rental period is three months.</span><span><b>3</b>Once your rent payments reach 75% of the shop price, the instrument is yours to keep.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">After the minimum period, you may return your instrument at any time and your deposit will be refunded, minus any repair costs. Rent already paid cannot be refunded. Guitars and ukuleles come with a free monthly check up and string change.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: The Korvi Pulse Lite Fitness Watch","<span class=\"xp\">(1) I bought the Pulse Lite, a 1,990 baht fitness watch from Korvi, for one reason: I wanted to know why I wake up exhausted even after eight hours in bed. The box promises “lab grade sleep insights,” a phrase that, after six weeks of wearing the watch every night, I can only describe as generous.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The hardware is hard to fault at this price. The watch is so light that I often forgot I had it on, the screen stays readable in direct sunlight, and the battery lasted eleven days between charges, almost twice as long as a friend’s far more expensive model. Setting it up took less than five minutes.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The sleep data is another matter. To test it, I kept a handwritten log of when I actually fell asleep and woke up. On most nights the watch was within twenty minutes of my notes, but it repeatedly recorded me as asleep while I was lying still and reading on my phone, and on two nights it credited me with ninety minutes of “deep sleep” that I am fairly sure I spent awake, worrying about an exam. It seems to measure stillness far more reliably than sleep.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) More frustrating is what happens after the first month. The detailed sleep reports that drew me to the watch are free for thirty days only; after that, they cost 99 baht a month. Without the subscription, you are left with a bare total of hours slept, information a bedside clock could give you.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) So is it worth buying? As a step counter and an everyday watch, comfortably yes. As a sleep scientist on your wrist, no. Ironically, the most useful thing the experiment produced was my own notebook, which showed that I was going to bed at wildly different times every night. Fixing that, not the watch, is what finally made my mornings easier.</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Talk, don’t type: university brings back the oral exam</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Students at Eastbridge University will soon face a new kind of final assessment: a ten minute conversation with their lecturers. The university announced on Monday that, from next semester, any course in which more than half of the grade comes from take home essays must also include a short oral examination, known as a viva, worth at least 20 percent of the final mark.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The decision follows a review which found that suspected cases of students submitting work produced by artificial intelligence tools had risen from 37 in 2023 to 412 last year. “An essay on its own no longer tells us reliably what a student understands,” said Professor Helen Okafor, the university’s vice president for teaching. “A conversation does. It is difficult to explain an argument you did not write.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Under the new system, students will be asked to talk through the reasoning behind work they have already submitted and to answer one or two further questions. Lecturers will not be grading accent or fluency, Professor Okafor stressed, but whether students can “own” their ideas.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Reactions have been mixed. Many lecturers welcomed it, although some asked how they would find time to examine classes of 200 students one by one. The university says it will hire 40 additional teaching assistants and allow vivas to be held online. The student union has raised concerns about students who experience severe anxiety when speaking and about those studying in a second language. “We are not against the idea,” said union president Daniel Reyes, “but a ten minute conversation can be just as unfair as an essay if the support isn’t there.” The university has promised that students with a documented anxiety disorder will be able to request a written alternative.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Some students are cautiously optimistic. “It might be less stressful than a three hour exam,” said Pim Chaiyasit, a second year economics student. A trial run in the philosophy department last year found that average marks fell slightly, while the number of failing grades dropped by almost a third, a pattern researchers are still trying to explain.</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Riverside Campus Cafeteria: lunch menu at a glance</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Price, energy and protein for one standard serving of each dish</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Dish</b><b>Price (baht)</b><b>Energy (kcal)</b><b>Protein (g)</b></span><span><b>Chicken rice</b><i>45</i><i>600</i><i>25</i></span><span><b>Basil pork rice</b><i>50</i><i>650</i><i>30</i></span><span><b>Tofu salad</b><i>55</i><i>300</i><i>18</i></span><span><b>Noodle soup</b><i>40</i><i>400</i><i>16</i></span><span><b>Grilled fish set</b><i>80</i><i>500</i><i>40</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">*Extra boiled egg with any dish: 10 baht, 70 kcal and 6 g of protein per egg. Meal card holders get 10% off the price of any dish listed at 50 baht or more; extras are not discounted.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Water levels in two reservoirs, January to October</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Water stored at the start of each month, as a percentage of each reservoir’s full capacity. North Ridge holds 800 million cubic meters when full; Lotus Valley holds 500 million cubic meters.</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Water level in two reservoirs, January to October\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0%</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"152.0\" y2=\"152.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"155.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">20%</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"118.0\" y2=\"118.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"121.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">40%</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"84.0\" y2=\"84.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"87.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">60%</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"53.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">80%</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"16.0\" y2=\"16.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"19.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">100%</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"259\" y1=\"126.5\" y2=\"126.5\" class=\"gl\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"129.5\" class=\"ax\">35% limit</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan</text><text x=\"59\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feb</text><text x=\"84\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mar</text><text x=\"109\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Apr</text><text x=\"134\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May</text><text x=\"159\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jun</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jul</text><text x=\"209\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Aug</text><text x=\"234\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text><text x=\"259\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,80.6 59,92.5 84,106.1 109,121.4 134,133.3 159,136.7 184,124.8 209,97.6 234,67.0 259,48.3\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"80.6\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"59\" cy=\"92.5\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"84\" cy=\"106.1\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"109\" cy=\"121.4\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"134\" cy=\"133.3\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"159\" cy=\"136.7\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"184\" cy=\"124.8\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"209\" cy=\"97.6\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"234\" cy=\"67.0\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"259\" cy=\"48.3\" r=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"264\" y=\"56.3\" class=\"lb\">North Ridge</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,104.4 59,109.5 84,112.9 109,118.0 134,121.4 159,118.0 184,99.3 209,78.9 234,60.2 259,55.1\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"104.4\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"59\" cy=\"109.5\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"84\" cy=\"112.9\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"109\" cy=\"118.0\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"134\" cy=\"121.4\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"159\" cy=\"118.0\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"184\" cy=\"99.3\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"209\" cy=\"78.9\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"234\" cy=\"60.2\" r=\"2.4\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"259\" cy=\"55.1\" r=\"2.4\"/><text x=\"264\" y=\"52.1\" class=\"lb\">Lotus Valley</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb10\"><span><b>Month</b><b>Jan</b><b>Feb</b><b>Mar</b><b>Apr</b><b>May</b><b>Jun</b><b>Jul</b><b>Aug</b><b>Sep</b><b>Oct</b></span><span><b>N. Ridge</b><i>62</i><i>55</i><i>47</i><i>38</i><i>31</i><i>29</i><i>36</i><i>52</i><i>70</i><i>81</i></span><span><b>L. Valley</b><i>48</i><i>45</i><i>43</i><i>40</i><i>38</i><i>40</i><i>51</i><i>63</i><i>74</i><i>77</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Water use restrictions apply in any month in which a reservoir starts below 35% of its capacity.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>The Tune Only You Can Hear","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In 1990, a graduate student at Stanford University named Elizabeth Newton asked people to take part in what sounded like a children’s game. One person, the “tapper,” was given a list of well known songs, such as “Happy Birthday,” and asked to tap out the rhythm of one of them on a table. A second person, the “listener,” had to guess the song. Before the listeners made their guesses, the tappers were asked to predict how often they would succeed.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The tappers expected the listeners to identify about one song in two. In fact, of the 120 songs tapped during the experiment, listeners recognized just three, a success rate of 2.5 percent. The gap between prediction and reality was not a matter of poor tapping. It arose because the tappers could not stop hearing the melody in their heads as they tapped. To the listeners, who had no such accompaniment, the performance was little more than a series of disconnected knocks.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Economists had already given this problem a name. In a 1989 paper, Colin Camerer, George Loewenstein and Martin Weber described the “curse of knowledge”: once we know something, we find it remarkably difficult to imagine what it was like not to know it. Our knowledge does not simply sit alongside our picture of other people’s minds; it seeps into it, so that we quietly assume others share the background we take for granted.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The effect is most damaging precisely where expertise is greatest. A software engineer writing instructions for new users may skip steps that seem too obvious to mention, and a doctor may describe a test result as “unremarkable” without realizing that an anxious patient hears only vagueness. Teachers face the same trap. Having long since mastered their material, they may move through an explanation at a pace that feels natural to them but leaves beginners behind. Ironically, the better we understand something, the harder it can be to teach.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Simply trying harder does surprisingly little. In the 1989 experiments, participants continued to show the bias even when they stood to earn money by predicting accurately. The bias, it seems, is not a failure of effort but a feature of how knowledge works: once acquired, it cannot be switched off at will.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) What does help is changing the situation rather than relying on good intentions. Experienced writers test their drafts on readers who resemble the intended audience and watch where they hesitate. Some teachers ask students to explain an idea back in their own words before moving on, turning hidden confusion into something visible. Others make a habit of offering concrete examples, which give a newcomer something to hold on to where an abstract definition offers nothing. Such strategies do not lift the curse, but they compensate for it by bringing the listener’s perspective back into view.</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Newton’s study is often remembered as a curiosity, but its lesson reaches well beyond music. Whenever we are certain that our meaning is obvious, it is worth picturing the tappers, drumming confidently on the table while the tune played only in their own heads.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ งานวิจัยที่อ้างถึงเป็นงานวิจัยจริง</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Paths Nobody Planned","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Walk across almost any park or university campus and you will find them: narrow strips of bare earth cutting diagonally across the grass, often only a few meters from a perfectly good paved walkway. Planners call them desire paths, or desire lines. No one designs them. They appear because enough people, one after another, decide that the official route is not the one they want to take.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) A desire path begins with a single walker taking a shortcut. On its own, one set of footsteps leaves almost no mark. But each person who follows flattens the grass a little more, and a faint track is easier to see, and therefore more tempting to use, than untouched lawn. Within a season, a path that began as an individual choice has become a collective one, worn into the ground by hundreds of people who never met.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) For many years, the standard response was to fight back. Groundskeepers put up fences, planted hedges or posted signs asking people to keep off the grass. The results were often disappointing. Walkers stepped over low fences, squeezed through gaps in hedges or simply moved the path a few meters to one side. A route that saves time every day, it turns out, is remarkably hard to discourage.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Some designers have drawn a different lesson. Rather than treating desire paths as damage, they read them as information: a free, constantly updated record of where people actually want to go. A widely repeated story tells of a university that opened a new campus without any paths at all, waited a year to see where the grass wore away, and then paved the routes its students had chosen. Versions of the tale are attached to several institutions, and it may owe more to legend than to record, but the principle it illustrates is now taken seriously. Some cities have studied aerial photographs and the GPS traces of pedestrians and cyclists when deciding where new crossings and cycle lanes should go.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Desire paths are revealing partly because they expose a gap between two ways of seeing the same space. A planner looking at a map sees right angles, symmetry and tidy borders. A student late for a lecture sees only the distance between where she is and where she needs to be. Neither view is wrong, but only one of them belongs to the people who will cross that space every day.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) This does not mean that every shortcut deserves to be paved. Some desire paths cut through areas that need protecting, and others lead walkers straight into traffic. A track worn by people in a hurry tells planners what users want, not necessarily what is safe or wise. The trick is to treat the path as evidence to be weighed rather than as an instruction to be obeyed.</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Seen this way, desire paths are less an act of rebellion than a form of feedback, offered quietly and in large numbers by people who were never asked for their opinion. The real question for designers is whether they are willing to read it.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ แนวคิด desire path เป็นแนวคิดจริงในการวางผังเมือง ส่วนเรื่องเล่าในย่อหน้า 4 เป็นเรื่องที่เล่าต่อกันมาโดยไม่มีหลักฐานยืนยันแน่ชัด</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">The dodo, a large flightless bird that lived only on the island of Mauritius, has become a symbol of extinction, yet there is still some debate about {{61}} it actually died out. Having evolved without any natural predators, the bird was remarkably {{62}}, and it showed little fear of the Dutch sailors who began arriving in 1598. Hunting certainly played a part, but it was probably not the main cause. Each of the rats, pigs and monkeys that came ashore with the sailors {{63}} a threat to the bird’s eggs, which were laid in nests on the ground. Within a century of the sailors’ arrival, the dodo had vanished, and so little was left of it that some later writers doubted it had ever existed. Only in 1865, when a large collection of bones was dug out of a marsh, {{64}} to study the bird properly. Today it lives on mainly in the phrase “as dead as a dodo,” used for anything hopelessly {{65}}.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">Few runners know that the race’s official length of 42.195 kilometers is something of a historical accident. At the first modern Olympic Games in 1896, the marathon was roughly 40 kilometers. At the next few Games, the length varied {{66}} the route chosen by each host city. When London hosted the Games in 1908, organizers planned a course from Windsor Castle to the Olympic stadium, and the distance came to 26 miles and 385 yards, or 42.195 kilometers. It is often said that the start {{67}} beneath the castle windows so that the royal children could watch, though the story may be a legend. Had the 1908 course been a little shorter, today’s runners {{68}} a couple of hundred meters less to cover. Officials eventually stopped {{69}} the distance from one Games to the next, and in 1921 the odd figure became the official standard. For many runners, those final 195 meters are now {{70}} the most memorable part of the race.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Few buildings are as instantly recognizable as the Leaning Tower of Pisa, yet its famous tilt was never part of the plan. Construction began in 1173, but by the time workers reached the third floor, the tower had already started to sink into the soft, sandy ground {{71}} it stood. Work then stopped for almost a century, largely because of wars, and this long pause may well have saved the building, since it gave the soil time to settle and grow firmer. {{72}} in one continuous effort, the tower would almost certainly have collapsed. By the late twentieth century, however, the lean had become so severe that engineers feared the tower might {{73}} at any moment. In 1990, it was closed to visitors, and a team of experts recommended that small amounts of soil {{74}} from beneath its higher side. The result was {{75}}: the tower was straightened by about 45 centimeters, and it is now expected to remain stable for at least two hundred years.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Once the desired depth has been reached, small amounts of water are pumped into or out of separate trim tanks until the vessel neither rises nor sinks.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>To dive, the crew opens valves that allow seawater to flood into these tanks, making the vessel heavier than the water it displaces, so that it begins to descend.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>A submarine controls its depth by means of large compartments called ballast tanks, which are usually located between its inner and outer hulls.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>When it is time to return to the surface, compressed air is blown into the ballast tanks, forcing the water out and making the submarine light enough to rise again.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The word “quarantine” has its roots in the plague epidemics that swept through Mediterranean ports in the fourteenth century.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>It is this longer period, quaranta giorni in the Italian of Venice, that eventually gave English its word for any period of enforced isolation.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In 1377, the port city of Ragusa, now Dubrovnik in Croatia, ordered that ships arriving from infected areas wait thirty days before anyone on board could come ashore.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Venice and other ports later adopted similar rules but extended the waiting period to forty days, perhaps because the number forty had strong religious associations.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Shared electric scooters, which can be unlocked with a smartphone and left almost anywhere, have spread rapidly through cities around the world over the past decade.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Critics, however, point to scooters abandoned across pavements, where they block pedestrians and create a serious hazard for people with visual impairments.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Supporters argue that the scooters fill a gap in public transport, offering a quick and cheap way to cover the short distance between a station and an office.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>As a result, a growing number of cities now allow scooters to be left only in marked parking zones, hoping to keep the benefits while reducing the clutter.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>When these calls strike an object, part of the sound bounces back, and by analyzing the returning echoes, the bat can work out the object’s distance, size and even direction of movement.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>As it closes in on its prey, the bat calls faster and faster, until the separate calls merge into a final burst that researchers have nicknamed the “feeding buzz.”</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>They manage this by echolocation: as they fly, they produce a rapid series of high pitched calls, most of which are far too high for humans to hear.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Most bats hunt in complete darkness, yet they can catch insects as small as mosquitoes in mid air.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>For this reason, a number of education experts argue that basic financial literacy should be taught in secondary schools alongside traditional subjects.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Many young people leave school able to solve quadratic equations but unsure how the interest on a loan is calculated.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Such lessons need not be dull; a well designed course might ask students to plan a monthly budget for a first job or to compare the true cost of two phone contracts.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>This gap matters, because the financial decisions people make in their late teens and early twenties, such as taking out a student loan or a first credit card, can shape their lives for years.</span></span>"]},"EN70G_I":"","EN70G_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: At a key cutting kiosk in a shopping mall","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Hi, could you copy this key for me? My roommate flies home tonight with our only spare.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Locksmith:</b> Let me have a look. Ah, this is a security key with dimples on the side. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Oh. How much longer are we talking about?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Locksmith:</b> I’d have to order a blank from my supplier, so Thursday at the earliest.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Thursday? {{2}} I’d be locked out for three whole days.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Locksmith:</b> Well, your building office might keep a master copy.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{3}} They charge a fortune, and they want a form signed by the landlord.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Locksmith:</b> In that case, I could make you a temporary copy. It’ll last a few weeks, but it might stick a little.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{4}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Locksmith:</b> Great. Give me twenty minutes, then.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two student editors finishing the campus newspaper the night before it goes to print","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> The front page is done. What do you think of my headline: “Cafeteria Prices Soar, Students Starve”?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> It’s certainly eye catching. {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Exaggerating? Prices went up fifteen percent!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Sure, but nobody is actually starving. {{6}} The university could file a complaint.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Fine. What would you suggest, then?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> How about “Cafeteria Prices Up 15 Percent, Students Feel the Pinch”?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{7}} It’s accurate, but it isn’t going to make anyone pick up the paper.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> I’d rather be boring than wrong. Besides, the interviews inside are strong enough to carry the story.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{8}} We’ll go with yours, but I’m writing the next one.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Deal.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two teammates at a university hackathon at 3 a.m.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> The demo just froze again. That’s the third time tonight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> {{9}} We present in five hours, and the judges won’t sit there waiting for it to load.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> I think it’s the map feature. It eats up far too much memory.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Then let’s cut it. {{10}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Cut it? That’s the whole point of the app!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> I know it hurts, but a simple app that works beats a brilliant one that crashes. {{11}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Fine, but I’m keeping the code. We can put it back after the competition.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Absolutely. Now, coffee or a twenty minute nap?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{12}} If I close my eyes now, I won’t open them until lunch.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A student who makes ceramics talks to a café owner at a craft fair","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> These mugs are gorgeous. Did you make them all yourself?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I did. I’ve been working with clay since my first year at university. {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> Well, actually, yes. I run a café in the old town, and I’ve been looking for something handmade to sell at the counter. Would you be interested in supplying us?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{14}} How many were you thinking of?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> Let’s say forty mugs a month to start with. What would you charge?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> At the fair, I sell them for 450 baht each.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> That’s fine for a single customer, but I’d need to resell them. {{15}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I see. I could probably go down to 350 for an order that size.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> Hmm. I was hoping for something closer to 300.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{16}} Each mug takes me almost two hours, and the glaze alone costs a fortune.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> I understand. What if I paid you at the start of every month? That way you’d have cash for materials.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> That would definitely help. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> Shall we say 320, then, paid up front?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{18}} But there’s one more thing. Forty mugs a month is a lot for one person, especially during exams.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> Then let’s not tie ourselves to a fixed number. {{19}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> That sounds much more realistic. Could we put all this in writing?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dao:</b> Of course. {{20}} I’ll email you a draft agreement by Friday.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">ONE STUDENT’S LOSS IS ANOTHER STUDENT’S BARGAIN</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Northgate University Lost Property Auction</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Friday, 6 March, 1 p.m. to 5 p.m., Old Gymnasium</b><br>Over 600 unclaimed items, from umbrellas and water bottles to calculators, headphones, and a surprising number of left shoes.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">BEFORE THE AUCTION</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Items found on campus between June and December last year are listed on the Security Office website.</span><span><b>2</b>Owners may claim their items free of charge until 27 February. Bring your student card and describe the item.</span><span><b>3</b>Anything still unclaimed after that date goes to auction.</span></span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Viewing</b>11 a.m. to 1 p.m. on the day</span><span><b>Starting bids</b>From 20 baht</span><span><b>Payment</b>Cash or QR code only</span><span><b>Phones and laptops</b>Wiped and reset before sale</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\">Every baht raised goes to the Student Hardship Fund, which last year helped 140 students pay for textbooks and bus fares.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Wallets, ID cards, and bank cards are never auctioned. Items are sold as seen.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">NEVER HELD A PADDLE? PERFECT.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Riverside Dragons: Open Trial Week</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Monday 9 to Saturday 14 June, 6 a.m. to 7:30 a.m.</b><br>Join the oldest dragon boat club on the Mae Lin River for a free taster session. Twenty paddlers, one drum, one boat. Nothing builds a team faster.</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Who can join</b>Anyone aged 16 or over who can swim 50 meters</span><span><b>What to bring</b>Water, a change of clothes, and shoes you don’t mind getting wet</span><span><b>Provided</b>Paddles, life jackets, and a coach in every boat</span></span><b class=\"xv-t2\">LIKED IT? STAY WITH US.</b><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\">Student membership: <b>450 baht a month</b>, paid by the term (four months).<br>Sign up during Trial Week and your first month is free. Bring a friend who also signs up, and you each get 300 baht off your first term.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Sessions are cancelled when the river is above the warning mark, so check our page by 5 a.m. Paddlers under 18 need a parent’s signature on the safety form.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Glowtide Night Kayaking, Ban Lom Bay: Magical Water, Muddled Management","<span class=\"xp\"><b>Rating: 3 out of 5</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(1) The photos on Glowtide’s website show kayakers drifting through water that seems to be lit from below, every stroke of the paddle leaving a trail of blue sparks. I booked the 1,600 baht “Glow Paddle” as a birthday treat for a friend, half expecting the pictures to have been edited.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) They were not. Once we were away from the pier lights, every movement of the water produced flashes of light from tiny plankton, and our guide, Nok, explained clearly why the effect is strongest on moonless nights. Watching the light drip from my fingers was one of the most memorable moments of my year.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Getting there, however, was a different story. We were told to arrive at 7 p.m., but we did not set off until almost 8:15 because two vans of guests turned up late and the staff refused to leave without them. The safety briefing, when it finally came, was perfunctory: a two minute talk delivered while half the group was still hunting for life jackets that fit. Nobody asked whether we could swim.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The kayaks themselves were stable and clean, and the double seats meant nervous beginners could share with someone more confident. But the advertised “two hours on the water” turned out to be closer to seventy minutes, since the return was rushed when the tide turned. When I raised this, the office manager shrugged and said the tide “is not something we can control,” which is true but rather beside the point when its timing is printed in every local calendar.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Would I go again? Probably, but on my own terms. Book the earliest slot on a weeknight, bring insect repellent, and leave your phone in the van: most phone cameras cannot capture the glow, and you will only waste the trip trying. Glowtide has a natural wonder on its doorstep. What it lacks is the organization to match.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ บริษัทและสถานที่ในรีวิวเป็นข้อมูลสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Deposit scheme cuts takeaway waste at Eastbrook University, but not everyone is convinced</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Since the semester began, students buying takeaway food at Eastbrook University’s four cafeterias have paid an extra 30 baht for a reusable steel container, which is refunded when it is returned to any of twelve campus collection points. In its first three months, the scheme has cut the number of single use foam and plastic boxes thrown away on campus from around 9,000 a week to about 2,500, according to the university’s Sustainability Office.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) “Students were never short of good intentions,” said Dr. Rattana Silp, who heads the office. “What they were short of was a reason to act on them at one o’clock, with ten minutes between classes. Thirty baht turned out to be that reason.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) However, roughly one container in eight has not come back. The office believes many are simply sitting in dormitory rooms and will come back by the end of the semester. Others have been found in rubbish bins, which suggests that some students treat the deposit as part of the price of a meal rather than as money they can reclaim.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Vendors have mixed feelings. Somchai Prasert, who sells noodles in the Engineering cafeteria, said washing returned containers had added nearly an hour to his working day. “The idea is good,” he said, “but nobody asked us before the containers arrived, and nobody pays us for the extra work.” The Sustainability Office said it would trial a central washing station next month, which would relieve vendors of the task.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Students have raised another concern. A petition signed by more than 600 students argues that the deposit is unfair to those on tight budgets, who may struggle to spare 30 baht until a container is returned. In response, the university announced that students receiving financial aid would be given one container free of charge for the whole semester.</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Officials from at least three other universities have already visited the campus. The real test, Dr. Rattana said, would come next year, “when the novelty has worn off and the containers are no longer shiny.”</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ มหาวิทยาลัย บุคคล และตัวเลขในข่าวเป็นข้อมูลสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Green Week Recycling Challenge: Results</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Kilograms of recyclable material collected by three faculties in one week</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Material</b><b>Arts</b><b>Science</b><b>Engineering</b></span><span><b>Paper</b><i>420</i><i>260</i><i>180</i></span><span><b>Plastic bottles</b><i>150</i><i>210</i><i>240</i></span><span><b>Aluminum cans</b><i>60</i><i>90</i><i>135</i></span><span><b>Glass</b><i>70</i><i>40</i><i>45</i></span><span><b>Electronic waste</b><i>20</i><i>75</i><i>120</i></span><span class=\"en7-tot\"><b>Total</b><i>720</i><i>675</i><i>720</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Points: paper and glass 1 point per kilogram; plastic bottles 2 points; aluminum cans 3 points; electronic waste 5 points. The faculty with the most points wins.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Page Turner Books: Sales by Channel, 2019 to 2025</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Yearly sales of a family bookstore in its shop and through its website (million baht)</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"In store and online sales of a family bookstore, 2019 to 2025\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"146.0\" y2=\"146.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"149.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">2</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"106.0\" y2=\"106.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"109.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">4</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"66.0\" y2=\"66.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"69.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">6</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"26.0\" y2=\"26.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"29.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">8</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2019</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text><text x=\"186\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2023</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2024</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2025</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,26.0 72,76.0 110,106.0 148,86.0 186,76.0 224,86.0 262,96.0\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"26.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"106.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"86.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"86.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"96.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"104.0\" class=\"lb\">In store</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,176.0 72,136.0 110,96.0 148,106.0 186,96.0 224,76.0 262,56.0\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"176.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"136.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"96.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"106.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"96.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"76.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"56.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"54.0\" class=\"lb\">Online</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Year</b><b>2019</b><b>2020</b><b>2021</b><b>2022</b><b>2023</b><b>2024</b><b>2025</b></span><span><b>In store</b><i>8.0</i><i>5.5</i><i>4.0</i><i>5.0</i><i>5.5</i><i>5.0</i><i>4.5</i></span><span><b>Online</b><i>0.5</i><i>2.5</i><i>4.5</i><i>4.0</i><i>4.5</i><i>5.5</i><i>6.5</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>Pulling Their Weight? The Puzzle of Social Loafing","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In the late nineteenth century, a French agricultural engineer named Maximilien Ringelmann asked volunteers to pull on a rope, first alone and then in groups, while he measured the force they produced. Common sense suggested that eight people pulling together would produce eight times the force of one. They did not. The larger the group, the less each member pulled on average, as if every extra pair of hands made the others a little lazier.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Ringelmann put the shortfall down to poor coordination: people in a group, he reasoned, simply fail to pull at exactly the same moment. That explanation survived for decades, until researchers in the 1970s repeated the experiment with a twist. Participants were blindfolded and told that several others were pulling behind them, when in fact they were pulling alone. With no one to coordinate with, they still pulled less hard than when they believed they were on their own. Something other than timing was at work, and psychologists later gave it a name: social loafing.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Several forces seem to drive it. In a crowd, individual effort becomes hard to identify, so neither praise nor blame lands on any one person. When a member believes that the group will succeed or fail regardless of what she does, her contribution feels dispensable. There is also a quieter motive. People who suspect that their teammates are slacking often reduce their own effort rather than carry others on their backs. This is sometimes called the “sucker effect,” and it can spread through a team surprisingly fast.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Social loafing, however, is not a law of nature. A large review of studies published in the 1990s found that it shrinks when each person’s output can be seen, when the task feels meaningful, and when members care about the group itself. The same review found the effect to be weaker, though still present, in cultures that place a high value on the group over the individual. In other words, how hard we pull depends not only on the rope but also on the people holding it with us.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) For anyone who has suffered through a group project, the practical lessons are familiar: give every member a visible role, keep teams small, and let members evaluate one another. Yet there is a catch. If individual effort is tracked too closely, members may start to work for their own scores rather than for the shared result, undermining the very cooperation the group was formed to achieve. The aim, then, is not to stop people from leaning on one another but to make sure that everyone is still holding the rope.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านจิตวิทยาสังคมที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Last Note: Why Endings Rule Our Memories","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Ask someone to describe a holiday from years ago, and you are unlikely to hear an hour by hour account. Instead, you will hear about a handful of moments: the best meal, the worst argument, the last evening by the sea. Psychologists have found that this selectiveness is not random. When we judge a past experience, we rely heavily on two moments in particular, its most intense point and its ending, while paying surprisingly little attention to how long it lasted. This pattern is known as the peak end rule.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) One of the clearest demonstrations involved nothing more than a bowl of cold water. Volunteers held one hand in painfully cold water for sixty seconds. In a second trial, they held their other hand in the same water for sixty seconds and then for another thirty, during which the water was warmed very slightly, though it remained unpleasant. Asked which trial they would rather repeat, a clear majority chose the second one, even though it contained all the pain of the first and then some. The milder ending had rewritten the memory of the whole.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The effect is not confined to the laboratory. In a study of patients undergoing an uncomfortable medical examination, those whose procedure ended with a few minutes of milder discomfort later rated the whole experience as less unpleasant, even though their procedure had lasted longer. They were also more willing to return for a follow up examination, which suggests that the way an ordeal ends can influence decisions about our health.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Why should memory work this way? One explanation is that the mind does not store a continuous recording of events. It stores a few salient snapshots and builds its verdict from them. The psychologist Daniel Kahneman described this as a contest between the “experiencing self,” which lives through each moment, and the “remembering self,” which tells the story afterwards. The remembering self is the one that keeps score, and it is a famously poor accountant.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Businesses have not been slow to notice. Theme parks end the day with fireworks, restaurants send a free dessert with the bill, and some airlines save their friendliest service for the final minutes of a flight. None of this is necessarily sinister; a good ending is a pleasure in its own right. But a rule that can make a longer ordeal seem better than a shorter one deserves some caution. If our choices are guided by memories that ignore duration, we may knowingly sign up for more discomfort, simply because it is packaged with a pleasant final note.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านจิตวิทยาที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Every January, something unusual happens in parks, rice fields, and city gardens across the region. {{61}} with binoculars, notebooks, and a free counting app, thousands of ordinary people spend a weekend recording every bird they see. Since the count began fifteen years ago, the number of volunteers taking part {{62}} from a few hundred to more than twenty thousand. Scientists once dismissed such data as too unreliable to be useful. {{63}}, careful checks have shown that, when enough people take part, their records match those of trained researchers remarkably well. Every unusual sighting is reviewed by experts, {{64}} the project could not guarantee the quality of its records. The result is an {{65}} source of information on how bird populations are changing, gathered at a scale that no university could afford on its own.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">In many rural areas, borrowing money has long meant turning to private moneylenders. If banks had opened branches in these villages decades ago, many families {{66}} on such lenders today, since moneylenders often charge {{67}} interest rates of 10 percent a month or more. Community savings groups offer an alternative. Twenty or so neighbors meet every week, and each adds a small, fixed amount to a shared fund, which is kept in a locked box with three keys held by three different members. Rules are strict, yet few members object to {{68}} fined for arriving late or missing a meeting. Not only {{69}} borrow from the fund at a fair rate, but they also share in the interest it earns at the end of each cycle. Researchers are still debating {{70}} such groups lift families out of poverty, but they agree that the groups help members cope with sudden costs such as hospital bills.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Rising seas are not the only reason coastal cities are losing ground to the water. In many places, the land itself is sinking. When groundwater is pumped from beneath a city faster than rain can {{71}} it, the layers of clay that once held the water are squeezed and settle lower. Judging by the marks on its concrete pillars, an old fishing pier that now floods at every high tide {{72}} well above the water forty years ago. Engineers have found that {{73}} the water is pumped, the faster the ground above it sinks. Worse still, much of the damage is {{74}}: once clay has been compressed, it cannot simply expand again when the water returns. For this reason, experts insist that pumping {{75}} strictly limited and that cities switch to piped water from rivers and reservoirs.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>As the refrigerant flows through the thin pipes inside the cabinet, it evaporates, and in doing so it absorbs heat from the food and the air around it.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>A compressor then squeezes this warm gas, and in the coils at the back of the appliance, it releases its heat into the kitchen and turns back into a liquid, ready to begin the cycle again.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Contrary to what many people assume, a refrigerator does not produce cold; instead, it moves heat from inside the cabinet to the room outside.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>To do this, it pumps a special fluid known as a refrigerant around a sealed loop of pipes, which carries heat away each time it circulates.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Soon after hatching, the cuckoo chick pushes the other eggs or chicks over the edge of the nest, leaving itself as the only mouth for the adults to feed.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Remarkably, the host parents go on feeding this enormous stranger for weeks, even after it has grown several times larger than they are.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Instead, the female waits until a smaller bird, such as a reed warbler, leaves its nest unguarded, then lays a single egg there in a matter of seconds, often removing one of the host’s eggs.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Unlike most birds, the common cuckoo never builds a nest of its own or raises its own young.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>For green tea, by contrast, the leaves are heated by steaming or pan firing soon after they are picked, a step that halts oxidation and keeps them green.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Although green tea and black tea taste very different, both are made from the leaves of the same plant, Camellia sinensis.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>For black tea, the leaves are rolled or crushed and then left to oxidize fully, which gives the drink its dark color and strong flavor.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>The difference lies mainly in a process called oxidation, in which chemicals in the leaves react with oxygen in the air and the leaves gradually darken.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Supporters point out that such taxes have often persuaded manufacturers to cut the sugar in their recipes, simply to avoid paying more.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Critics, however, argue that, whatever their effect on recipes, the taxes fall most heavily on poorer households, which spend a larger share of their income on food and drink.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Whether the benefits outweigh this unfairness may depend largely on how governments spend the money raised, for example on healthier school meals.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>In an effort to tackle rising rates of obesity, particularly among children, a number of countries have introduced special taxes on sugary soft drinks.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Farmers have long avoided this problem by rotating their crops, and legumes such as beans and peas play a special part in the cycle.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Growing the same crop in the same field year after year gradually drains the soil of the nutrients that the crop needs most.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>When the legumes are harvested, or when their remains are ploughed back into the field, much of this nitrogen stays in the soil for the cereal crop planted next.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Bacteria living in small swellings on the roots of these plants can take nitrogen from the air and convert it into a form that plants are able to use.</span></span>"]},"EN70H_I":"","EN70H_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: At a pharmacy the day before a ferry trip","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Hi there. What can I do for you?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> I’m taking the ferry to Koh Mai tomorrow, and I get seasick easily. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Sure. These tablets work well, but they can make you drowsy, so take one an hour before you board.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Drowsy? {{2}} I’m supposed to lead a snorkeling group when we arrive.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> In that case, try these ginger capsules. They’re milder, and they won’t knock you out.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Ginger? {{3}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Honestly, it varies from person to person. Some customers swear by them. You could also try these wristbands. They press on a point on your wrist.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{4}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Sensible. That’s 260 baht altogether. Oh, and sit in the middle of the boat.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two members of a university badminton club after practice","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Have you heard from Tee? That’s the third practice he’s skipped this month, and the tournament is in two weeks.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bee:</b> He texted me an hour ago. Apparently something urgent came up again. {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Exactly. Last time, “something urgent” turned out to be a concert. I think we need a new doubles partner.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bee:</b> Hold on. {{6}} Coach Mint paired us up because our styles fit so well.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Fair enough, but I can’t play doubles on my own. {{7}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bee:</b> Maybe. But before we go to the coach, why don’t we talk to Tee? Something might be going on.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> {{8}} If he’s got a real problem, I’d feel terrible for complaining about him.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bee:</b> Great. I’ll ask him to grab a coffee with us tomorrow.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: A daughter talks to her father at the family noodle shop after closing time","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Fon:</b> Dad, have you got a minute? {{9}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Father:</b> Not that delivery app again. They take thirty percent of every bowl. We’d be working for them, not for ourselves.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fon:</b> I know, I know. {{10}} We’d raise the prices on the app only, not the prices in the shop.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Father:</b> Hmm. And who’s going to cook all the extra orders? My back isn’t what it used to be.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fon:</b> {{11}} I can take the evening shift on weekdays, and my friend Pim is looking for part time work.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Father:</b> {{12}} Three months. If we’re not making more money by then, we pull the plug.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fon:</b> Deal. You won’t regret it, I promise.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A student phones the manager of her dormitory building","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Lakeside Residence, Prasert speaking.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Hi, this is Nina from Room 504. {{13}} There’s water dripping from my ceiling, right above my desk.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Oh dear. Since when?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Since last night’s storm. I put a bucket under it, but my laptop bag got soaked before I noticed.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> I see. Well, the maintenance team doesn’t work on weekends, {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Monday? It’s Friday afternoon! I can’t keep emptying a bucket for three days.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{15}} There’s a man who does emergency repairs, but he charges extra, and I’d have to check who pays for that.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{16}} The leak isn’t my fault. It’s coming from the roof, or maybe from the room upstairs.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Fair point. Then the building should cover it. I’ll call him now. And your bag? {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Not exactly. It’s leather, so it’s probably ruined. The laptop inside is fine, luckily.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> I’m sorry to hear that. If you send me a photo and the receipt, {{18}} I can’t promise anything, though.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{19}} Anything is better than nothing.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Good. The repairman should be there within the hour. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> Will do. Thanks for sorting this out so quickly.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">SAY IT WITHOUT A SOUND</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Hands Talk: Thai Sign Language for Beginners</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Six Saturdays from 9 January</b>, 1 p.m. to 3 p.m.<br>Sai Thong Community Center, Room 3</span><span class=\"xv-p\">Taught by Deaf instructors with the support of certified interpreters, this course is for anyone with no previous experience who wants to communicate with Deaf customers, classmates, patients or relatives.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">WHAT YOU WILL LEARN</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Weeks 1 and 2: fingerspelling, names and greetings</span><span><b>2</b>Weeks 3 and 4: numbers, time and everyday questions</span><span><b>3</b>Weeks 5 and 6: short conversations and Deaf culture</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>1,800 baht</b> for the full course, materials included<br>Students with a valid ID: <b>1,200 baht</b><br>Groups of four or more from the same workplace: 15% off the full price</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Places are limited to 16. A certificate is given to participants who attend at least five of the six sessions. No refunds after the second session.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">NOTICE TO ALL CUSTOMERS</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Planned Water Supply Interruption</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Chalerm Nakhon Waterworks will replace a 40 year old main pipe under Phet Sai Road. Water will be shut off in three zones as shown below.</span><span class=\"en7-tb\"><span><b>Zone</b><b>Areas</b><b>No water</b></span><span><b>A</b><i>Sai Ngam, Ban Thung</i><i>Tue 12 Jan, 10 p.m. to Wed 13 Jan, 6 a.m.</i></span><span><b>B</b><i>Nong Bua, Tha Kham</i><i>Wed 13 Jan, 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.</i></span><span><b>C</b><i>Hill Park, Rim Klong</i><i>Wed 13 Jan, 10 p.m. to Thu 14 Jan, 10 a.m.</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p\">When the supply returns, the water may look cloudy or brownish. Run your cold tap for about three minutes until it is clear before using your washing machine or water heater.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Hospitals and clinics in these zones will be supplied by tanker trucks. Residents who use home medical equipment that needs water must call 1659 by 8 January to request a priority delivery.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: The Silver Lantern Overnight Sleeper","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Booking a twelve hour train journey when a one hour flight costs about the same takes optimism. I chose the Silver Lantern, the new overnight sleeper to Doi Kham, out of guilt about my carbon footprint. I came away largely converted, though not without reservations.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) First, the good news. The second class cabins are spotless, and the bedding is a cut above anything I have slept on in older trains. Each berth has a reading light, a charging socket and a thick curtain that offers a surprising sense of privacy. The staff are attentive without hovering.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The dining car is where the Silver Lantern tries hardest and, oddly, falls flattest. The menu promises “regional dishes cooked on board”, but my green curry arrived lukewarm and tasted as if it had been reheated in a pouch. At 320 baht, it was the most expensive disappointment of the trip. Several seasoned travelers around me had brought their own food, and I soon understood why.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Sleep is the real test, and the results are mixed. The train is far smoother than its predecessors on the flat southern half of the line, but once it climbs into the hills after midnight, the carriages rock and screech on the bends. I was woken three times, once by a jolt that sent my water bottle flying. Light sleepers should pack earplugs; the eye masks provided will not help.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Punctuality, however, was a pleasant surprise. We pulled into Doi Kham at 7:05 a.m., ten minutes early, and I walked straight to the morning market. Once you add the trips to airports and the queues at security, the flight saves far less time than its one hour suggests, and the train saves you a night in a hotel.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Is it worth it? For travelers who treat the journey as part of the holiday, absolutely. Those who must arrive fresh for a morning meeting may want to think twice.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ รถไฟ สถานี และเมืองในรีวิวเป็นสิ่งสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Doctors in Ban Phrao prescribe choirs and gardening for lonely patients</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Patients at Ban Phrao District Hospital who visit their doctor with no clear medical problem may soon leave with an unusual prescription: a weekly singing class, a plot in a community garden or a seat at a chess club. The hospital announced on Tuesday that its “social prescribing” scheme, piloted last year with patients over 60, will be extended to all adult patients from March.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Under the scheme, doctors who suspect that loneliness, rather than illness, lies behind a patient’s repeated visits can refer them to a “link worker”. These trained staff members meet the patient two or three times to find out what they enjoy and then connect them with one of 41 local groups, most of which are run by volunteers.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Hospital figures suggest the pilot has eased pressure on the crowded outpatient department. Among the 312 patients who took part, the average number of doctor visits fell from 9.4 in the year before joining to 6.1 in the year after. “A pill cannot replace a conversation,” said Dr. Kanya Srisuk, who led the pilot.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Participants were largely enthusiastic. Somsak Phanit, 72, a retired bus driver, said he had “stopped talking to anyone except the television” before his doctor referred him to a woodworking group.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Not everyone is convinced. Dr. Arun Thepkasem, a public health researcher, cautioned that the figures did not prove the scheme worked. “Patients who agree to join a gardening club may already be healthier and more motivated than those who refuse,” he said. “Without a comparison group, we cannot tell how much of the drop is due to the program itself.” He said he supported the idea in principle but wanted a proper trial before other hospitals copied it.</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Volunteer organizers have raised a more practical concern. Several groups say they are already full and that the expansion could bring dozens of new members without any extra funding. The hospital said it would set aside 1.2 million baht for the groups in the first year, though it has not decided how the money will be shared.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ อำเภอ โรงพยาบาล และบุคคลในข่าวเป็นสิ่งสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">How much electricity does one household use?</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Power rating of seven appliances in a family home and the average number of hours each one is used per day</span><span class=\"en7-tb\"><span><b>Appliance</b><b>Power (watts)</b><b>Hours used per day</b></span><span><b>Air conditioner</b><i>1,200</i><i>8</i></span><span><b>Refrigerator</b><i>150</i><i>24</i></span><span><b>Water heater</b><i>4,000</i><i>0.5</i></span><span><b>Rice cooker</b><i>800</i><i>1</i></span><span><b>Television</b><i>120</i><i>5</i></span><span><b>Electric fan</b><i>60</i><i>10</i></span><span><b>Laptop</b><i>50</i><i>8</i></span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Units per month = watts x hours per day x 30 days, divided by 1,000. Electricity costs 4.5 baht per unit.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Can students still afford to live near campus?</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Average monthly rent for a studio room near Riverside University and average monthly income of students with part time jobs, 2019 to 2025 (baht)</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Studio rent and student part time income, 2019 to 2025\"><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"148.0\" y2=\"148.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"151.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">2,000</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"110.0\" y2=\"110.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"113.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">4,000</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"72.0\" y2=\"72.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"75.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">6,000</text><line x1=\"40\" x2=\"256\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"35\" y=\"37.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">8,000</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2019</text><text x=\"76\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2023</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2024</text><text x=\"256\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2025</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"40,72.0 76,83.4 112,94.8 148,72.0 184,49.2 220,43.5 256,53.0\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"40\" cy=\"72.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"76\" cy=\"83.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"112\" cy=\"94.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"72.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"184\" cy=\"49.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"220\" cy=\"43.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"256\" cy=\"53.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"51.0\" class=\"lb\">Income</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"40,100.5 76,100.5 112,106.2 148,94.8 184,83.4 220,72.0 256,66.3\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"40\" cy=\"100.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"76\" cy=\"100.5\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"112\" cy=\"106.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"94.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"184\" cy=\"83.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"220\" cy=\"72.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"256\" cy=\"66.3\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"74.3\" class=\"lb\">Rent</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Year</b><b>2019</b><b>2020</b><b>2021</b><b>2022</b><b>2023</b><b>2024</b><b>2025</b></span><span><b>Rent</b><i>4,500</i><i>4,500</i><i>4,200</i><i>4,800</i><i>5,400</i><i>6,000</i><i>6,300</i></span><span><b>Income</b><i>6,000</i><i>5,400</i><i>4,800</i><i>6,000</i><i>7,200</i><i>7,500</i><i>7,000</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>When Targets Bite Back","<span class=\"xp\">(1) There is a story, repeated in countless economics lectures, about a colonial government in Delhi that was alarmed by the number of venomous cobras in the city. To solve the problem, officials offered a reward for every dead snake. At first the scheme seemed to work, and thousands of cobras were handed in. Before long, however, enterprising residents began breeding cobras so that they could kill them and collect the money. When the government discovered this and cancelled the reward, the breeders released their now worthless snakes, and the city ended up with more cobras than before.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Historians have found little evidence that events unfolded quite like this, and the tale may owe more to imagination than to archives. Yet it has survived because it captures something real. Economists now use the term “cobra effect” for any attempt to fix a problem that, by rewarding the wrong behavior, makes the problem worse. The underlying mistake is not stupidity but a failure to imagine how people will respond once their incentives change.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) A related idea is often summarized as Goodhart’s law: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure. The insight is subtle. A number such as a test score or a hospital waiting time is useful precisely because it tends to move together with something we care about but cannot observe directly, such as learning or the quality of care. The moment people are rewarded or punished according to that number, however, they acquire a reason to change the number without changing the reality behind it.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Examples are not hard to find. A call center that judges its staff by the length of their calls may discover that customers with complicated problems are being transferred or cut off rather than helped. A school that is ranked by its pass rate may quietly discourage weaker students from sitting the exam at all. In each case the figure improves, and in each case the organization becomes worse at the very thing the figure was meant to track.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) None of this means that measurement is pointless. Without numbers, managers would be left with impressions and anecdotes, which are even easier to manipulate. The lesson is rather that a single indicator should rarely bear the full weight of rewards. Organizations that track several measures at once, change them from time to time and combine them with human judgment leave fewer gaps for clever gaming. Some also invite the people being measured to help design the measures, on the grounds that those closest to the work are best placed to spot how a target might be abused.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Ultimately, the cobra effect is a reminder that people are not passive objects of policy. They read the rules, look for loopholes and adapt, often far more quickly than the rule makers expect. Any scheme designed to change behavior is therefore a kind of conversation, and whoever designs it would do well to listen to the reply.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ เรื่องเล่าเกี่ยวกับงูเห่าเป็นเรื่องเล่าที่แพร่หลาย ซึ่งบทความเองก็ระบุว่าไม่มีหลักฐานยืนยันชัดเจน</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>Dinner at Thirty Thousand Feet","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Airline food has long been a reliable target for comedians, and passengers usually assume that the problem lies in the kitchen. In fact, a meal that tastes perfectly good on the ground can taste noticeably blander once it reaches cruising altitude. The fault lies less with the cooks than with the cabin itself, which turns out to be one of the most hostile environments for taste that humans have ever created.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The first culprit is the air. Cabins are usually pressurized to the level found on a mountain 1,800 to 2,400 meters high, and the air is extremely dry, often drier than many deserts. Both conditions numb the nose. Because much of what we call flavor is actually smell, detected as aromas travel from the mouth up the back of the throat, a weakened sense of smell leaves food tasting flat. Some estimates suggest that our sensitivity to salty and sweet tastes drops by as much as 30 percent in the air.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The second culprit is noise. The steady roar of the engines appears to interfere with taste in a curiously selective way. In one laboratory study, volunteers rated sweet foods as less sweet when they heard loud aircraft noise through headphones than when they ate in silence, yet with the noise on they found savory, umami flavors, the rich taste of foods such as tomatoes and soy sauce, unchanged or even stronger. Researchers suspect this may help explain why so many passengers order tomato juice in the air, a drink few of them would choose on the ground.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Airlines have responded with a mixture of science and showmanship. Dishes designed for the air are typically seasoned more heavily than their ground equivalents, and chefs lean on ingredients whose flavor survives the cabin, such as ginger, lemongrass, chili and curry spices. Some carriers even test new dishes in pressurized chambers that copy cabin conditions, a step that would have struck earlier generations of airline caterers as absurd.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) There are limits, however, to what any kitchen can achieve. Most meals are cooked on the ground hours before departure, chilled and then reheated in the aircraft’s small ovens, a process that dries out rice and toughens meat regardless of how skillfully the dish was first prepared. Salt raises another problem: adding more of it to compensate for numbed senses sits uneasily with the promise of many airlines to offer healthier meals.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Passengers are not entirely helpless. Drinking plenty of water counters some of the dryness, and noise canceling headphones may, if the laboratory findings hold, restore a little sweetness to dessert. Perhaps the most useful adjustment, though, is one of expectation. A disappointing meal at altitude says less about the chef than about the remarkable sensitivity of our senses to the conditions in which we eat.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ตัวเลขและงานวิจัยที่กล่าวถึงเป็นค่าประมาณที่นำเสนออย่างระมัดระวัง</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Tardigrades, tiny animals that are rarely longer than one millimeter, are often called water bears because of their plump bodies and slow, clumsy walk. Since they were first described in the eighteenth century, they {{61}} scientists with their extraordinary toughness. When their surroundings dry out, tardigrades curl up into a dry ball and enter a state {{62}} their metabolism almost completely stops. In this condition, they can {{63}} temperatures close to absolute zero, crushing pressures and doses of radiation that would kill most other animals. In 2007, a group of tardigrades was carried into orbit and exposed to the vacuum of space. Not only {{64}} the journey, but some of the females later laid eggs that hatched normally. {{65}}, tardigrades are far from indestructible. In their active state they are quite fragile, and they could not survive for long if their habitats on Earth disappeared.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">Order a curry from a delivery app tonight, and there is a growing chance that it will come from a restaurant you could never actually visit. The number of so called ghost kitchens, which cook food only for delivery, {{66}} grown rapidly in recent years. {{67}} no dining room, no waiters and no busy street location, such kitchens can operate from cheap industrial buildings, paying a rent that is a {{68}} of what a traditional restaurant pays. Some run five or six different brands from the same kitchen, each with its own name and menu on the app. Critics point out that customers often have no idea {{69}}, since the brand on their screen may look like a small family business. {{70}}, hygiene inspections designed for ordinary restaurants may fail to keep up with kitchens that change their names every few months.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Most drivers believe they would notice if they were falling asleep at the wheel. If you have ever arrived home with no memory of the last few kilometers, however, you {{71}} a microsleep without realizing it. These brief episodes, in which the brain switches off for a few seconds even while the eyes stay open, are particularly {{72}} because the driver rarely feels them coming. Imagine a driver who nods off for just five seconds. {{73}} she been traveling at 100 kilometers per hour, her car would have covered almost 140 meters with no one in control. Research has suggested that driving after 24 hours without sleep impairs performance about as much as {{74}} with a blood alcohol level of 0.1 percent. Opening a window or turning up the music offers little protection. Drivers who feel drowsy should stop {{75}} a twenty minute nap, or better still, let someone else take the wheel.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>As the trapped steam builds up, it raises the pressure inside the pot, and at this higher pressure the water does not boil until it reaches about 120 degrees.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>At sea level, water boils at 100 degrees Celsius, and no matter how high the flame is turned up, an open pot of boiling water will not get any hotter than that.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Because the food is surrounded by water and steam some 20 degrees hotter than usual, tough cuts of meat and dried beans become tender in about a third of the normal time.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>A pressure cooker gets around this limit by sealing the pot with a tight lid, so that the steam produced by the boiling water cannot escape.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>It took him years of experiments to copy this design in fabric, but in 1955 he finally patented a fastener made of two strips, one covered in hooks and the other in loops.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>He named his invention Velcro, combining the French words velours and crochet, meaning velvet and hook, and it is now used on everything from children’s shoes to spacesuits.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In 1941, a Swiss engineer named George de Mestral returned from a walk in the Alps and noticed that his trousers and his dog’s fur were covered in burrs, the prickly seed cases of a common plant.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Curious about why they clung so stubbornly, he examined one under a microscope and saw hundreds of tiny hooks that caught on anything with loops, such as fabric or hair.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The chemical is produced only when a knife breaks the onion’s cells, releasing enzymes that were stored separately and allowing them to mix with sulfur compounds.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>The eyes respond by producing tears to wash the irritant away, which is why chilling the onion first to slow the reactions, or using a very sharp knife that crushes fewer cells, can help.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Surprisingly, a whole onion sitting on a kitchen counter contains none of the chemical that makes us cry.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>One product of the resulting reactions is a gas that drifts upward and, on reaching the eyes, dissolves in their moist surface and stings the nerves there.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>There is also evidence that students who type their notes tend to copy the lecturer’s words without thinking, whereas those who write by hand are forced to summarize and so understand more.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Critics of such bans reply, however, that they punish students who rely on laptops for good reasons, such as those with disabilities that make handwriting slow or painful.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Their main concern is distraction: a student with a laptop open is only ever one click away from social media, and the glowing screen also distracts classmates sitting nearby.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>A growing number of university lecturers have begun to ban laptops from their classrooms, arguing that the devices do students more harm than good.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>If the employer still cannot move on salary, it may be willing to offer something else instead, such as extra days of leave, a training budget or an earlier performance review.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>In reality, most employers expect some discussion and deliberately leave room in their first offer, so politely asking for more rarely costs a candidate the job.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Many graduates accept the first salary they are offered, fearing that any attempt to negotiate will make them look ungrateful or cause the employer to withdraw the offer.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>The key is to base the request on evidence rather than on personal need, for example by pointing to typical salaries for similar roles in the same city.</span></span>"]},"EN70I_I":"","EN70I_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: At an optician’s shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Optician:</b> Morning. How can I help?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Hi. I sat on my glasses on the bus this morning, and one of the arms snapped right off. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Optician:</b> I’m afraid not. The frame has cracked where the arm joins it, so glue wouldn’t hold.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> I was afraid of that. The thing is, my exams start on Monday. {{2}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Optician:</b> Normally, lenses for your prescription take about a week to arrive from our lab.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> A week? {{3}} I can barely read a page without them.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Optician:</b> Well, if you don’t mind a basic frame from this shelf, we keep common lenses in stock. They’d be ready tomorrow afternoon.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{4}} I just need something to get me through the exams.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Optician:</b> Perfect. Let me measure you, then.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two volunteers at an animal shelter’s adoption day","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Leo:</b> We open in twenty minutes, and there’s still no sign of Tom. He promised he’d run the registration table.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kanya:</b> Oh, I’m sure he’ll be here on time. {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Leo:</b> Very funny. He missed the entire first hour last month. Anyway, someone needs to sit at that table. {{6}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kanya:</b> I would, but I’m supposed to walk the older dogs around so visitors can meet them.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Leo:</b> Right, I forgot. Then I’ll have to do it myself, but I’ve never used the new booking system.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kanya:</b> {{7}} You just type in the visitor’s name and number, and it does the rest.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Leo:</b> OK. And what if somebody wants to take a dog home today?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kanya:</b> They can’t. {{8}} We always do a home visit first.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two university friends talk about the debate club","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> So, have you thought any more about joining the debate club? We’re still a speaker short for the tournament.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ava:</b> I have, and it’s really sweet of you to think of me. {{9}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> Busy? Last week you told me your timetable was finally under control.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ava:</b> It is, sort of. But honestly, arguing in front of a room full of strangers isn’t my cup of tea.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> {{10}} Most of us were terrified at first. My hands shook all the way through my first round.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ava:</b> Maybe, but the tournament is only three weeks away. {{11}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> Fair enough. You wouldn’t have to speak, though. You could help us research the arguments.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ava:</b> {{12}} Send me the motion and I’ll see what I can dig up.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jin:</b> Deal. Thanks, Ava.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A student meets her academic advisor","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Come in, Pim, and have a seat. {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Thanks, Dr. Harlow. It’s about Statistics 201. I’m thinking of dropping it.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> I see. The deadline to withdraw without a grade is this Friday, so you’ve come at the right time. {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Mostly the midterm. I got 38 out of 100, and I’d studied really hard for it.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> That must have been discouraging. But one bad test doesn’t mean you can’t pass. The midterm is only worth thirty percent.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{15}} Even if I do well from now on, I’ll probably end up with a C at best.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> And would a C be such a disaster?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> For me, yes. I’m applying for the exchange program, and they want a GPA of at least 3.25. {{16}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> I wouldn’t be so sure. If you drop it now, you’ll have to take it again next year anyway, because it’s required for your major, and next year it clashes with the exchange semester.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Oh. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Most students don’t. It’s buried on page forty of the handbook. Look, the department runs free tutoring sessions on Tuesday evenings. {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I didn’t even know they existed. But what if I go and still don’t improve?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Then come back and see me before Friday, and we’ll look at your options again. {{19}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> That’s true. I suppose one session can’t hurt. Thanks so much, Dr. Harlow. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Not at all. That’s what I’m here for. Good luck on Tuesday.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">NOTICE TO ALL USERS</span><b class=\"xv-t\">PedalPoint Campus Bike Share: New Rules from 1 January</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Last semester, 340 bikes were found outside docking stations and nearly one in five bikes was out of service at any given time. To keep bikes available for everyone, the following changes will apply.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">RIDE FEES (STUDENTS WITH A VALID ID)</b><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Up to 30 minutes</b>Free</span><span><b>31 to 60 minutes</b>10 baht</span><span><b>After the first hour</b>20 baht for every extra 30 minutes or part of it</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\">Staff and visitors pay a flat 20 baht per ride of up to 60 minutes, by card through the PedalPoint app only.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">ALSO NEW</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Each account may now have only one bike out at a time.</span><span><b>2</b>Bikes left anywhere other than a docking station will be charged a 200 baht collection fee.</span><span><b>3</b>Bikes cannot be unlocked between 11 p.m. and 5 a.m.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Found a fault? Report it in the app within two minutes of unlocking, and neither the ride nor the damage will be charged to you.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">GROW YOUR OWN, TEN FLOORS UP</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Skyline Residences Rooftop Garden: Plots Now Open for Rent</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Twenty four raised plots are available on the Building A rooftop for the season beginning 1 February.</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Small plot</b>1 m × 2 m, 300 baht per month</span><span><b>Large plot</b>2 m × 2 m, 500 baht per month</span><span><b>Included</b>Water, shared tools and a compost bin</span><span><b>Minimum rental</b>Six months, paid in advance</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\">A refundable deposit of 1,000 baht is required for every plot.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">GARDEN RULES</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Open to residents only, with one plot per unit.</span><span><b>2</b>No chemical pesticides, and no plants taller than 1.5 m, because of strong winds.</span><span><b>3</b>Plots left untended for three weeks in a row will be forfeited and offered to the next person on the waiting list.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Apply at the management office by 15 January. If there are more applicants than plots, a lottery will be held on 20 January. Residents who took part in last year’s trial season will be given priority for large plots.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>App Review: StrollTales Audio Walking Tours","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Audio tour apps promise to turn your phone into a private guide, and StrollTales, which covers the old riverside town of Baan Tha Lom, is the most ambitious one I have tried. I spent two afternoons on its main route, “Merchants and Monsoons,” and came away charmed, but also with sore feet and a nearly dead battery.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The writing is where the app shines. Rather than reciting dates, the narration follows characters, from a rice trader to a ferry pilot’s daughter, whose stories unfold as you walk from one stop to the next. Local actors voice them, and the effect is closer to a radio drama than a lecture. At the old customs house, I stood perfectly still until the episode had finished.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The technology is less polished. Each episode is supposed to start automatically when GPS detects that you have reached a stop, but in the narrow lanes near the market the app kept losing my position, either staying silent or, more irritatingly, playing two episodes on top of each other. Downloading the route in advance cures the silence but not the overlap, and three hours of GPS and audio left my phone at eleven percent.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Then there is the route itself. The app claims it takes “about ninety minutes,” a figure that seems to assume nobody ever stops, takes photographs or gets lost. Allow twice that. Two of the twelve stops are reached by steep stone steps, a fact the app mentions only once you are standing at the bottom of them.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) At 249 baht per route, StrollTales costs about a third of a tour with a human guide, and you can pause for coffee whenever you like. What you cannot do is ask a question. For independent travelers with a power bank and an afternoon to spare, I would recommend it without hesitation. Anyone with limited mobility, or anyone who wants a conversation, should book a person instead.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ แอปและสถานที่ในรีวิวเป็นของสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Night buses roll out for city’s shift workers</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Pak Nam Thong will run buses through the night for the first time, after a survey found that almost half of the city’s hospital, factory and hotel workers start or finish their shifts when no public transport is available. The six month trial, which begins on 1 March, will put 14 buses on three routes between midnight and 5 a.m., linking the central hospital, the eastern industrial estate and the riverside hotel district.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Fares will match daytime fares at 15 baht a ride, but passengers who show a work ID from a participating employer will travel free. Eleven employers, including the hospital, have agreed to share the cost.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) “When my shift ends at one in the morning, my choices are a motorbike taxi that costs a third of my daily wage or a forty minute walk along a road with no streetlights,” said Sunee Wongkham, a cleaner at the hospital. “Most nights I walk.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Officials say the routes were planned around the timing of shift changes rather than around population. “A daytime network is built around where people live. A night network has to be built around when people work,” said Kittipong Arun, the city’s transport chief. Buses will therefore run every 20 minutes around the main changeovers, at midnight and 4 a.m., but only once an hour in between.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Not everyone is convinced that the trial is worth its estimated cost of 9 million baht. City councillor Darin Phromma pointed out that a similar scheme in a neighboring province was abandoned after a year because its buses often ran almost empty. “Nobody objects to helping shift workers,” he said. “What I object to is paying for buses to carry air at three in the morning.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Officials argue that the earlier scheme failed because it copied daytime routes. Passenger numbers will be reviewed every month, and any route averaging fewer than 150 passengers a night by the end of the third month will be cut or redesigned.</span><span class=\"xp\">(8) The trial will also test whether safer journeys home help employers keep their staff, a problem at the hospital, which lost nearly a fifth of its night nurses last year.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ บุคคลและสถานที่ในข่าวเป็นของสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Volunteers at Greenway Community Food Bank</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Number of regular volunteers by age group in 2023 and 2025, and the average number of hours each volunteer gave per month in 2025</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Age group</b><b>Volunteers 2023</b><b>Volunteers 2025</b><b>Hours per volunteer per month (2025)</b></span><span><b>15 to 19</b><i>40</i><i>96</i><i>4</i></span><span><b>20 to 29</b><i>120</i><i>150</i><i>6</i></span><span><b>30 to 49</b><i>85</i><i>80</i><i>5</i></span><span><b>50 to 64</b><i>60</i><i>72</i><i>9</i></span><span><b>65 and over</b><i>25</i><i>42</i><i>16</i></span><span class=\"en7-tot\"><b>Total</b><i>330</i><i>440</i><i></i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Ticket sales at two music festivals, 2019 to 2025</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Tickets sold per year (thousands). Neither festival was held in 2020.</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Ticket sales of two music festivals, 2019 to 2025\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"142.0\" y2=\"142.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"145.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"98.0\" y2=\"98.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"101.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"54.0\" y2=\"54.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"57.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"10.0\" y2=\"10.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"13.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2019</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2020</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2022</text><text x=\"186\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2023</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2024</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2025</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,80.4 72,186.0 110,159.6 148,98.0 186,65.0 224,54.0 262,67.2\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"80.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"186.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"159.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"98.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"65.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"54.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"67.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"77.2\" class=\"lb\">Riverbeat</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,142.0 72,186.0 110,146.4 148,111.2 186,93.6 224,58.4 262,40.8\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"142.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"186.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"146.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"111.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"93.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"58.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"40.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"36.8\" class=\"lb\">Hillsound</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Year</b><b>2019</b><b>2020</b><b>2021</b><b>2022</b><b>2023</b><b>2024</b><b>2025</b></span><span><b>Riverbeat</b><i>48</i><i>0</i><i>12</i><i>40</i><i>55</i><i>60</i><i>54</i></span><span><b>Hillsound</b><i>20</i><i>0</i><i>18</i><i>34</i><i>42</i><i>58</i><i>66</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ประกาศ ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>Why We Love What We Build","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In the 1950s, American food companies faced a puzzling problem. Instant cake mixes, which required nothing more than water, should have been a triumph of convenience, yet many home bakers seemed reluctant to buy them. According to a story that marketers still love to tell, sales improved only when the recipe was changed so that the baker had to add a fresh egg. The extra step, the argument goes, allowed people to feel that the cake was genuinely theirs. Food historians have since questioned how much the egg really mattered, but the story has survived because it captures something that psychologists have now documented in the laboratory.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) In a series of studies published in 2012, the researchers Michael Norton, Daniel Mochon and Dan Ariely asked participants to assemble simple storage boxes from a well known Swedish furniture company. Compared with people who were handed identical boxes already assembled, the builders were willing to pay considerably more for the products of their own labor. In a related experiment, participants who folded paper animals valued their creations almost as highly as those made by expert folders, while observers who had not been involved regarded the amateur versions as little more than crumpled paper. The researchers named the phenomenon the IKEA effect.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Crucially, the effect depended on success. When participants built something and then had to take it apart, or when the task was so difficult that they failed to finish it, the extra value vanished. Labor, in other words, does not create love on its own; it does so only when it ends in completion. This finding suggests that what people value is not effort as such but the sense of competence that comes from turning effort into a visible result.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The implications extend well beyond furniture. Companies that let customers design their own sneakers or choose every ingredient of a meal are, knowingly or not, exploiting the same tendency. Managers may fall prey to it as well: an executive who has spent months developing a project may judge it far more favorably than an outside reviewer would, and may be slow to abandon it even when the evidence turns against it. The pride of authorship that keeps a wobbly bookshelf in the living room for twenty years can keep a failing plan alive for far too long.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) None of this means that the effect is simply a flaw. A willingness to invest effort in things we expect to cherish may help explain why people bother to cook, garden or mend clothes at all, activities that are rarely efficient but often deeply satisfying. A world without the IKEA effect might be more rational, but it would almost certainly contain fewer hobbies.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) The practical lesson, then, is not to stop making things ourselves but to remember that our own work looks better to us than it does to anyone else. Before deciding how good something is, it may be worth asking a second question: would I still think so if someone else had made it?</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านจิตวิทยาผู้บริโภคที่เป็นที่รู้จัก</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Jam Study and the Limits of a Good Story","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In 2000, the psychologists Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper published a study that seemed to overturn a basic assumption of modern consumer culture: that more choice is always better. Over two Saturdays, shoppers at an upmarket grocery store in California passed a tasting table whose display was switched back and forth: at some times it offered 24 varieties of jam; at others, only six. The larger display attracted more visitors, but when it came to buying, the result was striking. Shoppers who had seen six jams were roughly ten times as likely to make a purchase as those who had seen twenty four.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The finding spread quickly. It offered a neat explanation for a familiar feeling, the paralysis of standing before a wall of nearly identical products, and it was taken up enthusiastically by journalists, consultants and policymakers. The idea of “choice overload” was soon invoked to justify everything from shorter restaurant menus to simpler pension plans. In the popular imagination, the jam study had proved that too many options leave people miserable and unable to decide.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Yet the evidence turned out to be less tidy than the story. In 2010, a team of researchers gathered the results of fifty experiments on choice overload and combined them statistically. Some studies found that large assortments discouraged choosing; others found that they encouraged it; many found no difference at all. Averaged together, the effect was close to zero. For a while, it looked as though one of psychology’s most quoted findings might simply evaporate.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) A later analysis reached a more nuanced conclusion. Choice overload, it suggested, is real but conditional. It appears mainly when options are hard to compare, when people do not already know what they want, and when the decision itself feels difficult or important. A coffee drinker who always orders the same thing is untroubled by a long menu; a first time buyer of a laptop, faced with dozens of models described in unfamiliar technical terms, may well walk away. The number of options, in short, matters less than how easy those options are to evaluate.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) This shift has practical consequences. If the problem is not quantity but complexity, the solution is not necessarily to remove choices but to organize them. Grouping products into clear categories, highlighting a sensible default, or letting buyers filter by the features they care about can preserve variety while easing the burden of deciding. Many online retailers already do exactly this, presenting thousands of items in a way that feels manageable.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) The career of the jam study also carries a lesson about how science reaches the public. A single striking result, especially one that confirms what people already suspect, can travel much further than the careful work that later qualifies it. The original researchers never claimed that choice is always harmful; the exaggeration was largely the work of those who retold their findings. The real moral may be that a good story deserves the same scrutiny as a bad one.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านการตัดสินใจที่เป็นที่รู้จัก</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Kintsugi, which means “golden joinery” in Japanese, is a technique {{61}} broken pottery is repaired with lacquer mixed with powdered gold. {{62}} to the late fifteenth century, the practice is often linked to a story about a military ruler who sent a damaged tea bowl away for repair and was disappointed when it came back held together with ugly metal staples. Rather than hiding the cracks, kintsugi {{63}} them, turning each golden line into a record of the object’s history. Not only {{64}} usable again, but it also becomes, in the eyes of many collectors, more beautiful than it was before it broke. Today, therapists and writers use kintsugi as a metaphor for recovery, suggesting that the marks left by hardship need not be {{65}}.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">At first, the claim that hot water can freeze faster than cold water seems to {{66}} common sense. Yet the observation is surprisingly old, and today it is named after a Tanzanian schoolboy, Erasto Mpemba. In 1963, while making ice cream at school, Mpemba noticed that a hot mixture he had put in the freezer {{67}} before his classmates’ cooler ones. His teacher dismissed the result, but Mpemba persisted, and he later published his findings with a visiting physicist. If he had accepted his teacher’s verdict, the effect {{68}} his name today. Since then, many explanations have been {{69}}, including faster evaporation, dissolved gases and currents within the water. However, the effect is notoriously hard to reproduce, and some researchers who have repeated the experiment under strictly controlled conditions believe that earlier positive results {{70}} by small errors in measurement.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Many households own a drill that spends most of its life in a drawer. This simple observation lies behind the rise of “libraries of things,” where members borrow tools, camping gear and kitchen equipment instead of books. The number of such libraries {{71}} steadily over the past decade, particularly in cities where storage space is limited. Supporters say that members not only avoid {{72}} money on items they rarely need but also get to know their neighbors. {{73}}, a single shared lawnmower can do the work of twenty privately owned ones, which means fewer machines have to be manufactured in the first place. Critics point out that most of these libraries {{74}} heavily on volunteers and small donations, which makes them fragile. Indeed, many organizers say they wish more members {{75}} willing to help run the library, rather than simply borrowing from it.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>To make the balloon lighter, the pilot opens a burner that sends a roaring flame into the open base of the envelope, heating the air trapped inside it.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>When the pilot wants to come down, he or she simply stops firing the burner or opens a vent at the top, allowing the hot air to cool or escape until the balloon slowly sinks.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>A hot air balloon rises for much the same reason that a cork floats in water: as a whole, it is lighter than the fluid around it, which in this case is the surrounding air.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Because warm air expands, this heated air is less dense than the cooler air outside, so the balloon, together with its basket and passengers, is pushed upward.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Empathy, by contrast, involves imagining yourself in the other person’s position and sharing, at least in part, the feelings that he or she is going through.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>This difference matters in practice: someone offering sympathy might say, “I’m sorry you feel that way,” whereas someone offering empathy is more likely to say, “That sounds really hard.”</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Sympathy means feeling concern or pity for someone who is suffering, while remaining, in a sense, outside that person’s experience.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Although the words “empathy” and “sympathy” are often used as if they meant the same thing, psychologists draw a clear line between them.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Realizing that Silver’s glue would let a bookmark stay put yet come away cleanly, Fry helped turn the idea into the small yellow sticky notes now found in almost every office.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>The adhesive could hold paper in place and then be peeled off without leaving a mark, but for several years nobody, including Silver himself, could think of a use for it.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In 1968, Spencer Silver, a chemist at an American manufacturing company, was trying to develop an extremely strong glue when he accidentally produced one that was remarkably weak.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>The solution finally came from a colleague, Art Fry, who was frustrated that the paper bookmarks in his church songbook kept falling out whenever he opened it.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Studies of such workplaces suggest that the main problem is not loud noise in general but nearby speech, since the brain finds it almost impossible to ignore words that it can understand.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>For this reason, some companies have introduced “quiet zones” where phone calls and conversations are not allowed, giving staff somewhere to retreat to for focused work.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Open plan offices were promoted as a way to encourage conversation and teamwork, but many employees now complain that the constant background chatter makes it hard to concentrate.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Others take the opposite approach and add a soft, steady background sound, which makes distant voices harder to understand and therefore less distracting.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Critics also point out that fares pay for a significant share of running costs, so abolishing them can leave less money for maintenance and new routes.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Several cities around the world have experimented with making their buses and trains completely free, hoping to persuade drivers to leave their cars at home.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>For these reasons, some transport experts argue that cities would achieve more by making services faster and more reliable than by making them free.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>The results so far have been mixed: ridership usually rises sharply, but many of the new passengers turn out to be people who would otherwise have walked or cycled rather than driven.</span></span>"]},"EN70J_I":"","EN70J_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: At the front desk of an indoor climbing gym","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Hi there! First time with us?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> It is. My friends keep dragging me along, so I figured I’d finally give it a go. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Not at all. Most of our regulars started out with zero experience. Just fill in this safety form first.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> Sure. Oh, and I didn’t bring any climbing shoes. {{2}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> We rent them for 80 baht. They’re meant to feel tight, so don’t panic if your toes are a bit squashed.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> Squashed? {{3}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Ha, I get that a lot. Trust me, you’ll grip the wall far better that way.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> Fine, I’ll take your word for it. Anything else?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Just one thing. {{4}} Falling is part of the sport, so we’ll teach you to land safely.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> Good to know. Thanks!</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: Two coworkers at a cinema snack counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Hey, Jay, got a sec? I hate to ask, but {{5}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jay:</b> Depends. Which one are we talking about?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Saturday evening. It’s my sister’s graduation party, and I completely forgot about it when I put my name down.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jay:</b> Saturday evening? That’s the busiest night of the week. {{6}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> I know, I know. I’d take your Tuesday morning in return, and I’ll even throw in the Sunday cleanup.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jay:</b> Tempting. {{7}} I was going to catch the new horror film that night with a few friends.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Couldn’t you go on Tuesday instead? You’d practically have the whole theater to yourselves.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jay:</b> Hmm, you drive a hard bargain. {{8}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> You’re a lifesaver! I owe you big time.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two volunteers at a water station during a city marathon","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Right, the first runners should reach us in about twenty minutes. {{9}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Almost. I’ve filled around three hundred cups, but we’re running out of table space.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Forget the tables. Just line the cups up along the barrier. {{10}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Wait, should I fill every cup right to the top?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Definitely not. Half full is plenty, or they’ll splash everywhere.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Makes sense. And what if someone stops and asks me for help? {{11}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> Just wave over a medic in a red vest. Never try to treat anyone yourself.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Got it. Honestly, I didn’t expect handing out water to be this complicated.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{12}} By lunchtime you’ll be doing it like a pro.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> I’ll hold you to that.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A student sees a physiotherapist at a campus sports clinic","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> So, Nat, tell me what happened.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> I was playing basketball on Thursday and landed awkwardly after a jump. I heard a sort of pop, and {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> Did you carry on playing afterwards?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> For about ten minutes, yes. I didn’t want to let the team down. {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> Well, it certainly didn’t do you any favors. Let me have a look. Point your toes for me. Does that hurt?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Ouch! A bit. Well, more than a bit, actually.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> The swelling has gone down, which is good news. {{15}} It looks like a moderate sprain rather than a fracture.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Phew. So I can still play in the final on the twentieth, right?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> {{16}} That’s barely two weeks away. Ligaments usually need three to six weeks to heal properly.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Three weeks? But I’m the team captain. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> I understand it’s frustrating. But if you rush back now, you could end up with an ankle that keeps giving way for years. {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> I suppose not. So what can I do in the meantime?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> Plenty. I’ll give you some balance exercises, and you can swim or cycle to stay fit, as long as it doesn’t hurt. {{19}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Every single day? That sounds like a lot of homework.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> Ten minutes, twice a day. You probably spend longer than that scrolling on your phone.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Fair point. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Physio:</b> Great. Book a follow up for next Friday, and we’ll see how you’re getting on.</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">NEVER SURFED? PERFECT.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Wave Riders Beginner Surf Weekend</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Saturday 7 to Sunday 8 June</b><br>Laem Krut Beach, a quiet bay with gentle waves ideal for first timers</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Small groups</b>One coach for every four surfers</span><span><b>All gear included</b>Soft boards and rash guards for every size</span><span><b>Video review</b>Watch your rides with your coach on Sunday afternoon, screens provided</span><span><b>Beach lodge</b>Two nights in a shared room, breakfast included</span></span><b class=\"xv-t2\">BEFORE YOU BOOK</b><span class=\"xv-p\">You must be able to swim 50 meters without stopping; coaches will check on Saturday morning. Lessons start at 6:30 a.m., before the afternoon sea breeze picks up.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>Early bird: 4,200 baht</b> (book by 20 May)<br>Standard: 4,800 baht. Students save 10% on the standard price.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Discounts cannot be combined. Minimum age 16; participants under 18 must bring a consent form signed by a parent. If storms close the beach on both days, choose a free place on a future weekend or a 50% refund.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">PARTICIPANTS WANTED</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Coffee and Sleep Study</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Sleep Research Unit, Faculty of Psychology, Northbridge University</span><span class=\"xv-p\">Does an afternoon coffee really keep you awake? Help us find out.</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">WHO CAN TAKE PART</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Healthy adults aged 18 to 25 who normally drink 1 to 3 cups of coffee a day. Unfortunately, we cannot accept night shift workers, smokers, or anyone taking medication to help them sleep.</span><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Complete a 10 minute online screening form.</span><span><b>2</b>Spend three nights in our Sleep Lab, one week apart, from 9 p.m. to 7 a.m.</span><span><b>3</b>Each night, have a drink that may or may not contain caffeine. You will not be told which.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p\">On each study day, have no caffeine after 12 noon and no alcohol in the previous 24 hours.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>600 baht per night</b>, plus a 700 baht bonus for completing all three nights. Breakfast provided.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">You may leave the study at any time without giving a reason. You will be paid for every night you have completed.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: Pantry Pilot Weekly Meal Kits","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When I moved into my first apartment, I lived mainly on instant noodles and whatever the shop downstairs had on offer. So when a friend recommended Pantry Pilot, a service that delivers measured ingredients with recipe cards, I signed up for a month, half expecting to quit after a week. Four months later, I am still a subscriber, though not an uncritical one.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The service’s biggest selling point is that it takes the guesswork out of cooking. Every ingredient arrives in exactly the quantity you need, down to a single spoonful of fish sauce, so nothing goes off in the fridge. The recipes are clearly written, and most dishes are on the table in about thirty minutes. For someone whose cooking skills once stopped at boiling eggs, this has been a revelation.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) That precision, however, comes at an environmental cost. Each box is packed with plastic: individual sachets, tiny bottles, and ice packs that the company insists are recyclable but that my local collection point refuses to accept. Pantry Pilot has promised to switch to paper packaging “within two years,” which is little comfort to those of us drowning in plastic now.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Value is harder to judge. A plan of three dinners for two costs 1,650 baht a week, roughly double what I would pay for the same ingredients at a market. Then again, I no longer throw away half bunches of herbs or order takeaway when I run out of ideas, so the real gap is narrower than it looks. Skipping a week takes two taps in the app, a flexibility I have used more often than I expected.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Would I recommend it? For confident cooks who enjoy browsing markets, probably not; they will find the recipes unadventurous and the price hard to swallow. But for busy beginners who want to build a few kitchen skills without wasting food, Pantry Pilot is well worth a try, provided you can live with the packaging.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Young designers bring a fading silk village back to life</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) For decades, the looms beneath the wooden houses of Ban Nong Kham fell silent one by one. Young people left for factory jobs in the city, and by 2019 only eleven weavers, the youngest of them 58, were still producing the village’s distinctive indigo silk. Today, the clatter of the looms is back: the village cooperative now has 46 members, and orders are booked six months in advance.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The turnaround began when three design graduates from the provincial university, all of whom had grandparents in the village, proposed a partnership. Rather than asking the weavers to alter their traditional patterns, they built modern products around them, such as laptop sleeves, sneakers and office jackets, and sold them online under the label Khram.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) “At first, the older weavers were suspicious,” said Arunee Phaosri, 27, one of the founders. “They had seen outsiders come here before, buy cloth cheaply and sell it in Bangkok for ten times the price.” To win their trust, the designers agreed that the cooperative, not the company, would set the price of every meter of fabric, and that 15 percent of online profits would go to the village school.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) The arrangement appears to be working. A meter of hand woven indigo silk, which once fetched about 600 baht at local markets, now earns weavers 1,400 baht. Eight of the new members are under 30, and two of them gave up jobs in Bangkok to return home.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Not everyone is convinced the boom will last. Dr. Kittipong Saelim, an economist who studies rural crafts, pointed out that fashion is fickle. “Demand built on social media can vanish as quickly as it appeared,” he said. “The village needs buyers who value the craft itself, not just a pattern that happens to be popular this year.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) The founders say they are aware of the risks. They now run weekend workshops for visitors to create a second source of income, and they are training young weavers to keep detailed records of traditional patterns, several of which had existed only in the memories of the oldest weavers.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Part time jobs held by university students</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Survey of 1,500 working students. Each student was counted under their main job only.</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Main job</b><b>Share of students</b><b>Average hours per week</b><b>Average pay per hour (baht)</b></span><span><b>Café or restaurant staff</b><i>31%</i><i>16</i><i>50</i></span><span><b>Retail sales</b><i>22%</i><i>14</i><i>55</i></span><span><b>Private tutoring</b><i>18%</i><i>6</i><i>250</i></span><span><b>Delivery rider</b><i>12%</i><i>20</i><i>65</i></span><span><b>Online freelance work</b><i>10%</i><i>8</i><i>150</i></span><span><b>Event staff</b><i>7%</i><i>5</i><i>90</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Monthly rainfall in two towns, May to November</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Rainfall in millimeters (mm). Ban Khao is an inland town; Laem Sai is on the coast.</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Monthly rainfall in Ban Khao and Laem Sai, May to November\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"138.0\" y2=\"138.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"141.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"90.0\" y2=\"90.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"93.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">200</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"42.0\" y2=\"42.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"45.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">300</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jun</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jul</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Aug</text><text x=\"186\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,109.2 72,118.8 110,104.4 148,85.2 186,51.6 224,114.0 262,166.8\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"109.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"118.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"104.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"85.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"51.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"114.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"166.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"170.8\" class=\"lb\">Ban Khao</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,142.8 72,128.4 110,114.0 148,99.6 186,70.8 224,37.2 262,46.8\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"142.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"128.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"114.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"99.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"70.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"37.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"46.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"50.8\" class=\"lb\">Laem Sai</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Month</b><b>May</b><b>Jun</b><b>Jul</b><b>Aug</b><b>Sep</b><b>Oct</b><b>Nov</b></span><span><b>Ban Khao</b><i>160</i><i>140</i><i>170</i><i>210</i><i>280</i><i>150</i><i>40</i></span><span><b>Laem Sai</b><i>90</i><i>120</i><i>150</i><i>180</i><i>240</i><i>310</i><i>290</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>Why We Misjudge Our Future Feelings","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Ask people how they would feel a year after failing an important exam or losing a job, and most will predict lasting misery. Ask them about winning a scholarship or moving to a dream city, and they will imagine a glow that never fades. Psychologists who study affective forecasting, our attempts to predict our own emotional reactions, have found that we are usually right about the direction of our feelings but strikingly wrong about their intensity and, above all, their duration.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) This tendency, known as the impact bias, has been documented in a remarkable range of situations. Sports fans overestimate how gloomy they will be after their team loses; voters overestimate how long they will mourn an election defeat. In one study, students who were assigned to dormitories they had not wanted predicted a miserable year, only to report levels of happiness almost indistinguishable from those of students who got their first choice. In each case, the event mattered, but it mattered for a shorter time than people expected.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) One explanation is focalism. When we imagine a future event, we place it at the center of the picture and leave out everything else that will be going on in our lives. A student picturing life after a rejection from her favorite university sees only the rejection, not the new friends, next week’s exam and the hundreds of small pleasures and irritations that will compete for her attention. Real life, unlike imagined life, is crowded, and the crowd dilutes the impact of any single event.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) A second explanation is what researchers call immune neglect. Just as the body has an immune system that fights infection, the mind has a set of largely unconscious processes that help us recover from setbacks. We reinterpret failures, find silver linings, and gradually convince ourselves that the outcome was not so bad after all. Because these processes operate quietly, we fail to take them into account when we predict how we will cope, and so we underestimate our own resilience.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) The bias is not merely a curiosity; it shapes decisions. Someone who expects a promotion to make him permanently happy may sacrifice health and friendships to win it, while someone who dreads the pain of rejection may never apply for the job or the scholarship at all. In both cases, an inaccurate forecast leads to choices the person may later regret. Ironically, the fear of regret is itself subject to the impact bias: people consistently predict that they will feel worse about a narrowly missed opportunity than they actually do.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Can we forecast better? One promising strategy is surprisingly simple: instead of imagining the future, ask someone who is already living it. In one experiment, people predicted their enjoyment of an event more accurately when they read a single stranger’s report of the experience than when they were given detailed information about the event itself. The finding is humbling, because most of us believe our own situation is unique. Yet when it comes to predicting feelings, other people’s present may be a better guide than our own imagination.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากงานวิจัยด้านจิตวิทยา</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Humble Box Behind Global Trade","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Few inventions have changed the world as profoundly while attracting as little attention as the shipping container. It is, after all, just a steel box. Yet without it, the clothes in your wardrobe, the phone in your pocket and the fruit in your kitchen would almost certainly cost more, and many of them would never have reached you at all.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Before the container, cargo moved as what was known as break bulk. Goods were packed in sacks, barrels, crates and bales of every shape and size, and each item had to be lifted on and off ships by teams of dockworkers. A single vessel might spend a week or more in port being loaded, and theft and damage were so common that shippers treated them as a normal cost of doing business. Transport was so expensive that, for many products, it made little sense to manufacture them far from where they would be sold.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The breakthrough came in 1956, when an American trucking businessman named Malcom McLean loaded fifty eight metal boxes onto an old tanker in New Jersey and sent them to Texas. McLean’s insight was not the box itself; versions of it had existed for decades. It was the idea that the box should never be opened between the factory and the customer. Instead of unpacking cargo at every stage, cranes would simply lift the sealed container from truck to ship to train. According to one estimate, the cost of loading a ton of cargo fell from almost six dollars to about sixteen cents.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Cheap boxes alone, however, did not transform world trade. In the early years, rival shipping companies used containers of different sizes, and a box designed for one company’s ships often could not fit another’s cranes or trains. The real revolution began only when governments and industry agreed on international standards in the late 1960s. Once every port, ship and truck could handle the same box, goods could cross the world without being unpacked, and the system began to grow at an astonishing rate.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) The consequences reached far beyond the docks. Because transport became a tiny fraction of a product’s price, companies could split production across continents, designing a product in one country, making its parts in several others and assembling it somewhere else entirely. Port cities were reshaped, too. Old harbors near city centers, too cramped for giant container ships, fell into decline, while new terminals rose on cheap land far from town. Tens of thousands of dockworkers lost jobs that had supported their families for generations.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Today, the largest container ships carry more than twenty thousand standard boxes, and the system is so efficient that it has become almost invisible. Its efficiency, however, is also a weakness. When a single giant ship blocked a major canal for six days in 2021, the delays that followed revealed how tightly the world’s economies had been bound together by a simple steel box, and how little slack the system had to absorb a shock.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากข้อมูลทางประวัติศาสตร์เศรษฐกิจที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Octopuses are among the most intelligent invertebrates on Earth, yet their minds could hardly be more different from ours. About two thirds of an octopus’s neurons {{61}} not in its brain but in its eight arms, which can taste, touch and even make simple decisions on their own. {{62}} a backbone or shell, an octopus can squeeze its body through any gap larger than its beak. Perhaps most remarkable is its skill at camouflage. Within a fraction of a second, it can change both the color and the texture of its skin to {{63}} rocks, coral or seaweed. Strangely, most octopuses are thought to be color blind, so many scientists believe the animals {{64}} some other way of sensing color, possibly through light sensitive cells in the skin itself. Because octopuses live for only a year or two and receive no care from their parents, they must learn almost everything by trial and {{65}}.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">Louis Braille was born in 1809 in a small French town. At the age of three, he injured his eye with a sharp tool in the workshop {{66}} his father worked, and an infection eventually left him completely blind. At ten, he entered a school for blind children in Paris, where pupils read heavy books in large raised letters. In 1821, a former army officer showed the school a code of raised dots designed for reading in the dark. It was too complicated, but it gave the young Braille an idea. By the time he was fifteen, he {{67}} a far simpler code in which each letter used no more than six dots, small enough to fit under one fingertip. {{68}}, his invention was not welcomed at once. Sighted teachers preferred raised letters, which they could read themselves. Not until 1854, after his death, {{69}} officially adopted by the school. Today, his code is used worldwide, a fitting {{70}} to a teenager who refused to accept that reading had to be slow.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">For centuries, lighthouses guided sailors safely past rocks and sandbanks, and the keepers who looked after them led some of the loneliest lives imaginable. Every night the lamp had to be lit, the lens polished and the clockwork that turned the light wound by hand. {{71}} these dedicated workers, many more ships would have been wrecked along dangerous coasts. During the twentieth century, however, electric lamps, automatic timers and, later, satellite navigation made the keeper’s job increasingly {{72}}. One by one, lighthouses were automated, and their keepers were sent ashore. Many former keepers say they do not miss {{73}} by storms for weeks at a time, but they do miss the sense of purpose the work gave them. Some even wish they {{74}} the chance to say a proper goodbye before they left. Today, many old lighthouses have been turned into museums, guesthouses and research stations, giving these buildings a new {{75}} on life.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>This grogginess, known as sleep inertia, usually fades within fifteen to thirty minutes, although after very little sleep it can last considerably longer.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Most people have experienced the strange heaviness of the first few minutes after waking, when thoughts feel slow and even simple tasks seem difficult.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>For this reason, some hospitals advise doctors who have been woken during a night shift to wait a few minutes, where possible, before making important decisions.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>While sleep inertia lasts, studies have found, reaction times and short term memory can be even worse than those of a person who has stayed awake all night.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>The most common method today, reverse osmosis, pushes seawater at high pressure through membranes whose pores are too small for salt to pass through.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Although the oceans hold almost all of the planet’s water, that water is far too salty to drink or to use on most crops.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Yet the process is far from perfect: it consumes large amounts of energy and leaves behind a concentrated brine that can harm marine life if it is simply poured back into the sea.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Removing the salt, a process known as desalination, is therefore an increasingly attractive option for dry regions with long coastlines.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Most historians, however, point out that there is no solid evidence for this claim in any surviving Roman text.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>They suggest instead that the link may simply reflect how valuable salt was in the ancient world, and that the exact original meaning of the word remains uncertain.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>The English word “salary” comes from the Latin salarium, which is related to sal, the Latin word for salt.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>From this connection, a popular story has grown up that Roman soldiers were once paid in salt, or given money specifically to buy it.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>When the rock crevice that houses a colony of tiny rock ants is damaged, the colony must find a new home, and remarkably it does so without any leader.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Once the number of ants at a site reaches a certain threshold, the scouts switch from guiding nestmates one at a time to simply carrying them there, which speeds up the move dramatically.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Scouts that discover a promising site do not decide alone; instead, each one returns home and leads another ant to the site so that the newcomer can inspect it too.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Because ants wait longer before recruiting others to a poor site than to a good one, the best site gradually attracts more visitors than its rivals.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Supporters argue that growing crops indoors, in stacked layers under LED lights, could feed city dwellers while using a fraction of the land and water that traditional farms need.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>The reason is simple: sunlight is free, whereas the electricity needed to replace it is not, especially when energy prices rise.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>In practice, however, several of the best known vertical farming companies have gone bankrupt in recent years, unable to sell their produce at a profit.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>As a result, most vertical farms today concentrate on high value crops such as herbs and leafy greens, which grow quickly and sell for high prices, rather than on staples like rice.</span></span>"]},"EN70_I":"","EN70_P":{"c1":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 1<br>Situation: Returning rented camping gear at an outdoor equipment shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Welcome back! How was the trip?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> The views were amazing, but the tent had other ideas. {{1}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Oh dear. Hmm, this pole has been bent right back on itself. That happens when it’s forced into the sleeve.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{2}} It was pitch dark, and the wind was howling.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I understand, but that kind of damage isn’t covered, so we’ll have to take 600 baht from your deposit.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> 600 baht? {{3}} The whole rental only cost 900!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> A new pole costs us that much. But since you’re a regular, I can knock it down to 400 if you rent from us again this month.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{4}} My friends are already planning another trip, so it’s a deal.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great. I’ll note it on your account.</span>"],"c2":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 2<br>Situation: A student asks a classmate for feedback on her scholarship essay","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ning:</b> Hey, Arm, have you had a chance to read my essay yet? Be honest. The deadline’s on Friday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> I have. {{5}} The story about your grandmother’s shop is really moving.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ning:</b> But...? I can hear a “but” coming.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Well, {{6}} The question asks why you deserve the scholarship, and you only answer it in the last paragraph.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ning:</b> Ouch. {{7}} I spent hours on that story.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Not scrap it, just trim it. Keep the part about helping her with the accounts and link it to why you want to study business.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ning:</b> That makes sense. {{8}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Arm:</b> Any time. Just buy me a coffee when you win.</span>"],"c3":["Section I Part I: Short Conversations, Conversation 3<br>Situation: Two friends on a day hike when it starts to rain","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Are you sure this is the right way? We haven’t seen a trail marker for ages.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Relax. {{9}} We just need to keep heading uphill.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> That’s what you said an hour ago. And now it’s starting to rain. {{10}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> There’s no need to be sarcastic. It’s only a light shower.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> A light shower? It’s coming down in buckets! And my phone’s almost dead.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> OK, OK. {{11}} There’s a ranger station on the map, about a kilometer past that waterfall we saw.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Finally, he admits it! So we go back to the waterfall and follow the stream?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Exactly. {{12}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Fine. But next time, I’m in charge of the map.</span>"],"lc":["Section I Part II: Long Conversation<br>Situation: A student phones her mobile network about a huge bill after an exchange trip","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you for calling Skyline Mobile. My name is Dao. How can I help you today?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Hi. I’ve just got back from an exchange program in Japan, and my bill is 18,000 baht. {{13}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I’m sorry to hear that. Let me pull up your account. I can see that most of the charges are for mobile data used abroad last month.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> But that’s impossible. {{14}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I see. And did you take your Skyline SIM out of the phone?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> No, my phone takes two SIMs, so I kept both in. {{15}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Unfortunately, it might have. If data roaming was on for your Skyline SIM, the phone may have switched to it whenever the local signal dropped.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{16}} I honestly can’t remember touching that setting.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Not at all. Lots of customers get caught out this way, and the setting is easy to miss. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Really? {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> As this is the first time it’s happened on your account, I can take 70 percent off the whole bill. That would bring the bill down to 5,400 baht.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{19}} Is there any way to spread the payment over a few months?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Certainly. We can split it into three monthly payments at no extra cost.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Brilliant. {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> My pleasure. And next time, switch off roaming before you fly!</span>"],"ad1":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 1","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">SELL LAST YEAR’S BOOKS. FUND NEXT YEAR’S.</span><b class=\"xv-t\">BookLoop Textbook Buyback Week</b><span class=\"xv-p\"><b>Monday 7 to Friday 11 June</b>, 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.<br>Library Plaza, Booth 3</span><b class=\"xv-t2\">HOW IT WORKS</b><span class=\"en7-steps\"><span><b>1</b>Bring your textbooks and your student ID card.</span><span><b>2</b>Our team checks and grades each book on the spot.</span><span><b>3</b>Get paid instantly by bank transfer, or choose BookLoop store credit and receive 10% extra.</span></span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>Grade A</b>Like new, no writing inside. We pay 40% of the cover price.</span><span><b>Grade B</b>Highlighting or notes on up to 20 pages. We pay 25% of the cover price.</span><span><b>Grade C</b>Heavy notes, loose pages or water damage. Not bought, but we will recycle it for you.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>Sell 5 or more Grade A books and get a 100 baht voucher for the Library Café!</b></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">We only buy current editions listed on the BookLoop website. Older editions may be donated to the Hill Schools Reading Project. Amounts over 3,000 baht per person per day are paid as store credit only.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"ad2":["Section II Part I: Advertisements, Advertisement 2","<span class=\"xv xv-ad en7-ad\"><span class=\"en7-kick\">LOVE TRAINS? LOVE TALKING TO PEOPLE?</span><b class=\"xv-t\">Old Line Railway Museum is hiring Weekend Gallery Guides</b><span class=\"xv-p\">Help visitors from around the world discover 120 years of rail history in our restored station building at Sila Junction.</span><span class=\"en7-feat\"><span><b>What you’ll do</b>Lead 45 minute tours in Thai and English. Chinese or Japanese is a plus.</span><span><b>Show and tell</b>Run our signal box demonstration twice a day.</span><span><b>Who we want</b>Aged 18 or over and confident speaking to groups</span><span><b>Your time</b>At least two weekend days per month</span><span><b>No experience?</b>No problem. Full training is provided.</span></span><span class=\"xv-p en7-price\"><b>Pay: 180 baht per hour</b>, plus 50 baht per hour for tours given in a third language.<br>Free museum pass for you and one guest.</span><span class=\"xv-p en7-fine\">Training takes place on 4 and 5 May. Applicants who cannot attend both days will not be considered. Apply by 20 April at oldline.example/jobs with a one minute video introducing yourself in English.</span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"rv":["Section II Part II: Product/Service Review<br>Review: A Refurbished Nimbus 14 Laptop from ReNew Tech","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When my old laptop died just before my finals, I could not afford a new one, so I took a gamble on a refurbished Nimbus 14 from ReNew Tech, an online shop that repairs and resells used machines. It cost 11,900 baht, roughly half the price of the same model new. After two months of daily use, I can say that the gamble has largely paid off.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The laptop arrived well packed and graded “Excellent,” which, according to the website, means “minor signs of use.” In reality, there was a scratch across the lid and a shiny patch on the touchpad. These flaws are purely cosmetic, but anyone expecting a machine that looks brand new may feel let down.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Where it matters, however, the Nimbus delivers. ReNew Tech had replaced the battery and the storage drive, and the machine copes with forty open browser tabs and video calls with my study group without slowing down. The battery comfortably lasts a full day of lectures.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Customer service was the real surprise. When the charger stopped working in the third week, I braced myself for a long battle. Instead, a replacement arrived within two days, and nobody asked me to prove that I had not damaged it. This alone made me trust the company more than some big brands I have dealt with.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) There are drawbacks beyond appearance. The warranty lasts only six months, compared with the usual two years for a new laptop, and the webcam is noticeably grainy in poor light. ReNew Tech also charges 490 baht for delivery, a cost that is easy to miss until the final checkout page.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Would I buy refurbished again? Without hesitation, provided I could see photos of the actual unit first. If you need a dependable machine on a tight budget and can live with a few scratches, refurbished is hard to beat. If you want something flawless to show off, save up for a new one.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">รีวิวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ สินค้าและร้านค้าในรีวิวเป็นของสมมติ</span>"],"nw":["Section II Part III: News Report","<span class=\"xp\"><b>(1) Short of teachers, Khao Rang schools switch to a four day week</b></span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Fourteen state schools in the hill district of Khao Rang will hold classes only from Monday to Thursday from next term, after struggling for three years to fill teaching posts, education officials confirmed on Tuesday.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Under the plan, each school day will be extended by 75 minutes so that students lose no teaching time over the year. Fridays will be set aside for teacher training and lesson planning, as well as optional activities run by community volunteers.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Officials say the change is designed to make jobs in the district more attractive. Of the 46 teachers who left Khao Rang schools last year, 31 said in exit surveys that long commutes and heavy workloads were their main reasons. “We cannot pay teachers more than the national scale,” said deputy director Wanida Saetang. “What we can offer is time.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Early signs are encouraging. Since the plan was announced in March, the district has received 58 applications for 20 vacant posts, compared with just 12 applications for the same number of posts a year earlier.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Parents, however, are divided. Some welcome the extra day, but others worry about who will look after younger children on Fridays. “For office workers in the city, a long weekend sounds lovely,” said Somchai Boonmee, who sells vegetables at the district market six days a week. “For people like me, it means my eight year old either comes to the market with me or stays at home alone.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) Education researchers urge caution. Dr. Kanya Ruangsri, who has studied similar schemes abroad, said results have been mixed. “In some places, test scores held steady and attendance actually rose. In others, the youngest pupils struggled with the longer days,” she said. “The schedule itself is not a magic bullet. It depends on what happens on that fifth day.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(8) The district will review the plan after one year, using test results, attendance and teacher turnover. If not enough volunteers can be found to run Friday activities, officials say school buildings will at least remain open as supervised study spaces.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">ข่าวนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ สถานที่ บุคคล และตัวเลขในข่าวเป็นข้อมูลสมมติ</span>"],"v1":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 1","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">University Sports Day: Medal Table</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Medals won by six faculties across 28 events. Faculties are ranked by points: each gold medal is worth 5 points, each silver 3 points and each bronze 1 point.</span><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb4\"><span><b>Faculty</b><b>Gold</b><b>Silver</b><b>Bronze</b></span><span><b>Engineering</b><i>5</i><i>6</i><i>9</i></span><span><b>Law</b><i>8</i><i>2</i><i>3</i></span><span><b>Medicine</b><i>6</i><i>7</i><i>2</i></span><span><b>Arts</b><i>4</i><i>5</i><i>6</i></span><span><b>Science</b><i>3</i><i>6</i><i>7</i></span><span><b>Business</b><i>2</i><i>2</i><i>1</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"v2":["Section II Part IV: Visuals, Visual 2","<span class=\"xv\"><b class=\"xv-t\">Riders on two campus shuttle routes, January to July</b><span class=\"xv-mut\">Number of riders per month (thousands). The university was on semester break for most of April.</span><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 214\" class=\"xv-svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Monthly riders on two campus shuttle routes, January to July\"><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"186.0\" y2=\"186.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"189.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"147.0\" y2=\"147.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"150.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">15</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"108.0\" y2=\"108.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"111.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">30</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"69.0\" y2=\"69.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"72.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">45</text><line x1=\"34\" x2=\"262\" y1=\"30.0\" y2=\"30.0\" class=\"gl\"/><text x=\"29\" y=\"33.0\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jan</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feb</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mar</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Apr</text><text x=\"186\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jun</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"204\" class=\"ax\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Jul</text><polyline class=\"ln c1\" points=\"34,76.8 72,69.0 110,82.0 148,139.2 186,128.8 224,63.8 262,56.0\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"76.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"69.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"82.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"139.2\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"128.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"63.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c1\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"56.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"66.0\" class=\"lb\">Library Loop</text><polyline class=\"ln c0\" points=\"34,134.0 72,118.4 110,105.4 148,154.8 186,121.0 224,71.6 262,50.8\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"34\" cy=\"134.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"72\" cy=\"118.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"110\" cy=\"105.4\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"148\" cy=\"154.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"186\" cy=\"121.0\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"224\" cy=\"71.6\" r=\"2.6\"/><circle class=\"c0\" cx=\"262\" cy=\"50.8\" r=\"2.6\"/><text x=\"268\" y=\"46.8\" class=\"lb\">Dorm Express</text></svg><span class=\"en7-tb en7-tb7\"><span><b>Month</b><b>Jan</b><b>Feb</b><b>Mar</b><b>Apr</b><b>May</b><b>Jun</b><b>Jul</b></span><span><b>Library Loop</b><i>42</i><i>45</i><i>40</i><i>18</i><i>22</i><i>47</i><i>50</i></span><span><b>Dorm Express</b><i>20</i><i>26</i><i>31</i><i>12</i><i>25</i><i>44</i><i>52</i></span></span></span><span class=\"xv-note\">โฆษณา ข่าว และข้อมูลในภาพชุดนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ ไม่ใช่ข้อมูลจริง</span>"],"a1":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 1<br>All Eyes on You? The Truth About the Spotlight Effect","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Imagine walking into a lecture hall ten minutes late, only to realize that there is a large coffee stain on your shirt. As you search for a seat, it feels as though every head in the room has turned toward you. Psychologists have a name for this uncomfortable conviction: the spotlight effect, our tendency to overestimate how much other people notice our appearance and behavior.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The effect was demonstrated in a now classic study at an American university. Students were asked to put on a shirt printed with the face of a singer whom most of their peers considered hopelessly unfashionable, and then to walk into a room where other students were already seated. Afterwards, the wearers estimated that about half of the people in the room had noticed the shirt. In fact, fewer than a quarter had.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Why are we so poorly calibrated? Part of the answer is that each of us is, inevitably, at the center of our own experience. We begin with how intensely we feel about ourselves and assume that others must feel something similar. We do make some allowance for the fact that other people are busy with their own concerns, but rarely enough. The result is a picture of the social world in which we play a far larger role than we actually do.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The same bias extends beyond clothing. In later studies, participants who had performed poorly in a group task believed that their teammates would judge them far more harshly than the teammates actually did. Nervous speakers, meanwhile, were convinced that their anxiety was obvious to the audience, a related error known as the illusion of transparency. Observers, it turned out, were largely unaware of the fear that the speakers felt was written all over their faces.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Paradoxically, the spotlight effect cuts both ways. Just as we overestimate how much others notice our blunders, we also overestimate how much they notice our triumphs. The student who spends an hour perfecting a hairstyle may be as disappointed by the lack of reaction as the student with the coffee stain is relieved by it. Other people, in short, are not ignoring us out of cruelty or kindness; they are simply preoccupied with their own spotlights.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) Recognizing the effect can be liberating. People who are reminded that others are paying less attention than they think tend to become more willing to speak up in class, ask questions and try unfamiliar activities. This does not mean that our actions are invisible or that nothing we do matters. Rather, it suggests that the real audience is usually smaller, and far less critical, than the one in our heads. The coffee stain, in all likelihood, will be forgotten by everyone else long before it is washed out.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากแนวคิดทางจิตวิทยาสังคมที่เป็นที่ยอมรับ ตัวเลขในบทความเป็นค่าโดยประมาณ</span>"],"a2":["Section II Part V: General Articles, Article 2<br>The Wood Wide Web: Wonder or Wishful Thinking?","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Beneath almost every forest floor lies a dense tangle of fungal threads. Many of these fungi form partnerships with tree roots known as mycorrhizae. The basic arrangement is ancient and uncontroversial: the fungus spreads far into the soil, gathering water and nutrients such as phosphorus that roots struggle to reach, and in return the tree supplies the fungus with sugars made through photosynthesis.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) What has proved far more contentious is the idea that these threads link neighboring trees into a shared network. In the late 1990s, a widely reported experiment appeared to show that carbon could pass from one tree to another through fungal connections. The finding, quickly nicknamed the “wood wide web,” captured the public imagination. In books, documentaries and countless social media posts, forests came to be portrayed as cooperative communities in which old “mother trees” feed their seedlings and warn their neighbors of insect attacks.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) In recent years, however, a number of forest ecologists have begun to push back. A review of the field concluded that the popular story had run well ahead of the evidence. Very few studies, the reviewers pointed out, had confirmed that fungal networks connect trees in natural forests rather than in laboratory pots or plantations. Where resources did seem to move between trees, it was often unclear whether they had traveled through fungal threads or simply through the soil. Studies reporting that seedlings grew better near large trees frequently could not rule out other explanations, such as differences in shade or soil chemistry.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The critics were careful about what they were not saying. They did not deny that mycorrhizal fungi are vital to forest health, nor did they rule out the possibility that some sharing takes place. Their concern was the gap between tentative findings and confident claims. Later papers, they noted, often cited the original studies as having proved far more than their authors had claimed, a pattern that could distort both public understanding and forest policy.</span><span class=\"xp\">(5) Supporters of the network idea reply that absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. Mapping underground connections is fiendishly difficult: threads thinner than a human hair cannot easily be traced through the soil without destroying the very links being studied. Some argue that the critics have set an unreasonably high bar, demanding a level of proof that few ecological claims could meet.</span><span class=\"xp\">(6) There is also a deeper disagreement about language. Describing trees as “mothers” that “talk” to one another may make science more appealing, but it risks smuggling human motives into places where none may exist. A fungus that moves carbon between trees could be serving its own interests, spreading its bets across several hosts, rather than acting as a charitable courier.</span><span class=\"xp\">(7) The debate, then, is less about whether forests are fascinating than about how scientists should talk about what they do not yet know. The forest may well turn out to be a web; for now, we should admit that we have seen only a few of its threads.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทความนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ โดยเรียบเรียงจากข้อถกเถียงทางนิเวศวิทยาป่าไม้ที่มีอยู่จริง</span>"],"w1":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 1","<span class=\"xp\">Sea otters, among the smallest marine mammals in North America, are often described as a keystone species, a term for an animal {{61}} the health of an entire ecosystem depends. They feed heavily on sea urchins, spiny creatures that graze on giant kelp. When otters were hunted almost to extinction for their thick fur in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, urchin numbers exploded, and along many coasts vast kelp forests {{62}} to bare, rocky stretches known as urchin barrens. {{63}} had otters returned to some of these coasts than the kelp began to recover, bringing back the fish, crabs and seabirds that shelter in it. Scientists now believe that healthy kelp forests may also help to {{64}} climate change by absorbing carbon dioxide as they grow. {{65}}, not everyone welcomes the otters’ return: shellfish divers complain that the animals eat the very clams and abalone that they rely on to make a living.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w2":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 2","<span class=\"xp\">Airlines routinely sell more tickets than there are seats on a plane. The practice may seem reckless, but it rests on careful statistics. On a typical flight, a small but predictable share of travelers never arrive at the gate, and the number of such passengers {{66}} surprisingly stable from season to season. {{67}} every ticket holder to show up, the airline would have to find volunteers willing to take a later flight, usually in exchange for vouchers or cash. Without overbooking, however, airlines would often fly with empty seats, and they argue that fares for everyone would rise as a result. Critics counter that the {{68}} of a passenger who is bumped from a flight is hardly trivial: someone who misses a wedding or a job interview cannot simply be paid off. Some carriers have therefore stopped {{69}} flights on certain routes altogether, deciding that the damage to their reputation {{70}} the savings.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"w3":["Section III Part I: Text Completion, Passage 3","<span class=\"xp\">Almost everyone has noticed it: one person in a meeting yawns, and soon several others {{71}}. Contagious yawning, as researchers call it, is surprisingly hard to explain. A popular theory holds that it is linked to empathy, the ability to share other people’s feelings. Supporters note that people seem more likely to catch a yawn from a close friend than from a stranger. {{72}} the theory is correct, however, remains uncertain. Large studies have found only a weak link with empathy scores. In one experiment, a volunteer sitting alone in a room yawned repeatedly while merely reading about yawning; he {{73}} been influenced by the words alone, since no one else was present. {{74}} by such mixed results, some scientists now suspect that yawns spread through a more basic, automatic response that has little to do with empathy. Whatever the explanation, the phenomenon is a reminder of how closely {{75}} our bodies are to the people around us.</span><span class=\"xv-note\">บทอ่านนี้เขียนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อใช้ฝึกทำข้อสอบ</span>"],"p76":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 76","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>These molecules try to line up with the field, and because its direction reverses billions of times every second, they are forced to flip back and forth at enormous speed.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>A microwave oven heats food in a way that is quite different from a conventional oven, which warms the outside of a dish first and relies on that heat slowly working its way inward.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>As the flipping molecules jostle their neighbors, this motion spreads through the food as heat, warming it from within rather than only at the surface.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Inside the oven, a device called a magnetron produces microwaves, which create a rapidly changing electric field that acts mainly on the water molecules contained in the food.</span></span>"],"p77":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 77","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>According to a popular story, John Montagu, the fourth Earl of Sandwich, was so absorbed in a long card game that he refused to leave the table for a proper meal.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>People had eaten bread with fillings for centuries, but in England the combination did not have a widely used name until the second half of the eighteenth century.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Instead, he asked a servant to bring him slices of meat between two pieces of bread, which allowed him to eat with one hand and keep playing without getting grease on the cards.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Whether or not the tale is true, the name stuck: people were soon said to be asking for “the same as Sandwich,” and the shortened form came to mean the dish itself.</span></span>"],"p78":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 78","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Some cities have responded by offering tax breaks to supermarkets that open branches in these areas, while others support weekly farmers’ markets and mobile grocery vans.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>In many large cities, there are neighborhoods where residents cannot easily buy fresh, affordable food, and researchers often describe such places as food deserts.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Without a car, people living there may have to rely on small convenience stores that sell mostly packaged snacks and sugary drinks, often at higher prices than supermarkets.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>Over time, such a diet has been linked to higher rates of obesity and diabetes, which is why the issue is increasingly treated as a matter of public health rather than of shopping habits.</span></span>"],"p79":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 79","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>Alligators have a broad, rounded snout, and when their mouths are closed, their lower teeth are mostly hidden from view.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Although alligators and crocodiles look so alike that many people use the two names interchangeably, they belong to different families and can be told apart by a few clear features.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>Crocodiles, by contrast, have a longer, more pointed snout, and a large fourth tooth on the lower jaw remains visible even when the mouth is shut, giving them a toothy grin.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>The two animals also differ in where they live: most alligators prefer fresh water, whereas many crocodiles have special glands that allow them to tolerate salt water for long periods.</span></span>"],"p80":["Section III Part II: Paragraph Organization, Item 80","<span class=\"xo\"><b>A</b><span>As a result, a large city can be several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside, especially at night, when the stored heat is slowly released back into the air.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>B</b><span>Unlike fields and forests, these hard surfaces absorb large amounts of solar energy during the day, and the lack of trees means that there is little shade or evaporation to cool the air.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>C</b><span>City centers are covered with materials such as concrete, asphalt and dark roofing, which have replaced much of the vegetation that once covered the land.</span></span><span class=\"xo\"><b>D</b><span>This extra warmth is not merely uncomfortable; it drives up demand for air conditioning, which in turn releases more waste heat and makes heat waves more dangerous for elderly residents.</span></span>"]},"SS70Q":"","TG3A_I":"","TG3A_S":"","TG3B_I":"","TG3B_S":"","TG3C_I":"","TG3C_S":"","TG3D_I":"","TG3D_S":"","TG70B_I":"","TG70B_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you give me a hand with these boxes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I heard you lost your part time job. I'm really sorry.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How often do you go to the gym?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you mind if I open the window?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What's the matter? You look upset.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm thinking of switching my major to economics.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can I get you anything to drink?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How did your job interview go?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Let's grab some dinner after the movie.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you like me to save you a seat in the hall?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a train station ticket counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> Excuse me. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, there are two. One leaves at 2:15 and the other at 4:40.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> I'll take the 2:15, please. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> About four hours, so you'll arrive at around 6:15.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Passenger:</b> Okay. {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> It's 300 baht for a second class seat.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends planning a weekend trip","<span class=\"xd\"><b>June:</b> Are we still going hiking on Saturday?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> {{14}}. The forecast says it's going to rain all day.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>June:</b> Oh no. {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Well, Sunday looks sunny, so we could go then.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>June:</b> Good idea. {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Beam:</b> Great. I'll pick you up at seven, then.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: A phone call to a dental clinic","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Bright Smile Dental Clinic. How can I help you?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> Hi, {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Of course. Have you visited us before?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Then I should have your records. We have an opening on Tuesday at 3 p.m. {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> I'm afraid I have class until four. Do you have anything later?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> How about 5:30?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Caller:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Great. See you on Tuesday, then.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student is interviewed for a summer internship at a museum","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Thank you for coming in, Pim. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I'm studying history, and I've always wanted to see how a museum works behind the scenes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Great. Our interns help with guided tours for visitors. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Quite comfortable, actually. I'm the president of my school's debate club.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Impressive. However, the internship sometimes involves weekend work. {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Not at all. I'm usually free on weekends.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Good to hear. {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> Yes. Will interns get any training before they lead tours?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Interviewer:</b> Absolutely. There is a training program that lasts two weeks. We will contact all applicants by Friday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{25}}</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two roommates talking about housework","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Hey, Tom. Do you have a minute? {{26}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Sure. What's up?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Well, the dishes keep piling up in the sink. {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> Oh, I'm sorry. I've been so busy with exams that I didn't even notice.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> I understand. {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> That's a good idea. How about I do the dishes on weekdays and you do them on weekends?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> I'll take it out every Monday and Thursday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ken:</b> Perfect. {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tom:</b> No problem. Thanks for telling me instead of getting angry.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Bangkok was once called the “Venice of the East” because of its huge network of canals, known in Thai as <b>khlong</b>. For centuries, these waterways {{31}} as the main routes for transport and trade, and floating markets were a common sight along their banks.</span><span class=\"xp\">As more roads {{32}} in the twentieth century, many canals were filled in to make room for cars. The canals that remained often became dumping grounds for waste, and the water turned dark and smelly. {{33}}, many residents began to see the canals as a problem rather than a treasure.</span><span class=\"xp\">Today, the city is trying to bring its waterways back to life. Canal boat services help commuters {{34}} the heavy traffic on the roads, and some neighborhoods have turned their canal banks into walking paths and small parks. However, cleaning the water remains a challenge {{35}} wastewater from homes and businesses still flows directly into many canals.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Many parents complain that their teenage children stay up late at night and find it almost impossible to wake up in the morning. While this is often blamed on laziness, scientists say that biology {{36}} a major role.</span><span class=\"xp\">During adolescence, the body begins to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, about two hours later than it does in children and adults. As a result, most teenagers do not feel tired until close to midnight. Experts recommend {{37}} eight to ten hours of sleep a night, but early school start times make this goal difficult to reach.</span><span class=\"xp\">Students who sleep too little are more likely to have trouble concentrating, and they are also {{38}} to accidents and mood problems. Some schools in other countries have moved their start times later, and studies show that their students now get more sleep and pay more attention in class. If more schools {{39}} the same, teenagers might perform better without studying any harder. In the meantime, experts advise teenagers to {{40}} screens at least an hour before bedtime, because the light from phones can delay sleep even further.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Not long ago, playing video games was seen as a waste of time. Today, competitive gaming, or esports, has grown into a global industry {{41}} billions of dollars. Professional players train for many hours a day, follow strict diets, and work with coaches, much like traditional athletes.</span><span class=\"xp\">The popularity of esports has also changed how people view gaming. Major tournaments now fill large stadiums, and millions more watch online. Esports was {{42}} as a medal event at the Asian Games in 2022, and many universities now offer scholarships to talented players.</span><span class=\"xp\">Despite this success, critics {{43}} concerns about the health of young players. Sitting in front of a screen for long periods can lead to eye strain, back pain, and poor sleep. Some also worry that young people may give up their studies in the hope of becoming professionals, {{44}} only a tiny number ever earn a living from gaming. Supporters reply that, like any sport, esports can teach teamwork and discipline, {{45}} it is practiced in a balanced way.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every Saturday morning, a small wooden house near the beach in a fishing village in Trang fills with children. Some sit on mats reading picture books, while others gather around a volunteer who is reading aloud. The house is a community library, and it exists because of a woman named Nok, who is only 24 years old.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Nok grew up in the village, where most families depend on fishing. As a child, she rarely had books of her own, and the nearest public library was more than an hour away by bus. After finishing university, she returned home and noticed that little had changed. Many children could read only slowly, and few of them read for pleasure.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) With her savings, Nok repaired her late grandfather’s old house and filled its shelves with books donated by friends and online followers. At first, parents were unsure. They worried that reading would distract their children from helping with the family business. Nok invited them to a reading event, where they watched their children perform short plays based on stories they had read. Several parents were so moved that they offered to help run the library.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Three years later, the library has more than 3,000 books and a team of volunteers. Teachers at the local school say that students’ reading scores have improved. For Nok, however, the greatest reward is simpler. “When a child asks to borrow a second book before finishing the first,” she says, “I know we are doing something right.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Almost everyone has put off an important task at some point, whether it is studying for an exam or answering a difficult email. This habit, known as procrastination, is often seen as a sign of laziness or poor time management. However, psychologists now believe that it has more to do with emotions than with time.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) According to this view, people delay tasks that make them feel bad, such as bored, anxious, or afraid of failing. By turning to something more pleasant, like scrolling through social media, they get a quick sense of relief. Unfortunately, this relief does not last. The task is still waiting, and the stress grows stronger as the deadline approaches.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) This explains why simply making a detailed schedule often fails to cure procrastination. If the underlying feelings are not addressed, people will continue to avoid the task no matter how well they plan their time. Researchers suggest more effective strategies, such as breaking a large task into very small steps and focusing only on the first one. Another approach is to treat oneself with kindness after procrastinating. Studies have found that students who forgave themselves for delaying their studies were less likely to procrastinate before the next exam.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) In short, beating procrastination may depend less on discipline and more on understanding our own feelings. The next time you find yourself avoiding a task, it may help to ask not “Why am I so lazy?” but “What about this task makes me uncomfortable?”</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In many Western countries, it is common for students to take a gap year, a break of about a year between finishing high school and starting university. In Thailand, however, the idea is still unusual. Most students move directly from one stage of education to the next, and a year away from school is often viewed as falling behind.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters of the gap year argue that it gives young people valuable time to discover who they are. Instead of choosing a university major under pressure, students can work, travel, volunteer, or try out different fields before making a decision. Many return with greater maturity and a clearer sense of purpose. Some universities abroad even encourage accepted students to delay their entry for a year.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, however, point out that a gap year is not equally available to everyone. Traveling abroad or doing unpaid volunteer work can be expensive, so the experience may mostly benefit students from wealthy families. There is also the risk that a year without clear goals becomes a year wasted. Some students find it hard to return to the routine of studying after a long break.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) The value of a gap year, then, seems to depend largely on how it is spent. A year that is carefully planned can be as educational as any classroom, while an unplanned one may simply delay the future. For students considering this option, the key question is not whether to take a break, but what they hope to gain from it.</span>"]},"TG70C_I":"","TG70C_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I love your new haircut! It really suits you.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, how do I get to the nearest BTS station?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I don't really like horror movies.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Sorry, could you say that again? It's a bit noisy in here.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Shall I help you carry those books to the library?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I wish I had studied harder for the test.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> This soup is far too salty. Could I speak to the manager?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> May I borrow your charger for a few minutes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> You've been working here for ten years? That's amazing!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> It was great seeing you again. Let's keep in touch!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a restaurant","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Waiter:</b> Good evening. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> No, we don't. Is there a table for two available?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Waiter:</b> Yes, right this way, please. Here are your menus. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Two iced teas, please. And {{13}}? My friend is vegetarian.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Waiter:</b> Yes. Our green curry can be made with tofu instead of chicken.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two classmates talking on the phone","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Fah:</b> Hey, Win. Did I miss anything important in class today?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Win:</b> {{14}}. The teacher announced a quiz for next Monday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fah:</b> Oh no! {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Win:</b> Chapters three and four. I'll send you my notes tonight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fah:</b> {{16}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Win:</b> No problem. That's what friends are for.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At the front desk of a fitness center","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Hi there. Welcome to FitZone.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great. We have two plans. The monthly plan is 1,200 baht, and the yearly plan is 10,800 baht.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, it does. Yearly members get free yoga and swimming classes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> That sounds good. {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Of course. We offer a free trial for one day. You just need to show your ID card.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> See you then!</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A university student visits a dormitory near campus","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> Hello. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Yes, I saw your post online. Is it still available?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> It is. It's on the third floor, and it has a private bathroom and a small balcony.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Sounds nice. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> It's 5,500 baht a month, not including electricity and water.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> I see. {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> Yes. Two months' rent in advance, which you'll get back when you move out.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> That's fine. What about the rules? {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> I'm afraid not. For security reasons, all visitors must leave by ten at night.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Understood. I think I'd like to take the room.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Sure. Do I need to bring anything?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Owner:</b> Just your ID card and the deposit.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: A coordinator talks to volunteers before a beach cleanup","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> Good morning, everyone, and thank you for coming. {{26}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> How long will the cleanup take?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> About three hours. We'll work in teams of five along the beach.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> Put them in the blue bags. They will be sent to a recycling center.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> And {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> Don't touch it with your hands. Use the tongs and put it in the red box.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> We have gloves and hats for everyone at the tent over there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Coordinator:</b> One more thing. {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> Thanks for the reminder. Let's get started!</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">In 2019, traditional Thai massage, known as <b>nuad thai</b>, was added to the UNESCO list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. The practice, which {{31}} down from teachers to students for generations, is now recognized as an important part of Thai culture.</span><span class=\"xp\">Unlike many types of massage that mainly aim to relax the muscles, nuad thai is {{32}} on the belief that energy flows through the body along invisible lines. Practitioners use their hands, elbows, knees, and even feet to apply pressure {{33}} specific points along these lines, and they gently stretch the body in ways similar to yoga.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{34}} it was once taught mainly in temples, such as Wat Pho in Bangkok, nuad thai is now taught at schools around the world. Supporters hope that the UNESCO recognition will help {{35}} this traditional knowledge for future generations.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Chiang Mai has become one of the most popular cities in the world for digital nomads, people who work online while traveling from place to place. The city {{36}} them with its low cost of living, reliable internet, and relaxed lifestyle.</span><span class=\"xp\">In recent years, cafes and shared offices {{37}} all over the city to serve this growing community. Many local business owners welcome the extra income. {{38}}, some residents worry that the rising demand for housing has pushed up rents, making it harder for local families to find homes they can afford.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{39}} attract more remote workers, Thailand has introduced a new type of visa that allows them to stay in the country for longer periods. The challenge now is to build a city that welcomes newcomers while remaining {{40}} for the people who have always lived there.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Fast fashion refers to cheap, trendy clothing that is designed, produced, and sold very quickly. It allows shoppers to {{41}} with the latest styles without spending much money. However, many of these clothes are worn only a few times {{42}} being thrown away.</span><span class=\"xp\">The environmental cost of this habit is high. Growing cotton and dyeing fabric require {{43}} water, and much of the dye ends up in rivers. In addition, many cheap garments are made from synthetic materials that release tiny pieces of plastic when they are washed.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{44}} more consumers become aware of these problems, some brands have started to offer repair services and to sell secondhand clothing. Experts say that buying less and choosing better quality can make a real difference. In fact, they often point out that the most sustainable piece of clothing is the one {{45}} you already own.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) For two years, Arm spent his days delivering food around Bangkok on his motorcycle. At red lights, while other riders checked their phones for new orders, he took out a small notebook of English vocabulary and quietly tested himself.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Arm had left school at sixteen when his father fell ill and the family needed money. Although he had been a good student, he believed that university was no longer a realistic goal for someone like him. That changed when a regular customer, a retired teacher, noticed his notebook and asked what he was studying. When she heard his story, she offered to tutor him for free every Sunday.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Studying while working full time was exhausting. Arm often slept only five hours a night, and there were times when he wanted to give up. His tutor kept reminding him that progress did not have to be fast, only steady. Little by little, his scores on practice tests improved.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Last year, at the age of 22, Arm was accepted into a public university to study engineering. He still delivers food on weekends to support his family, but these days he carries textbooks instead of a small notebook. “I used to think my life was already decided,” he says. “Now I know it can still be changed.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Bees are among the most important animals on Earth, although many people only think about them when they are afraid of being stung. Around three quarters of the world’s major food crops depend at least partly on pollinators such as bees. Without them, foods like apples, almonds, and coffee would become much scarcer and more expensive.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) In recent decades, however, bee populations in many parts of the world have declined. Scientists point to several causes. Some pesticides used on farms can damage bees’ nervous systems, making it harder for them to find their way back to their hives. The loss of wild flowers, as land is turned into farms and cities, also leaves bees with less food. Climate change adds further pressure by changing the time of year when flowers bloom.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Fortunately, some solutions are surprisingly simple. Farmers can plant strips of wild flowers along the edges of their fields, and city residents can grow flowering plants on balconies and rooftops. Several countries have also restricted the use of certain pesticides that are known to harm bees.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Protecting bees, scientists argue, is not only about saving one type of insect. It is about protecting the food we eat and the natural systems that support all life on the planet.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every year, hundreds of thousands of Thai students sit for university entrance exams. For many of them, a few days of testing will shape the next four years of their lives. This has led to an ongoing debate: should a single set of test scores decide a student’s future?</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters of exams argue that they are the fairest method available. Everyone answers the same questions under the same conditions, and the results cannot easily be influenced by money or personal connections. Exams also give universities a quick and consistent way to compare large numbers of applicants.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, however, say that exams measure only a narrow range of abilities. A student who is a talented musician, a skilled debater, or a respected community leader may not be able to show those strengths on a multiple choice test. They also point out that students from wealthy families can pay for expensive tutoring schools, so the competition is not as equal as it seems.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) In response, many universities now combine test scores with other methods, such as portfolios and interviews. As one admissions officer puts it, “The question is not whether exams are good or bad, but whether they are enough on their own.”</span>"]},"TG70D_I":"","TG70D_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Congratulations on winning the speech contest!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you rather cook at home or order food tonight?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How come you're still awake? It's past midnight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you keep it down a little? I'm trying to study.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Which bus goes to Central World?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm afraid I can't make it to your party on Saturday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you know where I can get my phone repaired?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What would you do if you won the lottery?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Thanks so much for lending me your notes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, is this seat taken?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a post office","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Next, please.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Sure. Would you like regular or express delivery?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Regular is 45 baht and takes three days. Express is 90 baht and arrives tomorrow.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> I'll take express, then. {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Clerk:</b> Yes. Your tracking number is printed on this receipt.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two coworkers at an office","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nan:</b> The meeting has been moved to 3 p.m. Did you get the email?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> {{14}}. I've been out of the office all morning.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nan:</b> Well, {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Oh no, I haven't finished mine yet. I'd better start now.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nan:</b> {{16}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> That would be great. Could you check the numbers for me?</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At the lost and found counter of a shopping mall","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I see. Can you describe it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me check. Is this yours?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Yes, that's it! {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> One of our cleaners brought it in about twenty minutes ago.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I will. Please sign this form before you go.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A high school student meets a guidance counselor","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> Hi, Mew. Come and sit down. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> Not yet. I'm stuck between psychology and social work.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> Both are great choices. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> I like learning how the mind works, but I also want to help people in the community directly.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> In that case, {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> That's a good idea. Do you know anyone I could contact?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> Yes. {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> Really? I'd love to ask her about her job.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Counselor:</b> I'll send you her email address. {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mew:</b> Of course. Thank you so much for your help.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends planning a surprise for a classmate","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> Hey, Ice. Joy's birthday is next Friday. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Great idea! Where should we have it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Perfect. How many people should we invite?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Okay. I'll take care of the cake. {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> Sure. I'll buy some balloons and string lights.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> Great. {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pond:</b> Don't worry. My lips are sealed.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Thailand is the largest exporter of durian in the world. The durian is a fruit {{31}} smell is so strong that it is banned in many hotels and on public transport in some countries. Yet for its many fans, the creamy flesh is worth every bit of the smell.</span><span class=\"xp\">Demand from Chinese consumers, who see durian as a luxury gift, {{32}} grown rapidly in recent years. To meet this demand, durian farms have {{33}} across eastern and southern Thailand, and many farmers have switched from other crops to durian.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, the boom has also brought problems. Some sellers have {{34}} durians before they were ripe in order to sell them earlier at higher prices, which has damaged buyers’ trust. {{35}} protect the reputation of Thai durian, the authorities now inspect fruit more carefully before it is exported.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Many people believe that children learn languages more {{36}} than adults. It is true that young children often achieve better pronunciation. However, adults have advantages of their own. They can use their knowledge of grammar and their study skills to learn {{37}} than children in the early stages.</span><span class=\"xp\">Learning a new language may also {{38}} the brain. Some studies suggest that people who speak two languages are better at switching between tasks, and that using more than one language may help delay memory problems in old age.</span><span class=\"xp\">Whatever your age, the key is regular practice. The {{39}} you use a language, the more confident you become. So if you have always dreamed of speaking another language, remember that it is never too late {{40}} one.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Online scams have become one of the most common crimes in Thailand. Criminals often {{41}} to be police officers or bank staff in order to gain their victims’ trust. They create a sense of urgency, telling victims that their bank accounts will be frozen {{42}} they transfer money immediately.</span><span class=\"xp\">Other scammers send fake links that lead to websites almost identical {{43}} real ones. When victims enter their personal information, the criminals use it to steal money from their accounts.</span><span class=\"xp\">Although older people are often targeted, young people are by no means {{44}}. Experts advise {{45}} passwords or verification codes with anyone, even someone who claims to work for a bank. If in doubt, hang up and call the bank directly using the number on its official website.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When customers first walk into Mai’s small cafe in Chiang Mai, they are often surprised. There is no music, and the staff do not take orders by speaking. Instead, customers point to pictures on the menu or write their orders on small cards, and the baristas reply with smiles and simple hand signs.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Mai, who was born deaf, opened the cafe three years ago after struggling for a long time to find work. “Employers would read my resume and seem interested,” she explains through an interpreter, “but once they found out I couldn’t hear, the interviews usually ended quickly.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Determined to create opportunities for people like herself, Mai hired five deaf staff members and trained them as baristas. She also offers free sign language lessons to customers every Saturday morning. The lessons have become so popular that there is now a waiting list.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) For Mai, the cafe is about more than coffee. “Many hearing people have never talked with a deaf person,” she says. “Here, they learn that communication is not only about sound. It is about the willingness to understand each other.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Along the coasts of southern Thailand, mangrove forests grow where the land meets the sea. Their tangled roots, which rise above the water like the legs of a table, may look strange, but they make mangroves one of the most valuable ecosystems on Earth.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Mangroves protect coastal communities by acting as natural barriers. Their roots slow down waves and reduce the damage caused by storms. After the Indian Ocean tsunami in 2004, researchers reported that some villages located behind healthy mangrove forests suffered less damage than villages without them.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Mangroves also serve as nurseries for marine life. Many kinds of fish, crabs, and shrimp lay their eggs among the roots, where the young are protected from larger predators. In addition, mangroves store large amounts of carbon in their muddy soil, which helps to slow down climate change.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Despite these benefits, large areas of mangrove forest were cleared in past decades to make way for shrimp farms and tourist resorts. Today, many local communities are working with the government to replant mangroves along the coast. Their efforts show that protecting nature and protecting people often go hand in hand.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In Thailand, students in school uniforms are a familiar sight every weekday morning. Recently, however, some schools have started allowing students to wear their own clothes on certain days, and this has reopened an old debate about whether uniforms are still necessary.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters believe that uniforms create a sense of equality. When everyone dresses the same, students from poorer families are less likely to be judged by what they wear. Uniforms also save time in the morning and make it easy for teachers to identify their students on school trips.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Opponents argue that uniforms limit students’ freedom to express themselves. They also point out that buying several sets of uniforms each year can be expensive, which ironically puts pressure on the very families that uniforms are supposed to help. Moreover, some studies have found no clear link between wearing uniforms and better behavior or higher grades.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Several schools have chosen a middle path: students wear uniforms on most days but may dress freely once a week. As one school director explains, “The point is not the clothes themselves, but what they teach students about belonging and responsibility.”</span>"]},"TG70E_I":"","TG70E_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you take a photo of us in front of the temple?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm sorry I'm late. The traffic was terrible.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What's the weather like in Chiang Mai in December?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Have you ever tried skydiving?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you think it will rain this afternoon?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you tell me what time the museum closes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I've got a terrible headache.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Mind your step! The floor is still wet.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Who's in charge of the school festival this year?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can you believe it's already November?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a cinema ticket counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Hi there. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Two tickets for Moonlight Island at 7:30, please.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> I'm sorry, but the 7:30 show is sold out. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hmm, that's a bit late, but it's fine.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Where would you like to sit?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> No problem. Row G, seats 10 and 11.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two neighbors talking outside their houses","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Lee:</b> Excuse me, is this your car? {{14}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Dao:</b> Oh, I'm so sorry! I didn't realize.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Lee:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ms. Dao:</b> Of course. I'll move it right away. {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mr. Lee:</b> Thanks. I really appreciate it.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: The beginning of an online class","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Good morning, everyone. Kai, {{17}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> Yes, I can, but your video keeps freezing.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Hmm. {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> That's much better now. Your voice is clearer, too.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Great. Before we start, {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> I haven't yet. Where can I find it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kai:</b> Got it. Thank you.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student volunteers at a care home for older people","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Welcome to Sunshine Home. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> Yes, it is. To be honest, I'm a little nervous.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Don't worry. {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> That sounds nice. What kind of things do they like to talk about?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> I'd love to hear those.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Just one thing. {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> I'll keep that in mind.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> {{25}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Volunteer:</b> Yes, please. I'm ready.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends planning a trip on a budget","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> I've been checking prices for our trip to Krabi. {{26}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> Really? How much more?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> That's a lot. Is there any way to save money?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> Good idea, but the bus takes twelve hours.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> True. {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> That works for me. Guesthouses are usually cheaper anyway.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bas:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Kim:</b> Great. Send me the link when you find a good one.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Bangkok is famous around the world for its street food. From grilled pork on sticks to spicy papaya salad, cheap and delicious meals can be found on almost every corner. In 2018, a street food cook known as Jay Fai became the {{31}} street vendor in Bangkok to receive a Michelin star.</span><span class=\"xp\">The award {{32}} international attention to the city’s street food culture. Since then, long lines have formed outside her small shop, with some customers waiting {{33}} hours to taste her famous crab omelet.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, the city has also tried to remove vendors from some busy streets {{34}} reduce traffic and improve cleanliness. Many people fear that without careful planning, Bangkok could lose an important part of its {{35}}.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Walking is {{36}} forms of exercise. It requires no special equipment, and almost anyone can do it. Unlike running, it puts little stress on the joints, {{37}} it suitable for people of all ages.</span><span class=\"xp\">Research shows that walking for just thirty minutes a day can lower the risk of heart disease and improve mood. People who walk regularly are also less likely {{38}} from depression.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{39}} these benefits, many people find it hard to fit walking into their busy schedules. Experts suggest making small changes, such as taking the stairs instead of the elevator or getting off the bus one stop {{40}} than usual.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Every year, millions of tons of plastic {{41}} in the ocean. Over time, sunlight and waves break this plastic into tiny pieces called microplastics, which are often {{42}} small to see without a microscope.</span><span class=\"xp\">Scientists have found microplastics in fish, sea salt, and even drinking water, and there are growing concerns about their effect {{43}} human health. Although research is still at an early stage, many countries have already {{44}} plastic bags that are designed to be used only once.</span><span class=\"xp\">Individuals can also help by carrying their own bottles and shopping bags, {{45}} avoiding products with unnecessary packaging. Small actions, repeated by millions of people, can make a real difference.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every Sunday morning, a group of teenagers in yellow shirts gathers by the Chao Phraya River in Nonthaburi. Armed with nets and gloves, they spend three hours pulling plastic bags, bottles, and foam boxes out of the water.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The group was started two years ago by Ton, who was only 15 at the time. One day, while fishing with his father, he caught more plastic than fish. “I was shocked,” he remembers. “I realized that if nobody did anything, the river would only get worse.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) At first, only three of Ton’s friends joined him. However, after he began posting short videos of their work online, more and more young people asked to take part. Today, the group has over 200 members, and several local businesses donate equipment and snacks.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Ton admits that cleaning the river cannot solve the problem on its own. “Most of the trash comes from people upstream,” he says. “That’s why we also visit schools to teach children not to throw waste into the river. Cleaning is important, but prevention is even more important.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Octopuses have long fascinated scientists because they show surprising intelligence for animals without a backbone. They can solve puzzles, open jars to reach the food inside, and even escape from their tanks in aquariums.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Unlike humans, whose nerve cells are mostly in the brain, an octopus has about two thirds of its nerve cells in its eight arms. This means that each arm can, to some extent, act on its own. An arm can explore a small hole and decide whether to grab something without waiting for instructions from the brain.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Octopuses are also masters of disguise. Special cells in their skin allow them to change color and even texture in less than a second. This helps them hide from predators and surprise the animals they hunt.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Interestingly, most octopuses live for only one or two years. Some scientists believe that this short life makes their intelligence even more remarkable, since young octopuses must learn everything quickly on their own. Their mothers usually die soon after the eggs hatch, so there is no one to teach them.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Tourism is one of Thailand’s most important industries, bringing in billions of baht and creating millions of jobs. However, popular destinations such as the Phi Phi Islands and Chiang Mai are under growing pressure from the large number of visitors. This has led to proposals to charge foreign tourists a small fee when they enter the country.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say that the money could be used to protect natural sites, improve public facilities, and provide insurance for tourists who are injured or fall ill during their stay. They point out that several other countries already charge similar fees, and that a small amount is unlikely to stop people from visiting.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Opponents, however, worry that any extra cost could make Thailand less attractive than neighboring countries, especially for travelers on a tight budget. Some also question whether the money would really be spent on the environment, pointing to a lack of transparency in how public funds have been used in the past.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Both sides agree on one thing: tourism must be managed carefully if Thailand’s natural beauty is to be enjoyed by future generations. The real challenge is finding a balance between welcoming visitors and protecting the places they come to see.</span>"]},"TG70F_I":"","TG70F_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you like to join us for lunch?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Your English has improved so much!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How long have you lived in Bangkok?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What do you do for a living?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Why don't we take a short break?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm going to Japan next week!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, does this train stop at Siam?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I can't decide what to wear to the party.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is it all right if I pay by card?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Take care, and say hello to your family for me.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a bookstore","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me check. I'm afraid it's out of stock at the moment.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Oh. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> We're expecting a delivery next Tuesday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Of course. Can I have your name and phone number, please?</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two students outside an exam room","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> You look worried. {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Not really. I didn't sleep well last night, and the exam starts in ten minutes.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> You're right. I know the material well. I just need to relax.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nina:</b> {{16}}!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Thanks. You too!</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a bicycle rental shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Sure. It's 150 baht for the whole day, or 40 baht an hour.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great. I'll need a deposit of 500 baht, which you'll get back when you return the bike.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, it is. We also give you a lock and a map of the old town.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> We close at 7 p.m., so please return it before then.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student asks a librarian for help with a project","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> Hi there. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Yes, please. I'm doing a project on climate change in Thailand, but I'm not sure where to start.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Do I need a password to use it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Great. What if I need printed books as well?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Can I borrow them for a week?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Librarian:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Perfect. Thank you so much for your help.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about adopting a pet","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I'm thinking of adopting a dog from the animal shelter. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That's a great idea, but have you thought about who will take care of it while you're at school?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That's good. Dogs need a lot of attention.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> I know. {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That's a big commitment. Are you sure you can get up that early every day?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Well, it sounds like you've really thought it through.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pim:</b> {{30}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Sure. I'd love to help you choose one.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Muay Thai, the national martial art of Thailand, {{31}} as “the art of eight limbs” because fighters use their fists, elbows, knees, and shins. It has a long history {{32}} hundreds of years, when it was used by soldiers on the battlefield.</span><span class=\"xp\">Today, there are Muay Thai gyms in many countries around the world, and {{33}} foreign visitors come to Thailand every year to train with local teachers. Many of them say that the sport teaches not only fitness but also respect and discipline.</span><span class=\"xp\">Before each fight, boxers perform a traditional dance called the <b>wai khru</b> to {{34}} their teachers and ask for protection. For many Thais, the worldwide popularity of Muay Thai has become a great source of national {{35}}.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Online courses have made education more {{36}} than ever before. Students can now learn from top universities {{37}} where they live, and many courses are free or very cheap.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, research has found that only a small percentage of learners who start these courses actually {{38}} them. One reason is that it is easy to lose motivation when nobody is {{39}} your progress.</span><span class=\"xp\">Experts therefore recommend setting a regular study schedule and joining online study groups {{40}} learners feel more connected and responsible to others.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Thailand is {{41}} becoming an aged society. In the coming years, a much larger share of the population will be over the age of sixty. This change is mainly {{42}} falling birth rates and longer life expectancy.</span><span class=\"xp\">As the number of people of working age {{43}}, the country may face a shortage of workers. At the same time, the government will be under pressure to provide health care and income support for {{44}}.</span><span class=\"xp\">Some experts suggest encouraging older people to keep working for longer, {{45}} others focus on attracting more young families by offering better childcare and parental leave.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Fon took over her family’s rice farm in Yasothon, she inherited debts as well as land. Like many farmers in the area, her parents had relied on chemical fertilizers that became more expensive every year, while the price of rice stayed low.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Fon decided to try organic farming, a choice that her neighbors considered risky. In the first year, her harvest fell by almost a third, and some villagers told her that she would soon give up. Instead, she used her smartphone to learn from organic farmers online and to keep careful records of the soil on her fields.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Slowly, the soil grew healthier, and her harvests recovered. More importantly, Fon began selling her rice directly to customers in Bangkok through social media, at a price nearly twice what the local rice mill had paid. Within five years, she had paid off all of her family’s debts.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Fon now runs free workshops for farmers in her district. “I’m not telling them to copy me,” she says. “I just want them to know that they have more choices than they think.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all marine species. Although corals may look like plants, they are actually animals. They get much of their food from tiny algae that live inside their bodies, and these algae also give corals their bright colors.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) When the ocean becomes too warm, corals become stressed and push the algae out. Without the algae, the corals turn white, a process known as bleaching. Bleached corals are not dead, but they are weak. If the water stays warm for too long, they may starve and eventually die.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) In recent years, rising sea temperatures have caused large bleaching events around the world, including in the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand. In some areas, Thai authorities have temporarily closed popular diving spots to give the reefs time to recover.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Scientists stress that reducing carbon emissions around the world is the only lasting solution. In the meantime, local actions, such as limiting the number of tourists and avoiding sunscreen that contains harmful chemicals, can help reefs survive.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In recent years, many countries have added coding, or computer programming, to their primary school curriculum. Thailand, too, has made computing science part of its national curriculum. Supporters see this as essential preparation for a digital future.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters argue that coding teaches more than how to write programs. It trains children to break big problems into smaller steps, to think logically, and to keep trying when something does not work. These skills, they say, are useful in any career, not just in technology.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics agree that these skills are valuable, but they question whether young children should spend limited school time in front of screens. They argue that reading, mathematics, and play are still the foundation of learning, and that coding can wait until secondary school. Others point out that many schools lack trained teachers and computers, which may widen the gap between rich and poor schools.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Perhaps the answer lies in how coding is taught. Some teachers now use “unplugged” activities, such as games and puzzles that require no computers, to teach the same ways of thinking. This approach may offer the benefits of coding without the drawbacks of extra screen time.</span>"]},"TG70G_I":"","TG70G_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you pass me the salt, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, I think you're sitting in my seat.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How far is the hospital from here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Have you heard the news about Mint?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> You look exhausted. Are you all right?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What a beautiful view!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would it be possible to move my appointment to Friday?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Oh no, I've lost my keys again!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Who does this umbrella belong to?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Thank you for having me. I had a wonderful time.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a mobile phone repair shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me take a look. Hmm, it'll need a new screen.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> About two hours. You can pick it up after lunch.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, don't worry. We won't touch any of your data.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends deciding where to have lunch","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> I'm starving. {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Gun:</b> How about that new Korean restaurant near the station?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Gun:</b> Then let's go now, before the lunch rush starts.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ploy:</b> {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Gun:</b> Great. I'll call a taxi.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At an airport information desk","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> Excuse me. {{17}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Go out through Gate 4 and turn right. The bus stop is just outside.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> {{18}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Every twenty minutes until midnight.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> You can pay the driver, but it's cheaper to buy a ticket from the machine over there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Traveler:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> You're welcome. Enjoy your stay.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student talks to his teacher after class","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> Hi, Krit. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Well, I'm afraid I won't be able to hand in my essay on time.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> I see. {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> My grandmother was in hospital, and I spent the whole weekend helping my family.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Yes, she's back home now. Thank you for asking.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> Thank you so much. I'll hand it in by Thursday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Teacher:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Krit:</b> I will. Thanks again.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two coworkers talking about a charity event","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> Our company is organizing a charity run next month. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> I'd like to, but I haven't run in years.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> Five kilometers? I think I can manage that.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> Good idea. How about Tuesdays and Thursdays?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> Perfect. By the way, where does the money go?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Dan:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>May:</b> That's wonderful. Count me in!</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Loy Krathong is one of the most beautiful festivals in Thailand. On the night of the full moon in the twelfth lunar month, people {{31}} small decorated baskets on rivers and canals to give thanks to the water goddess and to let go of bad luck.</span><span class=\"xp\">Traditionally, the baskets, called <b>krathong</b>, {{32}} from banana leaves and the trunks of banana trees. In recent decades, however, many people began to use foam krathong, which take a very long time to break down and {{33}} rivers and canals.</span><span class=\"xp\">To reduce the problem, some cities now encourage people to use krathong made of bread, which can be {{34}} by fish. Others suggest that families share one krathong {{35}} each person floating their own.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Electric vehicles, or EVs, are becoming a common sight on Thai roads. Sales have risen {{36}} in recent years, partly {{37}} government support and falling prices.</span><span class=\"xp\">Many EV owners say that their cars are cheaper to run {{38}} petrol cars, and that they enjoy the quiet, smooth ride. However, some drivers still hesitate to buy one because there are not {{39}} charging stations outside big cities, especially along long highways.</span><span class=\"xp\">Experts believe that if charging networks continue to expand, EVs {{40}} a major role in reducing air pollution in Thai cities over the next decade.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">{{41}} mistakes is a natural part of learning, yet many people are terrified of failure. Some of the most successful inventors in history failed hundreds of times {{42}} they finally created something that worked.</span><span class=\"xp\">Psychologists say that people who see mistakes as chances to learn are more likely to {{43}} trying when things get difficult. In contrast, people who fear failure often avoid challenges completely, {{44}} limits their personal growth.</span><span class=\"xp\">So the next time you make a mistake, instead of feeling ashamed, try asking yourself {{45}} you can learn from it.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Every morning at seven, Nurse Jin steps onto a small boat at a pier in Trat. Her destination is one of several small islands where there are no hospitals and only a few hundred residents, many of whom are elderly.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Jin has worked as a community nurse for eleven years. On each island, she checks blood pressure, gives vaccinations, and teaches families how to care for patients who cannot leave their beds. “Without these visits, many of my patients would have to spend a whole day traveling to the mainland just to see a doctor,” she explains.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) The job is not easy. During the rainy season, strong waves sometimes make the trip too dangerous, and Jin has to cancel her visits. To stay in touch, she has trained a volunteer on each island to use a tablet for video calls, so that patients can still talk to her when the weather is bad.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Jin says that the hardest part of her job is not the sea, but saying goodbye. “When you visit the same people for years, they become more than patients,” she says. “They become part of your life.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Elephants are famous for their loud trumpeting calls, but much of their communication actually happens at sounds too low for humans to hear. These deep rumbles, known as infrasound, can travel several kilometers through the air and even through the ground.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Scientists believe that elephants detect these vibrations not only with their ears but also through their feet. Special cells in the soles of their feet are very sensitive to movement in the ground, allowing elephants to “hear” messages from far away.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Researchers have found that elephants use these rumbles to keep their herds together, to warn one another of danger, and to find partners. Mothers and their young also use them to stay in contact when they become separated.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Understanding how elephants communicate may help to protect them. In some areas, sensors that pick up elephant rumbles have been tested as a way to warn villagers when a herd is approaching their farms. Such systems could reduce dangerous conflicts between people and elephants.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) In many buffet restaurants in Thailand, where customers pay one price and eat as much as they like, signs warn that customers will be charged extra for leaving food on their plates. The rule has become a topic of lively debate among diners.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say the charge is a fair way to reduce food waste. Buffet customers often take more than they can eat, and the leftover food is usually thrown away. A small charge, they argue, encourages people to think carefully before filling their plates.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, on the other hand, say the rule can be applied unfairly. Some customers may leave food because it is too spicy or not cooked properly, not because they were greedy. Others feel that once they have paid, they should not be told how much to eat.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Some restaurants have tried a different approach. Instead of punishing waste, they reward customers who finish everything with a small discount or a free dessert. Early results suggest that this positive method reduces waste just as well, while keeping customers much happier.</span>"]},"TG70H_I":"","TG70H_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you want to share a taxi to the airport?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, is there an ATM anywhere near here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How much do I owe you for the concert ticket?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you prefer tea or coffee?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm thinking about getting a cat.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can I get a refund for this shirt? It doesn't fit.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> When is the history assignment due?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm sorry, I didn't catch your name.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Shall we meet at the main gate at six?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How did you like the movie?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: A phone call to an animal clinic","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Good morning, Happy Paws Animal Clinic.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Oh, I'm sorry to hear that. Can you bring her in at four this afternoon?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Let me see. We have an opening at 11:30 this morning.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Great. Please bring her in a pet carrier.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about a book","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Fern:</b> {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Oat:</b> Almost. I have just two chapters left.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fern:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Oat:</b> Okay. I'll give it back to you on Friday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Fern:</b> {{16}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Oat:</b> I love it. I can’t wait to see how it ends.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a hair salon","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Hi there. What would you like today?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Sure. How much would you like me to cut off?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Got it. Would you like a hair wash as well?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> It's 100 baht more.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Stylist:</b> Great. Please follow me to the sink.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A student calls a university scholarship office","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Good afternoon, International Scholarship Office.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> Hi. {{21}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Sure. Are you a current university student?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Great. Then you are eligible to apply. Have you taken a Japanese language test?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> That's fine. You can submit the result later.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> The end of January. All documents must be uploaded online.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Student:</b> {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Officer:</b> Good luck with your application!</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about buying a used motorbike","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Not yet. The price is good, but I'm a little worried.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> The seller won't let me take it to a mechanic first.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> That's exactly what I was thinking.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Good idea. Do you know any good shops?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Joe:</b> Great. Can you send me the address?</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Songkran, the Thai New Year, falls {{31}} the middle of April, the hottest time of the year. Today, it is best known for its huge water fights, {{32}} people of all ages splash water on friends and strangers alike.</span><span class=\"xp\">Originally, however, Songkran was a quiet time for families. People {{33}} pour scented water gently over the hands of older relatives as a sign of respect and to ask for their blessings.</span><span class=\"xp\">{{34}} the festival has become more commercial in recent years, many Thais still travel back to their hometowns during Songkran to {{35}} respect to their elders and spend time with their families.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Studies show that children who read for pleasure {{36}} to do better at school, not only in language subjects {{37}} in mathematics. This may be because regular reading improves concentration and {{38}} vocabulary.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, the number of young people who read books for fun has {{39}} in recent years, as more of their free time is spent on phones and games.</span><span class=\"xp\">To reverse this trend, experts suggest that parents {{40}} with their children every day, even for just fifteen minutes, and let them choose books that match their own interests.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">In recent years, many companies have allowed their employees to work from home for {{41}} part of the week. Many workers say they are more productive at home because they {{42}} have to spend hours commuting in heavy traffic.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, others find it difficult to separate work from home life, and some feel {{43}} after spending long periods without seeing their colleagues. Managers also worry that teamwork may suffer when people rarely meet {{44}}.</span><span class=\"xp\">As a result, many companies now use a hybrid model, {{45}} employees spend some days in the office and the rest at home.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Ohm lost the use of his legs in a car accident at the age of nineteen, he believed his days of traveling were over. For almost a year, he rarely left his apartment in Khon Kaen.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Everything changed when a close friend persuaded him to join a trip to Chiang Mai. The journey was full of challenges. Some hotels had no ramps, and many tourist attractions could only be reached by stairs. Yet Ohm discovered that he still loved exploring new places.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) After the trip, he started an online channel that reviews travel destinations from the point of view of a wheelchair user. He rates hotels, restaurants, and attractions on how easy they are to access, and he shares honest videos of both good and bad experiences.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Today, his channel has more than 100,000 followers. Several hotels have even added ramps and accessible bathrooms after appearing in his videos. “I’m not trying to make people feel sorry for me,” Ohm says. “I just want everyone to be able to travel.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Chili peppers are an essential part of Thai cooking, but why do they make our mouths feel as if they are on fire? The answer lies in a chemical called capsaicin, which is found mainly in the white parts inside the pepper.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Capsaicin attaches to sensors in our mouths that normally detect heat. When these sensors are activated, they send a message to the brain that the mouth is burning, even though its temperature has not actually changed. In other words, the “heat” of chili is an illusion created by the brain.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Interestingly, drinking water does little to cool the burning feeling, because capsaicin does not mix well with water. Milk and yogurt are much more effective, since they contain a protein that helps wash capsaicin away.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Scientists believe that chili plants produce capsaicin to protect their seeds from mammals, which tend to destroy seeds when they chew. Birds, however, cannot feel the burn, so they happily eat the peppers and spread the seeds far and wide.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Some universities require students to complete a certain number of hours of community service before they can graduate. Supporters see this as a valuable part of education, while critics question whether service should ever be forced on anyone.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters argue that community service helps students apply what they learn in class to real problems. It also develops empathy and teamwork, which are difficult to teach in a lecture hall. In addition, many employers value graduates who have experience working with people from different backgrounds.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, however, argue that requiring service turns a generous act into an obligation. Students who are forced to volunteer may simply complete the hours without caring about the people they are supposed to help. Moreover, students who work part time to pay for their studies may find it hard to fit extra hours into their busy schedules.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) A possible solution is to give students more choice. Instead of setting a fixed number of hours, universities could allow students to choose projects linked to their own interests and fields of study. This way, service could become a meaningful experience rather than just another requirement.</span>"]},"TG70I_I":"","TG70I_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Can you give me a ride to the station?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you have any plans for the long weekend?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What time does the bank open?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is it okay to take photos in here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> You've got a good point there.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Guess what? I passed my driving test!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you speak a little more slowly, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Where did you get that nice bag?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What do you usually do in your free time?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'd better be going. It's getting late.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a clothing store","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Excuse me. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Let me check. Yes, we have it in large and extra large.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Sure. The fitting room is at the back of the store.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> It fits perfectly. {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, of course. Just scan this code here.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two neighbors in an apartment building","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> Hi, Jo. I'm going away for a week. {{14}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jo:</b> Sure, no problem. How often do they need water?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> {{15}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jo:</b> Okay. Where should I get the key?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mali:</b> {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Jo:</b> Got it. Have a nice trip!</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a museum ticket counter","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> Hi. {{17}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> It's 200 baht for adults, but students get in for half price.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great, that's 100 baht, then.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Yes, there's one at two o'clock, and it's free.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Visitor:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Wonderful. Please meet the guide in the main hall.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A customer calls an internet service provider","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you for calling FastNet. This is Ploy.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{21}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I'm sorry to hear that. Have you tried restarting your router?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I see. Let me check your line. Could I have your account number, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you. It looks like there is a problem with the cable in your area.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{24}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Our technicians should finish the repair by tomorrow evening.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{25}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Yes. We'll take two days off your next bill.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two university students talking about clubs","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Not yet. I'm thinking about the photography club or the volunteer club.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> I love taking pictures, but I also want to do something useful for society.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> That's a great idea! Then I could do both.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> Really? What kind of activities do you do?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ice:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tan:</b> That sounds wonderful. Sign me up!</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">The BTS Skytrain, which {{31}} in 1999, was the first mass transit rail system in Bangkok. Before it was built, many commuters spent hours {{32}} in traffic every day.</span><span class=\"xp\">Since then, the rail network has expanded to {{33}} much of the city, with several new lines connecting the suburbs to the city center.</span><span class=\"xp\">Although fares are {{34}} high for some low income workers, the trains remain popular {{35}} their speed and reliability, especially during the rush hour.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Chinese has become one of the most popular foreign languages {{36}} Thai students. This trend {{37}} the growing trade and tourism links between the two countries.</span><span class=\"xp\">Many students believe that being able to speak Chinese {{38}} them an advantage in the job market. However, learning the language is not easy, as thousands of written characters must be memorized.</span><span class=\"xp\">On the other hand, Chinese is a {{39}} language, like Thai, which may make pronunciation {{40}} for Thai learners than for native speakers of English.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Space tourism, once only a dream, is slowly becoming {{41}}. Several private companies have already sent paying customers {{42}} space for short trips.</span><span class=\"xp\">However, tickets are extremely expensive, often costing {{43}} of dollars. Critics argue that this money {{44}} on solving problems on Earth, such as poverty and climate change.</span><span class=\"xp\">Supporters, on the other hand, believe that space tourism could lead to new technologies {{45}} benefit everyone, just as earlier space programs led to inventions that we now use every day.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) For most of her life, Somsri could not read. She grew up in a poor farming family in Buriram and left school at the age of eight to help her parents in the rice fields. As an adult, she kept her secret by asking others to read signs and documents for her.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) At the age of 70, Somsri decided it was time for a change. She joined a free adult reading class at the local temple, where a retired teacher taught a small group of older learners twice a week.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Progress was slow at first. Her eyes tired quickly, and she often forgot letters she had learned the day before. But she practiced every evening by reading the labels on food packages and the headlines in the newspaper.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Two years later, Somsri can read short stories to her grandchildren. “I used to feel ashamed,” she says. “Now I feel as if a new door has opened in front of me.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Everyone yawns, from newborn babies to the elderly, and many animals do it too. Yet scientists still do not fully understand why we yawn.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) One popular explanation is that yawning increases the amount of oxygen in the blood. However, experiments have shown that breathing extra oxygen does not make people yawn less, so this explanation is now considered unlikely.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) A newer idea suggests that yawning helps to cool the brain. When we yawn, we take in a large amount of air, which may lower the temperature of the blood flowing to the brain. Supporting this idea, studies have found that people yawn more often in mild weather than in very hot or very cold conditions.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Yawning is also contagious. Simply seeing, hearing, or even reading about someone yawning can make us yawn too. Interestingly, people are more likely to “catch” yawns from friends and family than from strangers, which suggests that contagious yawning may be linked to empathy.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Childhood obesity has become a growing concern in Thailand. In response, some schools have banned the sale of sweet drinks, such as soda and sweetened milk tea, on campus. The policy has divided parents and teachers.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say the ban is a simple and effective way to reduce how much sugar children consume. Habits formed in childhood often last a lifetime, so schools should be places that encourage healthy choices. They also point out that Thailand already taxes sugary drinks, showing that the government takes the issue seriously.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Opponents, however, argue that a ban achieves little if children can simply buy the same drinks from shops right outside the school gate. They believe that education is more effective than rules. Teaching children to read nutrition labels and understand the effects of sugar, they say, will help them make better choices on their own.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Many experts suggest combining both approaches. “Rules can change what children drink at school,” one nutritionist explains, “but only knowledge can change what they choose for the rest of their lives.”</span>"]},"TG70J_I":"","TG70J_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Excuse me, which way is the restroom?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> My laptop just crashed, and I lost my essay!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How many people are coming to the party?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is this the right bus for the airport?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm so sorry for your loss.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Could you do me a favor?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How about going for a swim this afternoon?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Who's going to pick up the cake?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I can't stand this hot weather.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Did you remember to lock the door?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a bakery","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. {{11}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Sure. What flavor would you like?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{12}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Great choice. Would you like us to write a message on it?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> No problem. It'll be ready by noon on Saturday.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two friends talking on the phone outside a cinema","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mook:</b> Where are you? The movie starts in ten minutes!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Boss:</b> {{14}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mook:</b> {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Boss:</b> Yes, go ahead. I'll find you inside.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mook:</b> {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Boss:</b> Thanks. Sorry again for being late.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a hotel front desk in the morning","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Good morning. {{17}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Of course. May I have your room key, please?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{18}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Thank you. Did you take anything from the minibar?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{19}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> That's 30 baht. How would you like to pay?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{20}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Staff:</b> Thank you. Have a safe trip home.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A job interview at a cafe","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> Thanks for coming in, Tee. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Yes, I worked at a coffee shop near my university for about a year.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{22}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> I liked talking with customers and remembering their favorite drinks.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> That's great. {{23}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Yes, Saturdays and Sundays are fine, but I have classes on Monday mornings.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> Perfect. When could I start?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Manager:</b> {{25}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Tee:</b> That sounds great. Thank you so much!</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: Two friends talking about recycling","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> I try to, but I'm never sure what can be recycled.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{27}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> What about food boxes?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{28}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> Oh, I didn't know that. I always throw them away with the rest.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{29}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> Is there a recycling center near here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ann:</b> {{30}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Bow:</b> Great. I'll take my bottles there this weekend.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Pad Thai is {{31}} the most famous Thai dish outside Thailand. Interestingly, it is a fairly recent creation. The dish became popular in the middle of the twentieth century, when the government promoted it {{32}} a period of rice shortage.</span><span class=\"xp\">Eating rice noodles was encouraged as a way {{33}} the amount of rice people consumed, and the dish soon spread across the country.</span><span class=\"xp\">Today, Pad Thai can be found in Thai restaurants all over the world, {{34}} it is often changed to suit local tastes. Some cooks abroad even {{35}} tamarind with tomato sauce, which gives the dish a very different flavor.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Mental health is {{36}} important as physical health, yet many university students do not seek help {{37}} they are struggling with stress or anxiety.</span><span class=\"xp\">Many universities now offer free counseling services, {{38}} students can use privately. Experts also encourage students {{39}} to someone they trust, such as a friend or family member, when they feel overwhelmed.</span><span class=\"xp\">In addition, small daily habits, such as getting enough sleep and exercising regularly, can make a big {{40}} to how students feel.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Online shopping has {{41}} easier than ever to buy almost anything with just a few clicks. However, it has also {{42}} a huge increase in packaging waste and delivery traffic.</span><span class=\"xp\">Some customers order several sizes of the same item and send back the ones that do not fit, a habit {{43}} “bracketing.” This creates extra transport and often means that returned items cannot be sold again.</span><span class=\"xp\">Some companies now charge a small fee for returns, {{44}} to discourage this habit. Others offer online tools that help customers choose the right size {{45}} they place an order.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Kwan told her family that she wanted to become a car mechanic, her relatives laughed. “That’s a job for men,” one of them said. But Kwan had loved fixing things since she was a child, often taking apart old radios and putting them back together.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) After finishing vocational college, she applied to more than ten garages, but most of them rejected her without even giving her a test. Finally, a small garage in Lampang agreed to let her work for a trial month.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Kwan worked harder than anyone else. She arrived early, stayed late, and asked the senior mechanics endless questions. By the end of the month, the owner offered her a full time position. Soon, several customers began asking for her by name because she explained problems clearly and honestly.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Five years later, Kwan runs her own garage and trains young women who want to enter the field. “Skills don’t have a gender,” she says. “Anyone who is willing to learn can do this job.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Lotus plants grow in muddy ponds, yet their leaves always seem perfectly clean. This puzzled scientists for many years until they examined the leaves under powerful microscopes.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) They discovered that the surface of a lotus leaf is covered with tiny bumps, which are themselves coated with a waxy material. Because of this structure, water cannot spread across the leaf. Instead, it forms round drops that roll off easily.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) As the drops roll, they pick up dirt and dust and carry them away. This self cleaning ability, known as the lotus effect, helps the plant stay healthy by keeping its leaves free of dirt that could block sunlight.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Engineers have since copied this idea to create products such as glass that cleans itself when it rains, fabrics that stay dry in wet weather, and paints that help keep buildings clean. The lotus is a good example of how nature can inspire human inventions.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) Zoos have long been popular places for families to spend a weekend. In recent years, however, animal rights groups have questioned whether keeping wild animals in captivity can ever be justified.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters say that modern zoos play an important role in conservation. Many run breeding programs for endangered species and even return some animals to the wild. Zoos also educate millions of visitors, especially children, about wildlife and the threats it faces.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics argue that even the best zoos cannot provide the space and freedom that wild animals need. Large animals such as elephants and tigers may show signs of stress, such as walking back and forth for hours, when they are kept in small enclosures. Critics believe the money should instead be used to protect animals in their natural habitats.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Some zoos are responding by keeping fewer species, building larger and more natural enclosures, and working closely with wildlife sanctuaries. Whether such changes are enough, however, remains an open question.</span>"]},"TG70_I":"","TG70_P":{"qr1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Would you mind keeping an eye on my bag while I grab a coffee?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{1}}</span>"],"qr2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I can't believe I failed my driving test again.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{2}}</span>"],"qr3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> How long does it take to get to the airport from here?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{3}}</span>"],"qr4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Do you happen to know if the library is open on Sundays?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{4}}</span>"],"qr5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> I'm afraid I spilled coffee on your notebook. I'm so sorry.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{5}}</span>"],"qr6":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Shall we split the bill?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{6}}</span>"],"qr7":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Why don't you take a day off? You look exhausted.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{7}}</span>"],"qr8":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Is it okay if I bring a friend to your birthday party?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{8}}</span>"],"qr9":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> What do you think of the new canteen menu?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{9}}</span>"],"qr10":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part I: Question and Response (Items 1 to 10)<br>Directions: Choose the best response to each question or statement.","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Q:</b> Guess what? I've been offered a scholarship to study in Japan!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>R:</b> {{10}}</span>"],"c1":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 11 to 13)<br>Situation: At a pharmacy","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Good evening. {{11}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Yes, please. I've had a sore throat and a runny nose since yesterday.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> I see. {{12}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> No, I'm not. I've taken this kind of medicine before without any problems.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> Good. Then take one tablet three times a day after meals, and drink plenty of water.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{13}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Pharmacist:</b> About five days. If you don't feel better by then, please see a doctor.</span>"],"c2":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 14 to 16)<br>Situation: Two classmates talking about a group project","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Have you finished your part of the slides yet?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Not yet. {{14}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Really? But the presentation is this Friday!</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> I know. My laptop broke down last night, and I lost half of my work.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Oh no. {{15}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> That would be great. Could I borrow it this evening?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Mint:</b> Sure. {{16}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Ben:</b> Don't worry. I've learned my lesson.</span>"],"c3":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part II: Short Conversations<br>Conversation 3 (Items 17 to 20)<br>Situation: At a hotel front desk","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Hi, I'd like to check in, please. The reservation is under Arisa Wong.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Welcome to the Riverside Hotel. {{17}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Here you are.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Thank you. You're in room 512 on the fifth floor. Breakfast is served from 6:30 to 10:00 in the restaurant on the ground floor.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Great. {{18}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Yes, there is, and it is free. The password is printed on your key card holder.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> Perfect. Oh, and {{19}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> Certainly. We can keep your bags at the front desk until 6 p.m. on the day you leave.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Guest:</b> {{20}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Receptionist:</b> You're welcome. Enjoy your stay.</span>"],"c4":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 1 (Items 21 to 25)<br>Situation: A university student meets an academic advisor","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Come in, Nat. {{21}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Thanks for seeing me. I'm thinking about dropping my statistics course. I'm completely lost in class.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> I see. Before you make a decision, {{22}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Well, I failed the first quiz, and I don't understand most of the lectures.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Have you tried going to the tutoring center? Many students find it really useful.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Not yet. To be honest, {{23}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> There's nothing to be embarrassed about. The tutors are senior students who once struggled with the same course.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Really? Then maybe I should give it a try.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> I think so. Also, if you drop the course now, {{24}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> Oh, I didn't know that. I'd better not drop it, then.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Advisor:</b> Good decision. Come back in three weeks and {{25}}.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Nat:</b> I will. Thank you so much.</span>"],"c5":["SECTION I: SPEAKING SKILL (Items 1 to 30)<br>Part III: Long Conversations<br>Conversation 2 (Items 26 to 30)<br>Situation: A customer calls an online shop","<span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you for calling ShopSmart. This is Jenny speaking. {{26}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Hi. I ordered a pair of running shoes last week, but the ones I received are the wrong size.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> I'm sorry about that. {{27}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Sure. It's TH478152.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Thank you. I can see that you ordered size 42, but we sent you size 40. That was our mistake.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> {{28}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> Of course. We'll send a courier to collect the wrong pair tomorrow, and the new pair will arrive within three days.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> That's great. {{29}}?</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> No, you won't. The return is completely free of charge.</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Customer:</b> Wonderful. {{30}}</span><span class=\"xd\"><b>Agent:</b> My pleasure. Have a nice day.</span>"],"r1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 1 (Items 31 to 35)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Every year between January and April, many provinces in northern Thailand are covered by a thick haze. The main source of this pollution is {{31}} burning, as farmers clear their fields quickly and cheaply after the harvest. Forest fires and traffic emissions also {{32}} to the problem.</span><span class=\"xp\">When the level of fine dust, known as PM2.5, rises above the safe limit, schools may close and hospitals report more patients with breathing problems. {{33}}, tourism in cities such as Chiang Mai suffers, since visitors are less willing to travel to places with poor air quality.</span><span class=\"xp\">Experts say that the problem cannot be solved by {{34}} alone. Farmers need affordable alternatives, such as machines that turn crop waste into animal feed or fertilizer. Until such solutions become widely available, residents are advised to check the air quality index daily and to wear masks {{35}} they go outdoors.</span>"],"r2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 2 (Items 36 to 40)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">In an age when almost any information can be found online, many people assume that public libraries are {{36}}. Yet libraries around the world are proving them wrong by reinventing themselves as community centers.</span><span class=\"xp\">Instead of simply lending books, modern libraries now offer free coding classes, workshops with 3D printers, and quiet rooms {{37}} students can study late into the night. In Helsinki, the Oodi Central Library, {{38}} opened in 2018, attracts thousands of visitors a day and has become one of the most popular places in the city.</span><span class=\"xp\">Librarians say that their role has changed {{39}} gatekeepers of knowledge to guides who help people find, evaluate, and use information wisely. This skill is more important than ever in a world full of fake news. Far from disappearing, libraries may be more {{40}} today than at any time in history.</span>"],"r3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part I: Text Completion<br>Passage 3 (Items 41 to 45)<br>Directions: Choose the best answer to complete the passage.","<span class=\"xp\">Each year, roughly one third of all food produced for people is lost or wasted. This waste not only {{41}} families and businesses billions of dollars but also harms the environment, because rotting food in landfills releases methane, a greenhouse gas far more powerful than carbon dioxide.</span><span class=\"xp\">In Thailand, food makes up a large share of household waste, {{42}} much of it is still perfectly safe to eat when it is thrown away. Some supermarkets have begun selling “ugly” fruit and vegetables at lower prices, while mobile apps now connect restaurants with customers who want to buy leftover meals at a discount.</span><span class=\"xp\">Individuals can also make a difference. Planning meals before shopping, storing food properly, and understanding the difference between “best before” and “use by” dates can {{43}} reduce the amount we throw away. If every household cut its food waste {{44}} half, the impact on both family budgets and the planet would be enormous. In the end, tackling food waste is less about sacrifice and more about {{45}} what we already have.</span>"],"p1":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 1 (Items 46 to 50)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When Ploy graduated with a degree in marketing, her friends expected her to take a job with a good salary in Bangkok. Instead, she returned to her home village in Roi Et, where her grandmother and neighbors had been weaving <i>pha khao ma</i>, a traditional checked cloth, for decades. Ploy had always loved the cloth, but she noticed that the weavers earned very little because they sold their products to middlemen at low prices.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Ploy decided to change that. She photographed the weavers at work, wrote short stories about the meaning of each pattern, and began selling the cloth online. At first, orders were few, and some villagers doubted that young people in the city would ever pay for something they saw as out of date.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Everything changed when a popular travel blogger wore one of Ploy’s scarves in a video that went viral. Within a month, Ploy received more orders than the village could produce. Rather than buying cheaper cloth from factories to meet the demand, she chose to train younger villagers to weave, paying them fairly for every piece.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Today, more than forty families earn a steady income from the business. “I didn’t want to just sell a product,” Ploy says. “I wanted the people who make it to be proud of it again.”</span>"],"p2":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 2 (Items 51 to 55)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) On a hot afternoon, the center of Bangkok can be several degrees warmer than the rice fields just outside the city. Scientists call this the urban heat island effect. It occurs because concrete, asphalt, and glass absorb heat during the day and release it slowly at night, while cars, air conditioners, and factories add even more heat to the air.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) The effect is more than just uncomfortable. Higher temperatures increase the demand for electricity as people turn up their air conditioners, which in turn pump more hot air into the streets. This vicious circle also raises health risks, particularly for the elderly, outdoor workers, and people living in crowded housing without proper ventilation.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Fortunately, cities are fighting back. Singapore requires many new buildings to replace the greenery lost during construction with rooftop gardens and plant walls. Other cities paint roofs white to reflect sunlight, and some replace dark pavements with materials that stay cooler. In Bangkok, Chulalongkorn University Centenary Park was designed not only to provide shade and green space but also to collect rainwater and reduce flooding.</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Urban planners admit that no single solution will be enough. However, they agree that every tree planted and every roof turned green brings the city one step closer to a cooler, healthier future.</span>"],"p3":["SECTION II: READING SKILL (Items 31 to 60)<br>Part II: Reading Comprehension<br>Passage 3 (Items 56 to 60)<br>Directions: Read the passage and choose the best answer to each question.","<span class=\"xp\">(1) When AI chatbots that can write essays in seconds became widely available, many schools reacted with alarm. Some banned the tools completely, fearing that students would stop thinking for themselves. A few years later, however, the debate has become far more nuanced.</span><span class=\"xp\">(2) Supporters argue that AI, used properly, can act as a patient tutor available at any hour. A student who is confused about a math problem at midnight can ask for an explanation broken into small steps. Teachers, too, can save time by using AI to prepare practice questions, giving them more time to work with students individually.</span><span class=\"xp\">(3) Critics, on the other hand, point out that chatbots sometimes produce information that sounds convincing but is completely false. Students who copy answers without checking them may not only learn incorrect facts but also lose the chance to develop their own writing and reasoning skills. “Struggling with a difficult problem is part of learning,” says one high school teacher. “If the machine does the struggling, the student doesn’t grow.”</span><span class=\"xp\">(4) Many educators now believe that the real question is not whether students should use AI, but how. Some schools ask students to submit the questions they asked a chatbot along with their final work, so that teachers can see their thinking process. Others design assignments, such as oral presentations and classroom debates, that AI cannot easily complete. In the end, the goal is the same as it has always been: to produce learners who can think critically, with or without a machine.</span>"]},"TH70Q":""};
